/* Exam question bank (6 exams + final).
   AUTO-EXTRACTED from The Chase, then repaired.
   Sep 2026: the 150 'select all that apply' items were extracted as single-answer
   questions with the key defaulted to option A, which made them unanswerable. Each now
   carries an `m` array of ALL correct option indices; `m` is what marks an item as
   multi-select. `c` is kept only so older single-answer code does not crash. */
window.FINAL_BANK={"1":{"title":"HOMEOSTASIS UNDER SIEGE — Ch 1 Fluids/Lytes/ABGs · Ch 2 Immunity & Infection","questions":[{"q":"Approximately what percentage of total body weight is water in the average adult?","a":["40%","50%","60%","80%"],"c":2,"t":"Fluid Distribution","fb":"Roughly 60% of body weight is water. Two-thirds sits inside cells (intracellular) and one-third sits outside cells (extracellular), which is further split into intravascular, interstitial, and transcellular compartments.","d":"e"},{"q":"The nurse is reviewing fluid compartments with a student. Which statement correctly describes the extracellular fluid compartment?","a":["It holds roughly two-thirds of total body water","It is divided into intravascular, interstitial, and transcellular spaces","It is found only inside skeletal muscle cells","It is regulated primarily by intracellular protein"],"c":1,"t":"Fluid Distribution","fb":"Extracellular fluid is the one-third of body water that lives outside the cells and is subdivided into intravascular (plasma), interstitial (between cells), and transcellular (CSF, joint, pleural) spaces.","d":"m"},{"q":"What is the primary trigger that causes the kidneys to release renin and activate the RAAS pathway?","a":["A drop in arterial blood pressure or renal perfusion","Elevated serum potassium","Rising arterial oxygen saturation","High serum bicarbonate"],"c":0,"t":"RAAS","fb":"Renin is released by the juxtaglomerular cells of the kidney in response to low blood pressure or reduced renal perfusion. RAAS is fundamentally a hypotension-response system.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with chronic heart failure is started on a medication that blocks aldosterone. The nurse anticipates which renal effect?","a":["Increased reabsorption of sodium and water","Excretion of sodium and water with retention of potassium","Increased excretion of bicarbonate","Decreased excretion of magnesium"],"c":1,"t":"RAAS","fb":"Aldosterone normally tells the kidneys to reabsorb sodium (and water follows). Blocking it causes the kidneys to dump sodium and water while sparing potassium, which is why aldosterone antagonists are potassium-sparing diuretics.","d":"m"},{"q":"ANP and BNP are released in response to which physiologic stimulus?","a":["Low circulating blood volume","Stretching of the cardiac atria and ventricles from fluid overload","A drop in serum sodium","Hyperventilation"],"c":1,"t":"ANP/BNP","fb":"ANP (atrium) and BNP (ventricle) are released when the heart chambers are stretched by excess volume. They oppose RAAS by promoting sodium and water excretion, vasodilation, and inhibition of aldosterone.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client presents to the emergency department with severe hypotension after a motor vehicle collision. Which fluid regulation responses does the nurse expect to be activated? Select all that apply.","a":["Release of renin from the kidneys","Release of ADH from the posterior pituitary","Release of ANP from the cardiac atria","Aldosterone secretion from the adrenal cortex","Suppression of angiotensin II"],"c":0,"t":"RAAS","fb":"Hypotension activates RAAS (renin, angiotensin II, aldosterone) and triggers ADH release to conserve water. ANP is released when the heart is overstretched by volume, so it would be suppressed, not stimulated, in shock.","d":"m","m":[0,1,3]},{"q":"The hypothalamus detects increased blood osmolarity and signals the posterior pituitary. What is the expected effect of the resulting hormone on the kidneys?","a":["Increased water reabsorption from the collecting ducts","Increased excretion of free water","Increased sodium excretion","Decreased reabsorption of bicarbonate"],"c":0,"t":"ADH/Vasopressin","fb":"ADH (vasopressin) makes the renal collecting ducts more permeable to water, pulling water back into the bloodstream and concentrating the urine. The net effect is increased plasma volume and decreased serum osmolarity.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with liver cirrhosis develops generalized edema. The nurse recognizes that the most likely cause of this edema is a decrease in which of the following?","a":["Hydrostatic pressure","Oncotic pressure","Capillary permeability","Lymphatic drainage"],"c":1,"t":"Oncotic Pressure","fb":"A diseased liver cannot synthesize enough albumin. Low albumin lowers the pulling (oncotic) force inside the vessel, so fluid leaks into the interstitial space and produces edema.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which of the following statements correctly distinguishes hydrostatic pressure from oncotic pressure?","a":["Hydrostatic pressure pulls fluid in; oncotic pressure pushes fluid out","Hydrostatic pressure pushes fluid out of the vessel; oncotic pressure pulls fluid back in","Both pressures push fluid out of the vessel into the tissue","Both pressures are produced by albumin"],"c":1,"t":"Hydrostatic Pressure","fb":"Hydrostatic pressure is the pushing force generated by blood pressure inside the vessel. Oncotic pressure is the pulling force generated mainly by albumin. They normally balance to keep fluid in the vessel.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with severe sepsis develops widespread edema. Which mechanism best explains this finding?","a":["Increased capillary permeability from inflammatory mediators","Decreased ADH secretion","Increased oncotic pressure","Renal reabsorption of sodium"],"c":0,"t":"Edema Causes","fb":"In sepsis, inflammatory cytokines make capillaries leaky. Fluid and proteins escape into the interstitium, producing third-spacing and edema even when total body volume is depleted.","d":"e"},{"q":"A client recovering from pituitary surgery has a serum sodium of 122 mEq/L, low urine output, dark concentrated urine, and a weight gain of 4 pounds in 24 hours. Which condition does the nurse suspect?","a":["Diabetes insipidus","Syndrome of inappropriate ADH (SIADH)","Hyperaldosteronism","Adrenal insufficiency"],"c":1,"t":"SIADH","fb":"SIADH is too much ADH. The kidneys hold onto water, so the client becomes fluid overloaded, dilutionally hyponatremic, and produces small amounts of dark, concentrated urine.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with a recent head injury suddenly begins voiding 6 to 8 liters of pale, dilute urine per day and complains of intense thirst. Serum sodium is 156 mEq/L. The nurse suspects which disorder?","a":["SIADH","Diabetes insipidus","Acute kidney injury","Cushing syndrome"],"c":1,"t":"Diabetes Insipidus","fb":"Diabetes insipidus is the absence of ADH. The kidneys cannot retain water, so the client puts out large volumes of dilute urine, becomes hypernatremic, and is intensely thirsty.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which signs and symptoms are most consistent with hyponatremia? Select all that apply.","a":["Confusion and headache","Muscle cramps and twitching","Bounding pulses with a flushed face","Seizures in severe cases","Polyuria with hyperreflexia"],"c":0,"t":"Sodium Imbalances","fb":"Hyponatremia primarily produces neurologic symptoms from cerebral swelling: headache, confusion, muscle cramps, twitching, and in severe cases seizures or coma.","d":"m","m":[0,1,3]},{"q":"A client with persistent watery diarrhea has a serum sodium of 152 mEq/L. Which clinical finding does the nurse most expect?","a":["Intense thirst, dry mucous membranes, and lethargy","Bradycardia and bounding pulses","Frothy pink sputum and crackles","Hyperreflexia and tetany"],"c":0,"t":"Sodium Imbalances","fb":"Hypernatremia reflects water deficit relative to sodium. The client is intensely thirsty, dry, and neurologically depressed (lethargy, confusion, possibly seizures).","d":"m"},{"q":"The nurse is reviewing a client's EKG, which shows tall peaked T waves and a widened QRS. Vital signs reveal hypotension and the client appears restless. The nurse suspects which electrolyte abnormality?","a":["Hypokalemia","Hyperkalemia","Hypocalcemia","Hypermagnesemia"],"c":1,"t":"Potassium Imbalances","fb":"Hyperkalemia drives faster repolarization, producing the classic tall, peaked T waves along with widening QRS, hypotension, and neuromuscular changes such as restlessness or weakness.","d":"e"},{"q":"A client on a loop diuretic has a serum potassium of 2.8 mEq/L. The nurse expects which findings?","a":["Sharp, peaked T waves and bradycardia","Flattened T waves, U waves, and muscle weakness","Hyperactive reflexes and tetany","Bounding pulses and elevated BP"],"c":1,"t":"Potassium Imbalances","fb":"Hypokalemia delays cardiac repolarization (low and slow). EKG shows flattened T waves, ST depression, and U waves. Clients have muscle weakness, leg cramps, and decreased reflexes.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with end-stage renal disease is found to have a calcium of 7.6 mg/dL. Which assessment finding is consistent with this electrolyte imbalance?","a":["Painful bones and constipation","Lethargy and depressed reflexes","Positive Trousseau's sign and circumoral numbness","Polyuria and kidney stones"],"c":2,"t":"Calcium Imbalances","fb":"Hypocalcemia produces increased neuromuscular irritability. Classic signs include a positive Trousseau's sign (carpopedal spasm with BP cuff), positive Chvostek's sign (facial twitch), tingling around the mouth, and tetany.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client presents with fatigue, depression, constipation, and a history of recurrent kidney stones. Lab work shows an elevated parathyroid hormone level. Which electrolyte imbalance does the nurse anticipate?","a":["Hyponatremia","Hypocalcemia","Hypercalcemia","Hypermagnesemia"],"c":2,"t":"Calcium Imbalances","fb":"PTH pulls calcium out of bone. Hypercalcemia is remembered with the mnemonic moans (CNS depression), bones (bone pain), stones (kidney stones), and thrones (GI upset, constipation).","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with chronic kidney disease has a phosphate of 6.8 mg/dL. The nurse anticipates that the client's calcium level will most likely be:","a":["Elevated","Decreased","Unchanged","Fluctuating with respirations"],"c":1,"t":"Phosphorus Imbalances","fb":"Calcium and phosphate share an inverse relationship. When phosphate rises (as in renal failure), calcium falls. This reciprocal swing is a frequent NCLEX-style test point.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with longstanding Crohn's disease has a magnesium level of 3.2 mEq/L. Which assessment findings does the nurse anticipate?","a":["Hyperactive deep tendon reflexes and tetany","Diminished deep tendon reflexes, flushing, and hypotension","Tall peaked T waves and tachycardia","Hypertension and seizures"],"c":1,"t":"Magnesium Imbalances","fb":"Hypermagnesemia is the great relaxer. The client becomes hypotensive, flushed, hyporeflexic, and bradycardic. Severe cases progress to respiratory depression and cardiac arrest.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with chronic alcoholism is admitted with a magnesium of 1.0 mEq/L. The nurse anticipates which assessment findings? Select all that apply.","a":["Hyperactive deep tendon reflexes","Positive Trousseau's and Chvostek's signs","Bradycardia and flushing","Tremors and seizures","Hypotonic bowel sounds"],"c":0,"t":"Magnesium Imbalances","fb":"Low magnesium produces a hyperexcitable picture similar to hypocalcemia: hyperreflexia, positive Trousseau's and Chvostek's, tremors, and seizures. Magnesium acts as a natural muscle relaxant, so without it everything fires.","d":"m","m":[0,1,3]},{"q":"Using the ROME mnemonic for ABG interpretation, the nurse recalls that:","a":["Respiratory imbalances move pH and CO2 in the same direction","Respiratory imbalances move pH and CO2 in opposite directions","Metabolic imbalances move pH and HCO3 in opposite directions","Respiratory and metabolic imbalances are interpreted identically"],"c":1,"t":"ABG Interpretation","fb":"ROME stands for Respiratory Opposite, Metabolic Equal. In respiratory disorders, pH and CO2 move in opposite directions; in metabolic disorders, pH and HCO3 move in the same direction.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client has the following ABG: pH 7.28, PaCO2 52 mmHg, HCO3 24 mEq/L. The nurse interprets this as:","a":["Respiratory acidosis, uncompensated","Metabolic acidosis, uncompensated","Respiratory alkalosis, partially compensated","Metabolic alkalosis, fully compensated"],"c":0,"t":"ABG Interpretation","fb":"The pH is acidic, the CO2 is elevated (acidic), and the HCO3 is normal. Both pH and CO2 are abnormal in opposite directions, confirming uncompensated respiratory acidosis.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client in diabetic ketoacidosis presents to the emergency department. The nurse anticipates ABG values consistent with:","a":["pH 7.50, PaCO2 30 mmHg, HCO3 26 mEq/L","pH 7.22, PaCO2 30 mmHg, HCO3 14 mEq/L","pH 7.24, PaCO2 56 mmHg, HCO3 25 mEq/L","pH 7.48, PaCO2 42 mmHg, HCO3 32 mEq/L"],"c":1,"t":"ABG Interpretation","fb":"DKA produces metabolic acidosis from accumulated ketones. The pH is low and the HCO3 is low (both moving the same direction, metabolic). Rapid Kussmaul respirations partially compensate by blowing off CO2.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client experiencing a severe panic attack is hyperventilating. The nurse anticipates which acid-base imbalance if respirations continue to be rapid?","a":["Respiratory acidosis","Respiratory alkalosis","Metabolic acidosis","Metabolic alkalosis"],"c":1,"t":"ABG Interpretation","fb":"Hyperventilation blows off CO2 (an acid), driving the pH up. The result is respiratory alkalosis. Clients may also feel tingling around the mouth and in the fingers from associated low ionized calcium.","d":"e"},{"q":"A client has been vomiting persistently for three days. Which acid-base imbalance is most likely?","a":["Respiratory acidosis","Respiratory alkalosis","Metabolic acidosis","Metabolic alkalosis"],"c":3,"t":"ABG Interpretation","fb":"Vomiting loses gastric hydrochloric acid. Losing acid causes the pH to rise and bicarbonate to climb, producing metabolic alkalosis.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which of the following are potential causes of metabolic acidosis? Select all that apply.","a":["Severe diarrhea","Acute kidney injury with retained acids","Diabetic ketoacidosis","Persistent vomiting","Lactic acidosis from shock"],"c":0,"t":"ABG Interpretation","fb":"Metabolic acidosis comes from either losing bicarbonate (diarrhea) or gaining acid (DKA, lactic acidosis, kidney failure). Persistent vomiting causes the opposite picture, metabolic alkalosis.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,4]},{"q":"How does the serum (intracellular) buffer system respond to acute acidosis?","a":["The kidneys produce more bicarbonate over 2 to 3 days","Hydrogen ions move into the cell in exchange for potassium moving out, raising serum potassium","Respirations slow to retain CO2","Albumin precipitates out of solution to neutralize acid"],"c":1,"t":"Buffer Systems","fb":"The serum buffer is the fastest. Cells are potassium-rich, so they swap intracellular potassium for extracellular hydrogen ions. This clears acid from the blood but can drive a dangerous hyperkalemia.","d":"m"},{"q":"The renal buffer system corrects a sustained acidosis by:","a":["Exchanging potassium for hydrogen within minutes","Increasing respiratory rate to blow off CO2","Producing more bicarbonate and excreting hydrogen ions over 2 to 3 days","Releasing aldosterone to retain sodium"],"c":2,"t":"Buffer Systems","fb":"The renal buffer is slow but powerful. Over 2 to 3 days, the kidneys make new bicarbonate, reabsorb HCO3, and dump excess H+ as ammonium. It is the long-term solution for chronic acid-base imbalance.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client in the emergency department has a pH of 7.18. In an otherwise healthy client, how will the respiratory buffer system attempt to compensate?","a":["The respiratory rate will increase to blow off CO2 and raise the pH","The respiratory rate will decrease to retain CO2 and raise the pH","The kidneys will immediately excrete bicarbonate","The serum potassium will fall"],"c":0,"t":"Buffer Systems","fb":"The respiratory system kicks in within minutes. In acidosis, breathing becomes deeper and faster (Kussmaul) to exhale more CO2, an acid, and bring the pH back toward normal.","d":"m"},{"q":"The nurse is teaching a class about the consequences of severe acidosis. Which statement best describes the cellular impact?","a":["Cellular enzymes and proteins denature and stop functioning normally","Sodium-potassium pumps work overtime to compensate","Bone density increases due to calcium deposition","Insulin sensitivity improves dramatically"],"c":0,"t":"Acidosis Consequences","fb":"A low pH disrupts protein structure. Enzymes, transporters, and contractile proteins all rely on a narrow pH range. In acidosis, ATP production falls and proteins denature, which is why severe acidosis is life-threatening.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client returns to the clinic after a week of antibiotic therapy for a sore throat and reports no improvement. Which of the following could explain antibiotic failure? Select all that apply.","a":["The infection was viral, not bacterial","The client stopped the antibiotic early after feeling better","The client took half the prescribed dose","The bacterium has developed resistance to the prescribed drug","The client also took an extra dose at bedtime"],"c":0,"t":"Antibiotic Resistance","fb":"Antibiotics target bacteria only and have no effect on viruses. Stopping early, underdosing, or treating an organism that is already resistant all allow bacteria to survive and contribute to antibiotic resistance.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,3]},{"q":"A throat culture reveals a gram-positive organism. The nurse understands this result indicates:","a":["A bacterial infection where the organism retains crystal violet stain","A viral infection that can be treated with antibiotics","A fungal infection of the upper airway","A parasitic infection requiring antiparasitic therapy"],"c":0,"t":"Gram Staining","fb":"Gram staining is used only for bacteria. Gram-positive bacteria retain crystal violet stain because of a thick peptidoglycan cell wall and appear purple. Gram-negative bacteria stain pink.","d":"m"},{"q":"During viral replication, what occurs during the assembly stage?","a":["The virus attaches to a host cell surface receptor","Viral genetic material enters the host cell","Newly produced viral components are packaged into complete virions","The mature virus exits the host cell to infect new cells"],"c":2,"t":"Viral Replication","fb":"Assembly is the step where newly synthesized viral proteins and nucleic acid come together inside the host cell to form new virus particles, which are then released to infect other cells.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client returns to clinic and reports that his cough and congestion are starting to resolve. The nurse identifies the client as being in which stage of infection?","a":["Incubation","Prodromal","Acute illness","Convalescent"],"c":3,"t":"Infection Stages","fb":"The convalescent stage is the recovery period when signs and symptoms begin to fade. Incubation is silent, prodromal brings vague malaise, and acute illness is full-blown symptoms.","d":"e"},{"q":"A camper develops a target-shaped rash and tests positive for Lyme disease. The nurse identifies the primary route of transmission as:","a":["Direct contact","Droplet","Airborne","Vector"],"c":3,"t":"Infection Stages","fb":"Vector-borne diseases are transmitted by intermediate organisms, often arthropods. Lyme disease is spread by the bite of an Ixodes tick, making it a classic vector-borne infection.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with a positive PPD skin test has a normal chest x-ray and no respiratory symptoms. The nurse explains that this represents:","a":["Active tuberculosis requiring respiratory isolation","Latent tuberculosis with the immune system containing the organism","A false positive that should be ignored","Bacterial pneumonia"],"c":1,"t":"Tuberculosis","fb":"In latent TB, the immune system has walled off the bacteria in granulomas. The client tests positive but feels well and cannot transmit infection. If immunity weakens, TB can reactivate.","d":"m"},{"q":"An elderly immunocompromised client presents with a productive cough that has lasted over a month, hemoptysis, night sweats, and unintended weight loss. Which condition does the nurse suspect?","a":["Acute bronchitis","Influenza","Active pulmonary tuberculosis","Common cold"],"c":2,"t":"Tuberculosis","fb":"The classic triad of chronic cough with hemoptysis, night sweats, and weight loss in an immunocompromised client strongly suggests active TB. Reactivation typically occurs when the immune system can no longer contain the walled-off bacteria.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client steps on a rusty nail and the wound becomes contaminated with bacteria. Which components of the innate immune system are the first to respond?","a":["Antibodies and memory B cells","Cytotoxic T cells","Intact skin, phagocytic neutrophils, and the inflammatory response","Plasma cells producing IgG"],"c":2,"t":"Innate Immunity","fb":"The innate system is the body's nonspecific first line of defense. Physical barriers (skin), chemical barriers (lysozyme, gastric acid), and phagocytes (neutrophils, macrophages) act immediately, before adaptive immunity engages.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client who recovered from COVID-19 is later re-exposed and does not become ill again. Which adaptive immune feature is responsible?","a":["Increased mucus production","Memory B cells producing specific antibodies","Stomach acid","Skin barrier function"],"c":1,"t":"Adaptive Immunity","fb":"The adaptive immune system has memory. After an initial exposure, memory B cells and antibodies (and helper T cells) recognize the pathogen on re-exposure and mount a faster, stronger response.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with seasonal allergies presents with itchy eyes, sneezing, and hives. The nurse expects which white blood cell to be elevated?","a":["Neutrophils","Eosinophils","Monocytes","Basophils"],"c":1,"t":"White Blood Cells","fb":"Eosinophils rise with allergic reactions and parasitic infections. They release mediators that target allergens and parasites, so allergy panels typically show an eosinophilic spike.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which characteristics are true of neutrophils? Select all that apply.","a":["First responders during acute bacterial infection","Primary function is phagocytosis","Elevated counts suggest a bacterial infection","Primarily targets parasites","Mature into plasma cells that produce antibodies"],"c":0,"t":"White Blood Cells","fb":"Neutrophils are the most abundant WBC and are the first to arrive at a site of bacterial infection. They engulf and destroy bacteria through phagocytosis. Eosinophils target parasites; plasma cells come from B lymphocytes.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"Which immunoglobulin is bound to mast cells and is responsible for allergic and anaphylactic reactions?","a":["IgA","IgG","IgM","IgE"],"c":3,"t":"Immunoglobulins","fb":"Use the GAMED mnemonic: G (most abundant, crosses placenta), A (mucosal secretions), M (first responder), E (allergy/anaphylaxis, binds mast cells), D (B-cell surface). IgE drives mast cell degranulation in type I hypersensitivity.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with a known peanut allergy accidentally consumes peanut sauce and within minutes develops wheezing, swelling of the tongue, hives, and a drop in blood pressure. The nurse recognizes this as a type ___ hypersensitivity reaction.","a":["I","II","III","IV"],"c":0,"t":"Anaphylaxis","fb":"Anaphylaxis is the prototype type I, IgE-mediated, immediate hypersensitivity. Mast cells release histamine, producing bronchoconstriction, vasodilation, hives, and hypotension within minutes.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client returns from a hike and develops an itchy, vesicular rash 48 hours after contact with poison ivy. The nurse recognizes this delayed reaction as:","a":["Type I hypersensitivity","Type II hypersensitivity","Type III hypersensitivity","Type IV hypersensitivity"],"c":3,"t":"Hypersensitivity","fb":"Type IV is delayed, cell-mediated hypersensitivity driven by T cells, not antibodies. Reactions take 24 to 72 hours to develop. Classic examples include poison ivy contact dermatitis and the TB skin test.","d":"m"},{"q":"A newborn at 2 weeks of age is brought in for a well-child visit. The mother asks how the infant is protected from illness even before vaccines. The nurse explains this is an example of:","a":["Natural active immunity","Natural passive immunity","Artificial active immunity","Artificial passive immunity"],"c":1,"t":"Immunity Types","fb":"Natural passive immunity is the transfer of maternal antibodies (IgG across the placenta and IgA via breastmilk). It is temporary because the infant did not produce the antibodies themselves.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client receives a kidney transplant and develops rejection within the first weeks. The most likely underlying cause is:","a":["Poor surgical technique","Human leukocyte antigen (HLA) mismatch between donor and recipient","Inadequate hydration","A low-grade urinary tract infection"],"c":1,"t":"Transplant Rejection","fb":"Acute transplant rejection is usually driven by HLA (human leukocyte antigen) mismatch. The recipient's T cells recognize the donor HLAs as foreign and mount an immune attack, which is why HLA matching and immunosuppression are critical.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which feature contributes to the favorable prognosis often seen with Hodgkin lymphoma?","a":["It is typically diagnosed late after widespread metastasis","It tends to remain localized to contiguous lymph nodes with minimal extranodal spread","It rarely responds to chemotherapy","It only occurs in clients over 80 years of age"],"c":1,"t":"Hodgkin Lymphoma","fb":"Hodgkin lymphoma typically spreads from one lymph node region to the next in an orderly, contiguous pattern with little early extranodal involvement. This predictable progression, plus the presence of Reed-Sternberg cells, makes it highly treatable.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which statement best describes acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL)?","a":["Most commonly seen in older adults and arises from myeloid precursors","Most common in children and arises from B-cell and T-cell precursors","Has the slowest progression of any leukemia subtype","Almost exclusively involves the spleen"],"c":1,"t":"Leukemia","fb":"ALL is the most common childhood leukemia. It arises from lymphoid (B and T cell) precursors. AML affects myeloid lines, CLL and CML are chronic adult leukemias.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which of the following are known risk factors for developing an autoimmune disease? Select all that apply.","a":["Female sex","Family history of autoimmune disease","Increasing age","Living at high altitude","Underlying chronic infection or immune dysregulation"],"c":0,"t":"Autoimmune Disease","fb":"Autoimmune disease risk is increased by being female, having a family history, advancing age, and chronic immune dysregulation. Altitude is not an established risk factor.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,4]},{"q":"A client presents with an opportunistic Pneumocystis pneumonia. Which of the following are common causes of immunodeficiency? Select all that apply.","a":["HIV infection","Long-term corticosteroid therapy","Splenectomy","Chemotherapy for cancer","Routine seasonal influenza"],"c":0,"t":"Immunodeficiency","fb":"Immunodeficiency develops with HIV, immunosuppressive medications such as steroids or chemotherapy, and loss of immune organs (splenectomy). Seasonal influenza is not a baseline cause of immunodeficiency.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,3]},{"q":"A client diagnosed with HIV has a CD4 count of 175 cells/mm3. The nurse interprets this finding as:","a":["A normal CD4 count for an HIV-positive client","An indication that the client has progressed to AIDS","Evidence that the client has been cured of HIV","An indicator of acute kidney injury"],"c":1,"t":"HIV/AIDS","fb":"AIDS is diagnosed when an HIV-positive client has a CD4 count below 200 cells/mm3 OR develops an AIDS-defining opportunistic illness, regardless of CD4 count.","d":"m"},{"q":"What is the primary role of CD4 helper T cells in the immune system?","a":["They directly kill virus-infected cells","They produce antibodies against pathogens","They coordinate the adaptive immune response by signaling B cells and other T cells","They engulf bacteria through phagocytosis"],"c":2,"t":"HIV/AIDS","fb":"CD4 cells are the conductors of the adaptive immune orchestra. They release cytokines that activate B cells, cytotoxic T cells, and macrophages. HIV destroys CD4 cells, which is why the entire adaptive system collapses as the count drops.","d":"m"},{"q":"An older adult client is brought to the clinic with new confusion. A urinalysis shows elevated leukocyte esterase and nitrites, vital signs are normal, and there is no flank pain. Which type of urinary tract infection is most likely?","a":["Cystitis","Pyelonephritis","Glomerulonephritis","Renal cell carcinoma"],"c":0,"t":"Cystitis","fb":"Cystitis is a lower UTI confined to the bladder. Symptoms stay local (dysuria, urgency, frequency), and older adults frequently present atypically with confusion. The absence of fever and flank pain argues against pyelonephritis.","d":"e"},{"q":"A client treated for cystitis returns several days later with fever, chills, and severe right flank pain. The nurse suspects:","a":["Resolution of the original infection","Progression to pyelonephritis","A simple recurrence of cystitis","Bladder cancer"],"c":1,"t":"Pyelonephritis","fb":"Pyelonephritis is an upper UTI that has ascended to the kidney. The hallmark is systemic illness (fever, chills, nausea) plus costovertebral angle tenderness and flank pain on the affected side.","d":"m"},{"q":"A nurse is reviewing fluid balance with a student. Which statements about body fluid composition are correct? Select all that apply.","a":["Potassium is the principal intracellular cation","Sodium is the principal extracellular cation","Calcium is more concentrated inside cells than outside","Chloride is the major extracellular anion","Intracellular fluid is regulated mainly by sodium content"],"c":0,"t":"Fluid Distribution","fb":"The sodium-potassium ATPase pump keeps potassium high inside cells and sodium high outside. Chloride is the major extracellular anion (paired with sodium). Calcium is far more concentrated extracellularly, and intracellular volume is regulated by potassium, not sodium.","d":"m","m":[0,1,3]},{"q":"A client receiving aggressive IV fluid resuscitation develops dependent edema and crackles in the lung bases. Which Starling force change is most responsible for this finding?","a":["Increased capillary hydrostatic pressure","Decreased capillary permeability","Increased plasma oncotic pressure","Decreased interstitial hydrostatic pressure"],"c":0,"t":"Hydrostatic Pressure","fb":"Overfilling the intravascular space raises capillary hydrostatic pressure, pushing more fluid into the interstitium. The result is pulmonary and dependent edema even though oncotic pressure and permeability are unchanged.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with severe burns is losing protein-rich fluid through the wound. Which Starling force change is most likely contributing to the edema seen in the unburned tissues?","a":["Increased plasma oncotic pressure","Decreased plasma oncotic pressure","Decreased capillary hydrostatic pressure","Increased lymphatic return"],"c":1,"t":"Oncotic Pressure","fb":"Burns leak albumin through damaged skin, dropping plasma oncotic pressure. With less pulling force inside the vessel, fluid escapes into the interstitium, producing widespread edema beyond the burn site.","d":"m"},{"q":"A nurse is caring for a client receiving an ACE inhibitor for heart failure. Which physiologic effect of the medication is the nurse reinforcing during teaching?","a":["It increases conversion of angiotensin I to angiotensin II","It blocks the conversion of angiotensin I to angiotensin II, reducing vasoconstriction and aldosterone release","It directly stimulates the release of ADH","It increases renin secretion from the kidneys"],"c":1,"t":"RAAS","fb":"ACE inhibitors block angiotensin-converting enzyme so angiotensin I never becomes angiotensin II. Without angiotensin II, vasoconstriction and aldosterone secretion fall, lowering blood pressure and reducing fluid retention.","d":"m"},{"q":"A nurse is reviewing the physiologic actions of ANP and BNP. Which effects are produced when these natriuretic peptides are released? Select all that apply.","a":["Increased sodium excretion in the urine","Vasodilation of blood vessels","Inhibition of aldosterone","Suppression of renin release","Increased sympathetic nervous system tone"],"c":0,"t":"ANP/BNP","fb":"ANP and BNP oppose RAAS. They promote natriuresis (sodium loss), vasodilation, and inhibit renin, aldosterone, and ADH. They actually dampen sympathetic outflow rather than increase it. BNP levels are used clinically to diagnose volume overload in heart failure.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,3]},{"q":"A client with congestive heart failure is admitted with worsening pitting edema and weight gain. Which compensatory responses are working against the client and worsening the fluid retention? Select all that apply.","a":["Activation of the RAAS pathway","Release of ADH from the posterior pituitary","Release of ANP and BNP","Sympathetic nervous system activation","Increased natriuresis"],"c":0,"t":"RAAS","fb":"In heart failure, the kidneys sense low perfusion and inappropriately activate RAAS, ADH, and the sympathetic nervous system, all of which retain salt and water. ANP and BNP are released to counter this but cannot overcome it.","d":"m","m":[0,1,3]},{"q":"A client with small cell lung cancer has serum sodium 118 mEq/L, low urine output, urine sodium 60 mEq/L, and urine osmolality 600 mOsm/kg. Which intervention does the nurse anticipate?","a":["Free fluid restriction and possible hypertonic saline","Aggressive D5W infusion","High-volume oral hydration","Administration of desmopressin"],"c":0,"t":"SIADH","fb":"SIADH is dilutional hyponatremia from excess ADH, often paraneoplastic with small cell lung cancer. Treatment centers on restricting free water; symptomatic or severe hyponatremia may require hypertonic (3%) saline. Desmopressin would worsen SIADH.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client recovering from neurosurgery suddenly produces 9 L of dilute urine in 24 hours. Serum sodium is 158 mEq/L and urine specific gravity is 1.001. Which medication does the nurse anticipate administering?","a":["Furosemide","Spironolactone","Desmopressin (DDAVP)","Hypertonic saline"],"c":2,"t":"Diabetes Insipidus","fb":"Central DI is loss of ADH. Replacing ADH with desmopressin (synthetic vasopressin) restores water reabsorption in the collecting ducts. Diuretics would worsen the water loss.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with central DI asks why the urine is so pale. Which response by the nurse is most accurate?","a":["Your kidneys are filtering out too much sodium","Without ADH, the kidneys cannot reabsorb water, so the urine stays very dilute","Your bladder is producing extra water","The pituitary is releasing too much aldosterone"],"c":1,"t":"Diabetes Insipidus","fb":"Without ADH the collecting ducts stay impermeable to water. Water passes straight into the urine, producing massive volumes of nearly colorless, dilute urine while plasma becomes concentrated and hypernatremic.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client is admitted with severe nausea, vomiting, and seizures. Serum sodium is 116 mEq/L. The nurse anticipates that the priority intervention is:","a":["Rapid infusion of D5W","Cautious infusion of hypertonic (3%) saline with frequent sodium monitoring","Fluid restriction with no other intervention","Encouragement of oral free water"],"c":1,"t":"Sodium Imbalances","fb":"Symptomatic severe hyponatremia (seizures, coma) requires hypertonic saline to gently raise serum sodium. Correction must be slow (under 8-12 mEq/L in 24 hours) to avoid osmotic demyelination syndrome.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which clients are at greatest risk for hypernatremia? Select all that apply.","a":["A nursing home resident with dementia who refuses fluids","A client with uncontrolled diabetes insipidus","A client with persistent vomiting and excess free water intake","A tube-fed client receiving inadequate free water flushes","A client with marathon-induced overhydration"],"c":0,"t":"Sodium Imbalances","fb":"Hypernatremia reflects a water deficit. Older adults with poor thirst, untreated DI, and tube-fed clients without enough free water are classic. Vomiting with free water and marathon overhydration tend to produce hyponatremia.","d":"m","m":[0,1,3]},{"q":"A nurse is preparing to administer IV potassium chloride. Which safety practices must the nurse follow? Select all that apply.","a":["Never administer potassium by IV push or bolus","Use an infusion pump and dilute properly","Monitor the IV site for burning or infiltration","Verify renal function and urine output before infusing","Infuse rapidly to correct the deficit quickly"],"c":0,"t":"Potassium Imbalances","fb":"IV potassium is high risk. It must NEVER be bolused (can cause fatal dysrhythmia), must be diluted and pump-controlled, and the site monitored for irritation. Adequate urine output confirms the kidneys can handle the load.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,3]},{"q":"A client with chronic kidney disease has a potassium of 6.8 mEq/L. The nurse anticipates administering which medications to lower the serum potassium? Select all that apply.","a":["IV calcium gluconate to stabilize the cardiac membrane","Regular insulin with IV dextrose","IV potassium chloride","Sodium polystyrene sulfonate or patiromer","Albuterol nebulizer"],"c":1,"t":"Potassium Imbalances","fb":"Hyperkalemia management is: stabilize the heart (calcium), shift K+ into cells (insulin/dextrose, albuterol, bicarbonate), and remove K+ from the body (kayexalate, patiromer, dialysis). Note the distinction this question is testing: IV calcium gluconate is given first and is life-saving, but it protects the myocardium from the potassium — it does not lower the serum level. Only the shifting and removal agents actually bring the number down. Giving more KCl would be lethal.","d":"m","m":[1,3,4]},{"q":"A client who underwent a total thyroidectomy yesterday reports tingling around the mouth and in the fingers, and the nurse elicits a positive Chvostek's sign. The nurse suspects:","a":["Hypercalcemia from PTH release","Hypocalcemia from inadvertent parathyroid injury","Hypokalemia from anesthesia","Hypernatremia from IV fluids"],"c":1,"t":"Calcium Imbalances","fb":"The parathyroid glands sit on the back of the thyroid and can be bruised or removed during thyroidectomy. Loss of PTH drops serum calcium, producing perioral and digit tingling, Chvostek's and Trousseau's signs, and possible tetany.","d":"m"},{"q":"A nurse is caring for a client with hypercalcemia of malignancy. Which assessment findings are consistent with this electrolyte imbalance? Select all that apply.","a":["Polyuria and dehydration","Constipation","Lethargy and confusion","Bone pain and pathologic fractures","Carpopedal spasm and tetany"],"c":0,"t":"Calcium Imbalances","fb":"Hypercalcemia produces 'moans, bones, stones, and thrones': CNS depression, bone pain, kidney stones, and constipation. Polyuria results from impaired ADH response in the kidneys. Tetany and carpopedal spasm are signs of LOW calcium, not high.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,3]},{"q":"A client on long-term TPN develops weakness, bone pain, and a serum phosphorus of 1.4 mg/dL. The nurse anticipates which intervention?","a":["Sevelamer with meals","Aluminum hydroxide antacids","Oral or IV phosphate replacement","Calcium gluconate IV"],"c":2,"t":"Phosphorus Imbalances","fb":"Hypophosphatemia (under 2.5 mg/dL) impairs ATP production and muscle function. Refeeding syndrome and TPN are common triggers. Treatment is phosphorus replacement, while monitoring calcium because the two are inverse.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with preeclampsia is receiving an IV magnesium sulfate infusion. The nurse should monitor for which signs of magnesium toxicity? Select all that apply.","a":["Absent deep tendon reflexes","Respiratory rate under 12","Urine output under 30 mL/hr","Hyperactive bowel sounds","Hypertensive crisis"],"c":0,"t":"Magnesium Imbalances","fb":"Magnesium is a CNS and neuromuscular depressant. Toxicity shows up as loss of deep tendon reflexes (first), respiratory depression, hypotension, and oliguria. Calcium gluconate is the antidote.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"A client is admitted with weakness, leg cramps, and a magnesium level of 1.1 mEq/L. The nurse anticipates that which other electrolyte is also likely to be low?","a":["Sodium","Potassium","Phosphorus","Chloride"],"c":1,"t":"Magnesium Imbalances","fb":"Magnesium is required for the sodium-potassium ATPase pump to retain potassium inside cells. Hypomagnesemia almost always travels with hypokalemia, and the potassium will not correct until magnesium is replaced first.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client has the following ABG: pH 7.32, PaCO2 60 mmHg, HCO3 30 mEq/L. The nurse interprets this as:","a":["Uncompensated respiratory acidosis","Partially compensated respiratory acidosis","Fully compensated respiratory acidosis","Metabolic alkalosis"],"c":1,"t":"ABG Interpretation","fb":"The pH is acidic and the CO2 is high (respiratory acidosis). The HCO3 is also elevated, showing the kidneys are working to compensate, but the pH is still abnormal, so the compensation is only partial.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with a long history of COPD has ABGs of pH 7.36, PaCO2 58 mmHg, HCO3 33 mEq/L. The nurse interprets this as:","a":["Acute respiratory failure","Metabolic acidosis","Fully compensated respiratory acidosis","Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis"],"c":2,"t":"ABG Interpretation","fb":"The pH is within normal range (7.35-7.45), but both CO2 and HCO3 are abnormal. Chronic COPD allows the kidneys time to retain bicarbonate, fully compensating. The pH is on the acidic side of normal, confirming the underlying problem is respiratory acidosis.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with severe sepsis and lactic acidosis has the following ABG: pH 7.22, PaCO2 26 mmHg, HCO3 12 mEq/L. The low CO2 reflects which compensatory mechanism?","a":["The kidneys are excreting bicarbonate","The lungs are blowing off CO2 to compensate for the metabolic acidosis","The buffer system has failed","The client is hypoventilating"],"c":1,"t":"ABG Interpretation","fb":"In metabolic acidosis, the lungs respond within minutes by increasing rate and depth (Kussmaul respirations) to exhale CO2 and raise pH. A low CO2 in the setting of acidosis is appropriate respiratory compensation.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with a heroin overdose has the following ABG: pH 7.20, PaCO2 78 mmHg, HCO3 24 mEq/L. The nurse identifies the primary acid-base disturbance as:","a":["Metabolic acidosis","Respiratory acidosis","Respiratory alkalosis","Metabolic alkalosis"],"c":1,"t":"ABG Interpretation","fb":"Opioid overdose suppresses the brainstem respiratory center, causing CO2 retention. With a high CO2, low pH, and normal HCO3, the picture is uncompensated respiratory acidosis.","d":"m"},{"q":"The nurse is assessing a client with end-stage renal disease. Which acid-base imbalance is most expected?","a":["Respiratory acidosis","Respiratory alkalosis","Metabolic acidosis","Metabolic alkalosis"],"c":2,"t":"ABG Interpretation","fb":"Failing kidneys cannot excrete hydrogen ions or regenerate bicarbonate, so acid accumulates. ESRD characteristically produces a chronic metabolic acidosis with a low HCO3 and may show compensatory tachypnea.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which client is at highest risk for developing respiratory acidosis?","a":["A client hyperventilating during a panic attack","A client with status asthmaticus and fatigue","A client with severe diarrhea","A client at high altitude"],"c":1,"t":"ABG Interpretation","fb":"When an asthma exacerbation progresses to fatigue, the client can no longer move air, CO2 accumulates, and respiratory acidosis develops. A rising CO2 in asthma is an ominous sign of impending respiratory failure.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which clinical scenarios can cause metabolic alkalosis? Select all that apply.","a":["Prolonged nasogastric suction","Persistent vomiting","Overuse of antacids containing bicarbonate","Severe diarrhea","Diuretic-induced potassium and hydrogen loss"],"c":0,"t":"ABG Interpretation","fb":"Metabolic alkalosis comes from losing acid (vomiting, NG suction, diuretic-driven H+ loss) or gaining bicarbonate (antacid overuse, IV bicarb). Diarrhea loses bicarbonate from the lower GI tract and causes metabolic ACIDOSIS instead.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,4]},{"q":"A nurse is teaching about acid-base buffer systems. Which buffer system responds within minutes by changing breathing pattern?","a":["Renal buffer system","Respiratory buffer system","Serum (intracellular) buffer system","Phosphate buffer system"],"c":1,"t":"Buffer Systems","fb":"The respiratory system corrects pH within minutes by changing ventilation. Acidosis triggers hyperventilation to blow off CO2; alkalosis causes hypoventilation to retain CO2. Renal compensation is slower (days), and the cellular shift is fastest but capacity is limited.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client in DKA has the following ABG: pH 7.15, HCO3 9 mEq/L, K 5.8 mEq/L. The nurse understands the elevated potassium results from:","a":["Loss of potassium in the urine","Hydrogen ions moving into the cell in exchange for potassium moving out","Increased aldosterone secretion","A laboratory error"],"c":1,"t":"Buffer Systems","fb":"In acidosis, cells absorb excess H+ and push potassium out into the serum to maintain electroneutrality. Total body potassium may actually be low, but the serum value rises until acidosis is corrected.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client presents to the emergency department with severe acidosis. Which clinical manifestations does the nurse expect? Select all that apply.","a":["Kussmaul respirations","Confusion or decreased level of consciousness","Hyperkalemia","Hypertension with bradycardia","Cardiac dysrhythmias"],"c":0,"t":"Acidosis Consequences","fb":"Severe acidosis causes hyperventilation (Kussmaul), CNS depression, hyperkalemia from cellular shifts, and dysrhythmias from enzyme denaturation. Hypotension, not hypertension, is more typical.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,4]},{"q":"A client who completed 5 of 10 prescribed days of antibiotics stops taking the medication once symptoms improve. The nurse explains the primary risk is:","a":["A vitamin deficiency","Survival of partially resistant bacteria and recurrent infection","Decreased liver function","Lower risk of future infections"],"c":1,"t":"Antibiotic Resistance","fb":"Stopping antibiotics early leaves behind the hardiest, less susceptible bacteria. These survivors can multiply and develop or transmit resistance, leading to harder-to-treat recurrence.","d":"m"},{"q":"A nurse is providing community teaching about preventing antibiotic resistance. Which actions should be included? Select all that apply.","a":["Take antibiotics only when prescribed for a confirmed bacterial infection","Complete the entire prescribed course even if you feel better","Never share antibiotics with friends or family","Save leftover antibiotics for the next illness","Request antibiotics for every cold or flu"],"c":0,"t":"Antibiotic Resistance","fb":"Resistance is prevented by appropriate use: take antibiotics only for true bacterial infections, complete the full course, and never share or save them. Demanding antibiotics for viral illnesses and stockpiling leftovers both drive resistance.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"A gram stain shows pink, rod-shaped bacteria. The nurse interprets this finding as:","a":["Gram-positive cocci","Gram-positive rods","Gram-negative rods","Gram-negative cocci"],"c":2,"t":"Gram Staining","fb":"Gram-negative organisms stain pink because their thin peptidoglycan layer cannot retain crystal violet. Rod-shaped, gram-negative bacteria include E. coli, Pseudomonas, and Klebsiella - common causes of UTIs and pneumonia.","d":"e"},{"q":"During viral replication, which step does an antiviral medication target when it prevents the virus from binding to a host cell's surface receptor?","a":["Attachment","Penetration","Assembly","Release"],"c":0,"t":"Viral Replication","fb":"Attachment is the first step of viral replication, when the virus binds to a specific receptor on the host cell. Blocking attachment (as some HIV and influenza drugs do) prevents the entire replication cycle from starting.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client visits the clinic with vague low-grade fever, fatigue, and body aches but no specific signs. The nurse identifies this stage of infection as:","a":["Incubation","Prodromal","Acute illness","Convalescent"],"c":1,"t":"Infection Stages","fb":"The prodromal stage is the period of early, vague symptoms (malaise, low-grade fever, body aches). The disease is communicable and clients often feel like 'something is coming on' before the acute illness develops.","d":"e"},{"q":"A nurse provides care for a client with active pulmonary TB. Which type of personal protective equipment is essential?","a":["Standard precautions only","Surgical mask and gloves","N95 respirator and a negative-pressure airborne isolation room","Gown and goggles only"],"c":2,"t":"Tuberculosis","fb":"TB is transmitted by airborne droplet nuclei that remain suspended. Care requires an N95-fit-tested respirator and a negative-pressure airborne infection isolation room (AIIR). A surgical mask is inadequate.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with active TB is started on a four-drug regimen. The nurse emphasizes which teaching point most strongly?","a":["Stop the medications once you feel better","Take all medications consistently for the full 6 to 9 month course to prevent resistance","Take the medications only when symptomatic","One dose per week is enough"],"c":1,"t":"Tuberculosis","fb":"Multidrug-resistant TB develops when therapy is inconsistent. The full multidrug regimen must be completed for 6 to 9 months under directly observed therapy when possible to prevent MDR-TB.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which assessment findings represent intact innate immunity? Select all that apply.","a":["Intact skin","Stomach acid","Antibody production by plasma cells","Mucus and ciliated airway epithelium","Memory T cell formation"],"c":0,"t":"Innate Immunity","fb":"Innate immunity is the nonspecific first line: barriers (skin, mucous membranes), chemicals (stomach acid, lysozyme), and phagocytes. Antibody and memory cell responses belong to adaptive immunity.","d":"m","m":[0,1,3]},{"q":"A client is being taught about the difference between innate and adaptive immunity. Which statement indicates correct understanding?","a":["Innate immunity has memory and improves with repeat exposure","Adaptive immunity is specific to particular pathogens and develops memory","Innate immunity is slower than adaptive immunity","Adaptive immunity is present at birth in its fully developed form"],"c":1,"t":"Adaptive Immunity","fb":"Adaptive immunity is slower to develop but is specific and forms memory cells, so subsequent exposures generate a faster, stronger response. Innate immunity is fast, nonspecific, and has no memory.","d":"e"},{"q":"A client's CBC shows elevated monocytes. The nurse recognizes that monocytes:","a":["Are the body's primary first responders to bacterial infection","Mature into macrophages that engulf debris and pathogens","Are responsible for IgE-mediated allergic reactions","Produce antibodies"],"c":1,"t":"White Blood Cells","fb":"Monocytes circulate in the blood and migrate into tissues where they mature into macrophages. They engulf cellular debris, pathogens, and play a critical role in chronic infections such as TB.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client receiving chemotherapy has an absolute neutrophil count (ANC) of 450 cells/mm3. The nurse anticipates which precaution?","a":["Standard precautions only","Droplet precautions","Neutropenic precautions","Contact precautions"],"c":2,"t":"White Blood Cells","fb":"An ANC under 500 indicates severe neutropenia and high risk for infection. Neutropenic precautions include private room, no fresh flowers or raw produce, meticulous hygiene, and avoiding sick visitors.","d":"m"},{"q":"A nurse is teaching a class on immunoglobulins. Which immunoglobulin is the most abundant and provides long-term immunity by crossing the placenta?","a":["IgA","IgE","IgG","IgM"],"c":2,"t":"Immunoglobulins","fb":"IgG is the most abundant antibody and the only one that crosses the placenta. It provides long-term immunity from past infections or vaccinations and gives newborns natural passive protection.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which immunoglobulin is the first antibody produced during an initial immune response?","a":["IgA","IgG","IgM","IgE"],"c":2,"t":"Immunoglobulins","fb":"IgM is the first responder and is elevated in acute, primary infections. IgG appears later and dominates secondary (memory) responses. The GAMED mnemonic helps: G most abundant, A mucosal, M first responder, E allergy, D B-cell surface.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with secretory IgA deficiency is at greatest risk for which type of infection?","a":["Bloodstream infections","Mucosal infections such as sinusitis, GI, and respiratory infections","Skin infections only","Bone marrow infections"],"c":1,"t":"Immunoglobulins","fb":"IgA guards mucosal surfaces (saliva, tears, breastmilk, GI, and respiratory secretions). Deficiency leads to recurrent sinus, respiratory, and GI infections at those mucosal sites.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client experiencing anaphylaxis develops stridor, hypotension, and hives after a bee sting. Which medication is the priority intervention?","a":["Oral diphenhydramine","IM epinephrine","Oral corticosteroids","Albuterol inhaler"],"c":1,"t":"Anaphylaxis","fb":"Epinephrine IM into the lateral thigh is the first and only life-saving treatment for anaphylaxis. It reverses bronchoconstriction, restores blood pressure, and stabilizes mast cells. Antihistamines and steroids are adjuncts that work too slowly to be primary.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with a history of multiple blood transfusions develops fever, chills, back pain, and hematuria shortly after the start of a new transfusion. The nurse recognizes this as which type of hypersensitivity?","a":["Type I","Type II","Type III","Type IV"],"c":1,"t":"Hypersensitivity","fb":"ABO-incompatible transfusion reactions are type II hypersensitivity. Recipient antibodies bind donor red cell antigens, triggering complement-mediated cell lysis. The reaction is antibody-mediated against specific cells.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with lupus develops glomerulonephritis from circulating immune complexes deposited in the kidneys. The nurse identifies this as which type of hypersensitivity reaction?","a":["Type I","Type II","Type III","Type IV"],"c":2,"t":"Hypersensitivity","fb":"Type III hypersensitivity involves antigen-antibody immune complexes that get trapped in tissues such as the kidney, joints, and skin. Lupus nephritis and serum sickness are classic examples.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client receives a hepatitis B vaccine. The nurse recognizes this as which type of immunity?","a":["Natural active immunity","Natural passive immunity","Artificial active immunity","Artificial passive immunity"],"c":2,"t":"Immunity Types","fb":"Vaccines stimulate the client's own immune system to produce antibodies and memory cells. Because the exposure is intentional rather than natural, it is artificial active immunity and provides long-lasting protection.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client exposed to rabies receives rabies immune globulin (RIG). The nurse identifies this immunization as:","a":["Natural active immunity","Natural passive immunity","Artificial active immunity","Artificial passive immunity"],"c":3,"t":"Immunity Types","fb":"Immune globulin provides ready-made antibodies from another source. Because the protection is given rather than produced by the client, it is artificial passive immunity. It works immediately but only lasts weeks.","d":"m"},{"q":"A nurse is teaching a transplant client about lifelong immunosuppression. Which points are essential to include? Select all that apply.","a":["Take immunosuppressants exactly as prescribed without skipping doses","Avoid live vaccines","Report fever or signs of infection promptly","Use sun protection due to increased skin cancer risk","Stop the medication once the new organ is functioning"],"c":0,"t":"Transplant Rejection","fb":"Immunosuppression must continue for the life of the transplant or rejection follows. Live vaccines are contraindicated. Clients are at higher risk for infections and skin cancers and must seek care for early symptoms.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,3]},{"q":"A nurse is teaching about Hodgkin lymphoma. Which microscopic finding confirms the diagnosis?","a":["Auer rods","Reed-Sternberg cells","Bence Jones proteins","Philadelphia chromosome"],"c":1,"t":"Hodgkin Lymphoma","fb":"Reed-Sternberg cells (giant binucleated B lymphocytes with 'owl-eye' nuclei) are pathognomonic for Hodgkin lymphoma. Their presence distinguishes Hodgkin from non-Hodgkin lymphoma and supports its favorable prognosis.","d":"e"},{"q":"A 65-year-old client presents with fatigue, weight loss, and a high WBC count made up mostly of mature lymphocytes. The nurse anticipates the diagnosis is:","a":["Acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL)","Acute myeloid leukemia (AML)","Chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL)","Chronic myeloid leukemia (CML)"],"c":2,"t":"Leukemia","fb":"CLL is the most common adult leukemia. It involves accumulation of mature but dysfunctional B lymphocytes and progresses slowly. ALL is a pediatric leukemia of lymphoid blasts; AML and CML involve the myeloid line.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with rheumatoid arthritis asks how an autoimmune disease starts. The nurse explains:","a":["The immune system loses tolerance and begins attacking the body's own tissues","Bacteria permanently colonize the joints","The thyroid produces too much hormone","Allergens accumulate in the cartilage"],"c":0,"t":"Autoimmune Disease","fb":"Autoimmune disease results from a breakdown in self-tolerance. The immune system mistakes self-antigens for foreign and attacks healthy tissue. In RA, antibodies and T cells target joint synovium.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with HIV has a CD4 count of 410 cells/mm3, no opportunistic infections, and a viral load of 25,000 copies/mL. The nurse identifies this stage as:","a":["AIDS","Acute primary HIV infection","Chronic HIV without AIDS","HIV cured"],"c":2,"t":"HIV/AIDS","fb":"AIDS is diagnosed when CD4 drops below 200 OR an AIDS-defining illness occurs. With a CD4 of 410 and no opportunistic illness, the client has chronic HIV infection but does not meet criteria for AIDS.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which opportunistic infections are AIDS-defining illnesses? Select all that apply.","a":["Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia","Kaposi's sarcoma","Esophageal candidiasis","Seasonal influenza","Cytomegalovirus retinitis"],"c":0,"t":"HIV/AIDS","fb":"AIDS-defining illnesses include PCP, Kaposi's sarcoma, esophageal/tracheal candidiasis, CMV disease, cryptococcal meningitis, and disseminated TB. Seasonal flu is common in the general population and is not AIDS-defining.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,4]},{"q":"A 24-year-old female client presents with dysuria, urinary urgency, and suprapubic pain but no fever or flank pain. Which intervention does the nurse anticipate?","a":["Hospital admission for IV antibiotics","Short-course oral antibiotics with increased fluid intake","Urgent renal ultrasound","No treatment unless symptoms worsen"],"c":1,"t":"Cystitis","fb":"Uncomplicated cystitis in a non-pregnant woman is treated with a short course of oral antibiotics (e.g., nitrofurantoin or TMP-SMX) plus increased fluids. Fever and flank pain would suggest pyelonephritis and require more aggressive treatment.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with pyelonephritis is started on IV antibiotics. Which assessment finding indicates the infection is improving?","a":["Increasing flank pain","Decreasing fever and improved urinary symptoms","Worsening CVA tenderness","New onset confusion"],"c":1,"t":"Pyelonephritis","fb":"Resolving fever, improving urinary symptoms, and decreasing flank tenderness are signs that pyelonephritis is responding to treatment. Worsening symptoms suggest treatment failure, abscess formation, or possible urosepsis.","d":"m"}]},"2":{"title":"THE CELL TURNS — Ch 3 Cell Injury & Cancer · Ch 4 Sepsis, Pain & Neuro-degen","questions":[{"q":"A nurse is reviewing the chart of a client who experienced a brief drop in coronary blood flow during a stress test, with no evidence of cell death. Which term best describes this event?","a":["Ischemia","Infarction","Apoptosis","Necrosis"],"c":0,"t":"Ischemia","fb":"Ischemia is a temporary, reduced supply of oxygenated blood to tissue without cell death. Infarction and necrosis involve permanent cell death, while apoptosis is programmed (non-pathologic) cell death.","d":"e"},{"q":"A client with severe anemia is admitted with confusion and shortness of breath. The nurse understands that the cells are switching to anaerobic metabolism. Which sequence best explains the hypoxic injury that follows?","a":["Decreased ATP, sodium-potassium pump failure, cellular swelling","Increased ATP, calcium pump activation, apoptosis","Increased oxygen use, lactic acid clearance, cellular shrinkage","Decreased glycolysis, increased protein synthesis, cell repair"],"c":0,"t":"Hypoxic Injury","fb":"Hypoxia forces anaerobic glycolysis, which yields too little ATP to power the Na+/K+ pump. Sodium and water rush in, producing cellular edema and eventual organelle breakdown.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with long-standing hypertension is found to have left ventricular enlargement on echocardiogram. The nurse recognizes this as which cellular adaptation?","a":["Hypertrophy","Hyperplasia","Atrophy","Metaplasia"],"c":0,"t":"Cellular Adaptations","fb":"Hypertrophy is an increase in the size of existing cells in response to greater workload, as seen in a pressure-overloaded ventricle. Hyperplasia would be an increase in cell number.","d":"m"},{"q":"A bedridden client has noticeably smaller calf muscles compared with admission six weeks ago. The nurse understands this is an example of which cellular adaptation?","a":["Atrophy","Hypertrophy","Dysplasia","Neoplasia"],"c":0,"t":"Cellular Adaptations","fb":"Disuse atrophy reduces cell size and overall tissue mass when demand on a tissue falls. Hypertrophy is the opposite response.","d":"e"},{"q":"A client with chronic gastroesophageal reflux is told that the lining of their lower esophagus has changed from squamous to columnar epithelium. Which statements about metaplasia are accurate? Select all that apply.","a":["The cells changed type in response to chronic irritation","Metaplasia can often reverse if the trigger is removed","Metaplasia always progresses to cancer","Metaplasia represents an adaptive cellular response","The new cell type is identical to the original tissue"],"c":0,"t":"Cellular Adaptations","fb":"Metaplasia is a reversible substitution of one mature cell type for another, often as protection against chronic stress. It is not inevitable cancer, though persistent metaplasia can predispose to dysplasia.","d":"m","m":[0,1,3]},{"q":"A client's Pap smear shows cells that are disorganized and varied in size and shape, but not invading nearby tissue. The nurse explains this finding as:","a":["Dysplasia","Hyperplasia","Hypertrophy","Atrophy"],"c":0,"t":"Cellular Adaptations","fb":"Dysplasia is abnormal cellular growth with altered size, shape, and organization. It is a precancerous change that may regress or progress to neoplasia.","d":"m"},{"q":"A school-age client is described as having free-radical damage from secondhand smoke exposure. Which statement best explains why free radicals are harmful?","a":["They are unstable molecules that steal electrons from cellular structures","They neutralize toxins by donating electrons","They are produced only by sunlight exposure","They strengthen DNA bonds during replication"],"c":0,"t":"Free Radicals","fb":"Free radicals carry an unpaired electron and snatch electrons from DNA, lipids, and proteins, causing oxidative damage. Antioxidants help neutralize them.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client asks the nurse which everyday exposures are considered carcinogens. Which would the nurse identify as carcinogenic? Select all that apply.","a":["Tobacco smoke","Ultraviolet radiation","Certain industrial chemicals","Hydration with water","Whole-grain cereals"],"c":0,"t":"Carcinogens","fb":"Carcinogens include chemical (tobacco, asbestos, benzene), physical (UV, ionizing radiation), and biologic agents. Routine hydration and whole grains are not carcinogens.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"A nurse is teaching a community class on viruses that can promote cancer. Which oncogenic organisms should the nurse include? Select all that apply.","a":["Human papillomavirus (HPV)","Hepatitis B virus","Epstein-Barr virus","Helicobacter pylori","Rhinovirus"],"c":0,"t":"Oncogenic Organisms","fb":"HPV, HBV, HCV, EBV, HHV-8, and H. pylori are all linked to specific cancers. Rhinovirus causes the common cold and is not oncogenic.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,3]},{"q":"A client reports unintended weight loss of 15 pounds, persistent fatigue, low-grade fevers, and night sweats over the past two months. Which set of findings is the nurse most concerned about?","a":["General early warning signs of cancer","Typical signs of seasonal allergies","Expected findings for a healthy adult","Side effects of regular exercise"],"c":0,"t":"Cancer Warning Signs","fb":"Unexplained weight loss, fatigue, persistent fevers, and night sweats are general, early constitutional signs of cancer and warrant evaluation.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client is told their tumor is a carcinoma. The nurse explains that this means the malignancy arose from which tissue?","a":["Epithelial tissue","Connective tissue","Lymphatic tissue","Glial tissue"],"c":0,"t":"Cancer Types by Tissue","fb":"Carcinomas arise from epithelial cells and make up roughly 80-90% of cancers. Sarcomas arise from connective tissue, lymphomas from lymphatic tissue, and gliomas from glial cells.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client is diagnosed with osteosarcoma. The nurse knows this cancer originated in which tissue?","a":["Bone (connective tissue)","Skin epithelium","Lymph nodes","Plasma cells"],"c":0,"t":"Cancer Types by Tissue","fb":"Sarcomas arise from connective tissues; osteosarcoma is a bone-origin sarcoma. Lymphomas, carcinomas, and myelomas arise from different tissues.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client is being evaluated for a tumor of the central nervous system. Which cancer type is most consistent with a brain or spinal cord origin?","a":["Glioma","Adenocarcinoma","Melanoma","Leukemia"],"c":0,"t":"Cancer Types by Tissue","fb":"Gliomas develop from glial support cells of the brain and spinal cord. Adenocarcinomas are glandular epithelial cancers, melanomas arise from melanocytes, and leukemias from bone marrow.","d":"m"},{"q":"A pathology report describes a client's tumor as poorly differentiated. The nurse understands this finding is part of which classification process?","a":["Grading","Staging","TNM scoring","Screening"],"c":0,"t":"Cancer Grading","fb":"Grading describes how closely tumor cells resemble normal tissue under the microscope. Poorly differentiated tumors look very abnormal and tend to be more aggressive. Staging describes spread.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client asks the difference between grading and staging. Which statement by the nurse is correct?","a":["Grading describes how the cells look; staging describes how far the cancer has spread","Grading is determined by lab values; staging by patient symptoms","Grading is only used for benign tumors; staging only for malignant","Both terms describe the size of the primary tumor"],"c":0,"t":"Cancer Staging","fb":"Grading evaluates cellular differentiation, while staging (often using TNM) describes tumor size, lymph node involvement, and metastasis.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with breast cancer has been told the disease has invaded the axillary lymph nodes but has no distant spread. Which stage is most consistent with this picture?","a":["Stage III","Stage I","Stage II","Stage IV"],"c":0,"t":"Cancer Staging","fb":"Lymph node involvement without distant metastasis typically corresponds to Stage III. Stage IV requires distant metastasis; Stages I and II are limited to or near the tissue of origin.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with colon cancer is told the cancer has metastasized. Which sites would the nurse expect to be most commonly involved?","a":["Liver and lungs","Pancreas and spleen","Kidneys and adrenals","Stomach and esophagus"],"c":0,"t":"Metastasis","fb":"Colon cancer spreads through the portal venous system, making the liver the most common metastatic site, followed by the lungs. Brain and bone are also common late sites.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client diagnosed with cancer asks how a tumor becomes metastatic. The nurse explains the process in which order?","a":["Local invasion, intravasation into vessels, travel, extravasation, growth at distant site","Apoptosis, fibrosis, calcification, dispersion","Hyperplasia, atrophy, dysplasia, healing","Vasoconstriction, edema, scarring, encapsulation"],"c":0,"t":"Metastasis","fb":"Metastasis involves local tissue invasion, entry into blood or lymphatic vessels, travel to a distant site, exit from vessels, and seeding of a new tumor with angiogenesis.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which features are most characteristic of a malignant tumor? Select all that apply.","a":["Rapid, uncontrolled growth","Poor differentiation","Capacity to metastasize","Encapsulated and slow-growing","Cells closely resemble normal tissue"],"c":0,"t":"Benign vs Malignant","fb":"Malignant tumors grow rapidly, are poorly differentiated, invade surrounding tissue, and metastasize. Encapsulation and well-differentiated cells suggest a benign tumor.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"A client's Pap smear returns showing dysplasia. The nurse explains that this test screens for which cancer?","a":["Cervical cancer","Endometrial cancer","Ovarian cancer","Vulvar cancer"],"c":0,"t":"Cancer Screening","fb":"The Papanicolaou (Pap) test screens for cervical cancer by detecting dysplastic cells in the cervical transformation zone.","d":"e"},{"q":"A client sustains a sprained ankle and notices that the area becomes warm, red, swollen, and painful. The nurse understands these classic findings are produced primarily by which mediator?","a":["Histamine","Bradykinin","Insulin","Acetylcholine"],"c":0,"t":"Histamine","fb":"Mast cells release histamine, causing vasodilation and increased capillary permeability. Warmth, redness, swelling, pain, and loss of function follow.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which effects are caused by histamine during acute inflammation? Select all that apply.","a":["Vasodilation of arterioles","Increased capillary permeability","Vasoconstriction","Decreased blood flow to the injury","Smooth muscle contraction in bronchioles"],"c":0,"t":"Histamine","fb":"Histamine dilates arterioles, increases vascular permeability (causing edema), and constricts bronchial smooth muscle (contributing to wheeze in allergic reactions). It increases, not decreases, blood flow to inflamed tissue.","d":"m","m":[0,1,4]},{"q":"Which findings are characteristic of an acute inflammatory response? Select all that apply.","a":["Onset within minutes to hours","Predominance of neutrophils early on","Resolves within days","Predominance of lymphocytes and plasma cells","Causes fibrosis after a few hours"],"c":0,"t":"Inflammation","fb":"Acute inflammation begins quickly, is led by neutrophils, and typically resolves in days. Lymphocyte/plasma cell infiltration and fibrosis are hallmarks of chronic inflammation.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"A client has been managing an autoimmune disease for the past year and continues to have persistent joint inflammation. Which feature would the nurse expect of chronic inflammation?","a":["Ongoing tissue damage and fibrosis","Rapid resolution within days","Predominance of neutrophils only","Absence of immune cell infiltration"],"c":0,"t":"Inflammation","fb":"Chronic inflammation persists for weeks or longer, with macrophages and lymphocytes producing ongoing tissue damage, scarring, and fibrosis.","d":"m"},{"q":"During the inflammatory response, antibodies and complement proteins coat a microbe so that phagocytes can more easily engulf it. What is this process called?","a":["Opsonization","Chemotaxis","Diapedesis","Margination"],"c":0,"t":"Opsonization","fb":"Opsonization 'tags' pathogens with antibodies or complement (especially C3b) so neutrophils and macrophages recognize and ingest them efficiently.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client has a fresh skin abrasion. Which white blood cell would the nurse expect to arrive first at the injury site?","a":["Neutrophils","Eosinophils","Basophils","Lymphocytes"],"c":0,"t":"Neutrophils","fb":"Neutrophils are the first responders in acute inflammation, arriving within hours. Macrophages follow as the 'clean-up crew', and lymphocytes dominate later in chronic phases.","d":"e"},{"q":"A client presents to the ED with a temperature of 38.9 degrees Celsius, heart rate of 118, respirations of 24, and a WBC of 14,500. The nurse recognizes these findings most consistent with:","a":["Systemic Inflammatory Response Syndrome (SIRS)","Localized cellulitis","Normal vital signs","Mild dehydration"],"c":0,"t":"SIRS/Sepsis","fb":"SIRS is diagnosed by meeting at least two of: temperature >38 or <36 degrees C, HR >90, RR >20, and WBC >12,000 or <4,000. This client meets all four.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with pneumonia is exhibiting fever, tachycardia, tachypnea, and leukocytosis along with a positive blood culture. The nurse documents this clinical picture as:","a":["Sepsis","Pure inflammation","Anaphylaxis","Atrophy"],"c":0,"t":"SIRS/Sepsis","fb":"Sepsis is defined as SIRS plus a confirmed or suspected infection. Severe sepsis adds organ dysfunction; septic shock adds refractory hypotension.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which findings would the nurse expect in a client experiencing systemic inflammation? Select all that apply.","a":["Elevated body temperature","Elevated white blood cell count","Increased C-reactive protein (CRP)","Decreased erythrocyte sedimentation rate","Normal vital signs"],"c":0,"t":"SIRS/Sepsis","fb":"Systemic inflammation produces fever, leukocytosis, and elevations of acute-phase reactants like CRP and ESR. Vital signs are abnormal, not normal.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"A client describes a dull, aching pain in the left calf after a long hike. The nurse classifies this pain as:","a":["Somatic pain","Visceral pain","Neuropathic pain","Phantom pain"],"c":0,"t":"Pain","fb":"Somatic pain arises from skin, muscle, bone, and connective tissue and is usually well-localized. Visceral pain is diffuse and often referred; neuropathic pain is burning and from nerve injury.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with diabetes describes a burning, tingling sensation in both feet that worsens at night. The nurse documents this most consistent with:","a":["Neuropathic pain","Somatic pain","Visceral pain","Acute nociceptive pain"],"c":0,"t":"Pain","fb":"Neuropathic pain results from damage to the peripheral or central nervous system and is often described as burning, electric, or tingling. Diabetic neuropathy is a classic cause.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client steps on a tack. The nurse traces the pain pathway. Which step occurs in the spinal cord, where the signal can be intensified or dampened before reaching the brain?","a":["Modulation","Transduction","Transmission","Perception"],"c":0,"t":"Pain","fb":"The five steps are transduction (stimulus to electrical signal), transmission (along nerves to spinal cord), modulation (signal intensified or dampened in the cord), perception (brain awareness), and behavioral response.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client has had unrelenting low back pain for the past 10 months. Which statement best distinguishes chronic from acute pain?","a":["Chronic pain is non-protective and may not match the apparent injury","Chronic pain always resolves within 2 weeks","Chronic pain protects the body from further harm","Chronic pain only occurs after surgery"],"c":0,"t":"Pain","fb":"Acute pain is protective and proportional to tissue damage. Chronic pain persists beyond expected healing time, may not correlate with visible injury, and serves no protective purpose.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client is in the proliferation phase of wound healing. Which assessment findings would the nurse expect? Select all that apply.","a":["Granulation tissue forming","Fibroblasts producing collagen","Epithelialization at the wound edges","Profuse arterial bleeding from the wound","Black eschar covering the entire wound"],"c":0,"t":"Wound Healing","fb":"Proliferation features granulation tissue, fibroblast activity, collagen deposition, angiogenesis, and epithelial migration. Active bleeding belongs to hemostasis; eschar suggests necrosis.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"A client returns for follow-up several weeks after a deep laceration and is found to have a raised, thickened scar that extends beyond the original wound borders. The nurse documents this as:","a":["Keloid scar","Hypertrophic scar","Dehiscence","Evisceration"],"c":0,"t":"Wound Healing","fb":"Keloids overgrow the original wound margins. Hypertrophic scars stay within margins. Dehiscence is wound separation; evisceration is protrusion of viscera through a dehisced wound.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with Parkinson's disease asks why their tremor and shuffling gait occur. The nurse's best explanation is that there is:","a":["Loss of dopamine-producing neurons in the substantia nigra","Destruction of the myelin sheath in the CNS","Amyloid plaque accumulation in cortical neurons","Loss of acetylcholine receptors at the neuromuscular junction"],"c":0,"t":"Parkinson Disease","fb":"Parkinson disease results from degeneration of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra, causing resting tremor, rigidity, bradykinesia, and postural instability.","d":"m"},{"q":"A young adult client reports intermittent episodes of blurred vision, weakness, and urinary urgency that worsen with heat and then partially improve. The nurse suspects:","a":["Multiple sclerosis","Parkinson disease","Alzheimer disease","Huntington disease"],"c":0,"t":"Multiple Sclerosis","fb":"MS is an autoimmune demyelination of CNS axons, producing variable neurologic symptoms that often relapse and remit. Symptoms classically worsen with heat (Uhthoff's phenomenon).","d":"m"},{"q":"A client newly diagnosed with multiple sclerosis asks what is happening in their body. The nurse explains the underlying problem is:","a":["Autoimmune destruction of the myelin sheath in the central nervous system","Buildup of uric acid crystals in the joints","Degeneration of motor neurons in the peripheral nervous system","Blockage of acetylcholine release at the neuromuscular junction"],"c":0,"t":"Multiple Sclerosis","fb":"MS is characterized by autoimmune attack on CNS myelin, slowing or blocking nerve conduction. ALS targets motor neurons; gout involves urate; botulism blocks ACh release.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with Alzheimer disease has progressed to having difficulty with coordinated movement when buttoning their shirt. The nurse documents this finding as which of the 'A's' of Alzheimer's?","a":["Apraxia","Amnesia","Agnosia","Aphasia"],"c":0,"t":"Alzheimer Disease","fb":"The 5 A's of Alzheimer's include Amnesia (memory loss), Aphasia (language), Apraxia (loss of learned motor skills), Agnosia (failure to recognize), and Anomia (word-finding).","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with advanced amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS) is admitted. Which complications would the nurse most anticipate? Select all that apply.","a":["Respiratory muscle weakness","Difficulty swallowing","Loss of voluntary movement","Loss of cognition and memory","Loss of bowel and bladder sensation"],"c":0,"t":"ALS","fb":"ALS destroys upper and lower motor neurons, producing progressive weakness, dysphagia, and respiratory failure. Cognition and sensation are typically spared.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"A client reports ascending paralysis that began in the feet and is now in the thighs, about two weeks after a respiratory illness. The nurse suspects:","a":["Guillain-Barre syndrome","Botulism","Myasthenia gravis","Alzheimer disease"],"c":0,"t":"Guillain-Barré","fb":"Guillain-Barre is an acute, ascending, symmetric paralysis often following infection (commonly Campylobacter jejuni). Botulism causes descending paralysis.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with a family history of Huntington disease asks about the typical course. Which information is most accurate?","a":["It is an autosomal dominant disorder causing involuntary movements and cognitive decline","It is autoimmune and usually reversible with corticosteroids","It is caused by amyloid plaques and primarily affects memory","It is triggered by viral infection and resolves in months"],"c":0,"t":"Huntington Disease","fb":"Huntington disease is an autosomal dominant CAG-repeat disorder of the HTT gene. Clients develop chorea (involuntary movements), psychiatric changes, and dementia. There is no cure.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with a history of red meat consumption and heavy alcohol use awakens with a swollen, red, exquisitely painful great toe. Which lab value is most likely elevated?","a":["Uric acid","Calcium","Sodium","Hemoglobin A1c"],"c":0,"t":"Gout","fb":"Gout is caused by hyperuricemia leading to monosodium urate crystal deposition in joints. Red meat, shellfish, and alcohol are common dietary triggers.","d":"e"},{"q":"A client diagnosed with fibromyalgia asks the nurse what to expect. Which symptoms would the nurse anticipate? Select all that apply.","a":["Widespread chronic pain","Fatigue","Poor sleep quality","Acute joint deformity with erosion","Butterfly facial rash"],"c":0,"t":"Fibromyalgia","fb":"Fibromyalgia features widespread musculoskeletal pain, fatigue, sleep disturbance, and cognitive 'fog' without joint destruction. Erosive deformity suggests RA; malar rash suggests SLE.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"A client with long-standing rheumatoid arthritis asks why their fingers feel so stiff in the morning. Which feature is characteristic of RA?","a":["Morning stiffness lasting more than an hour that improves with activity","Pain that worsens with use and improves with rest","Sudden monoarticular swelling of the big toe","Skin tightening over the fingers and face"],"c":0,"t":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","fb":"RA is an autoimmune symmetric polyarthritis with prolonged morning stiffness that improves with movement, unlike osteoarthritis. Erosion and deformity develop over time.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client presents with fatigue, joint pain, a butterfly-shaped rash across the cheeks and nose, anemia, and proteinuria. The nurse suspects:","a":["Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE)","Rheumatoid arthritis","Scleroderma","Gout"],"c":0,"t":"SLE","fb":"SLE is an autoimmune disease in which autoantibodies target nuclear material, producing multi-system involvement: malar rash, arthritis, nephritis, anemia, and serositis.","d":"m"},{"q":"A nurse is teaching a client about scleroderma. Which statement best describes the underlying pathophysiology?","a":["Autoimmune endothelial damage and excessive collagen deposition","Loss of dopamine in the substantia nigra","Demyelination of CNS axons","Crystal deposition in synovial joints"],"c":0,"t":"Scleroderma","fb":"Scleroderma is characterized by vascular damage and fibrosis from excess collagen, producing skin tightening, Raynaud phenomenon, and GI dysmotility.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client who ate improperly home-canned vegetables develops blurred vision, dry mouth, and progressive weakness that began in the face and is descending. The nurse suspects:","a":["Botulism","Tetanus","Myasthenia gravis","Guillain-Barre syndrome"],"c":0,"t":"Botulism","fb":"Clostridium botulinum toxin blocks acetylcholine release at the neuromuscular junction, producing descending flaccid paralysis. Improperly canned foods are a classic source.","d":"m"},{"q":"An unvaccinated client steps on a contaminated nail and a week later develops jaw stiffness ('lockjaw') and painful muscle spasms. The nurse suspects:","a":["Tetanus","Botulism","Myasthenia gravis","Parkinson disease"],"c":0,"t":"Tetanus","fb":"Clostridium tetani toxin blocks inhibitory neurons in the CNS, leading to sustained muscle contraction. Trismus (lockjaw), risus sardonicus, and opisthotonos are classic.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client reports drooping eyelids and double vision that worsen by evening and improve with rest. The nurse suspects:","a":["Myasthenia gravis","Botulism","Tetanus","Parkinson disease"],"c":0,"t":"Myasthenia Gravis","fb":"Myasthenia gravis is an autoimmune attack on acetylcholine receptors at the NMJ. Fatigable weakness of ocular, facial, and bulbar muscles worsens with use and improves with rest.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with peripheral arterial disease (PAD) reports calf pain that begins with walking and disappears after a few minutes of rest. The nurse identifies this pattern as which form of tissue oxygen mismatch?","a":["Intermittent ischemia from exertional demand outstripping perfusion","Infarction from a thromboembolic event","Apoptosis triggered by anaerobic metabolism","Necrosis of the gastrocnemius muscle"],"c":0,"t":"Ischemia","fb":"Claudication is intermittent ischemia: when activity raises oxygen demand above what diseased arteries can deliver, tissue becomes briefly hypoxic and pain develops. Rest restores supply, so no permanent cell death occurs.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which mechanisms can cause cellular injury? Select all that apply.","a":["Reduced oxygen delivery to tissue","Exposure to ionizing radiation","Adverse effects from certain medications","Routine glucose metabolism","Normal mitochondrial respiration"],"c":0,"t":"Cellular Injury","fb":"Hypoxia, physical agents (radiation, trauma, temperature), chemical agents (medications, toxins), microbes, immune reactions, genetic defects, and nutritional imbalances all cause cellular injury. Normal metabolism does not.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"A client is found unresponsive after a heroin overdose. Prolonged hypoventilation has produced hypoxic injury throughout the brain. Which intracellular event is most directly responsible for the cellular swelling the nurse expects?","a":["Failure of the sodium-potassium ATPase pump","Activation of the sodium-potassium ATPase pump","Increased aerobic ATP production","Massive efflux of intracellular calcium"],"c":0,"t":"Hypoxic Injury","fb":"Without oxygen, mitochondria cannot make adequate ATP. The Na+/K+-ATPase pump fails, sodium and water accumulate intracellularly, and the cell swells. Calcium actually moves INTO the cell, worsening injury.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client has worked construction in full sun for 30 years and now has thickened, leathery skin on the forearms. The nurse recognizes this is an example of which adaptation?","a":["Hyperplasia","Atrophy","Dysplasia","Metaplasia"],"c":0,"t":"Cellular Adaptations","fb":"Repetitive mechanical and UV stress drives an increase in the NUMBER of epidermal cells (hyperplasia), producing skin thickening. Hypertrophy would be a size increase; metaplasia a cell-type change.","d":"m"},{"q":"A postmenopausal client has a routine endometrial biopsy that shows hyperplasia. The nurse explains this finding as:","a":["An increase in the number of endometrial cells","An increase in the size of existing endometrial cells","A change of endometrial cells into another tissue type","A decrease in endometrial cell number"],"c":0,"t":"Cellular Adaptations","fb":"Hyperplasia is a quantitative increase in cell NUMBER. Estrogen excess is a classic driver of endometrial hyperplasia, which may predispose to dysplasia and carcinoma.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client recovering from a cast removal after eight weeks of immobilization has a noticeably thinner forearm. Which type of atrophy does this represent?","a":["Disuse atrophy","Denervation atrophy","Ischemic atrophy","Endocrine atrophy"],"c":0,"t":"Cellular Adaptations","fb":"Disuse atrophy follows decreased mechanical load on a tissue. Denervation atrophy follows nerve injury; ischemic atrophy follows chronic low perfusion; endocrine atrophy follows hormone loss.","d":"e"},{"q":"A long-time cigarette smoker has bronchial biopsies showing replacement of normal pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium with stratified squamous epithelium. The nurse documents this as:","a":["Metaplasia","Hyperplasia","Dysplasia","Anaplasia"],"c":0,"t":"Cellular Adaptations","fb":"Chronic smoke irritation triggers a protective swap to a hardier squamous cell type. This metaplasia is initially reversible but sets the stage for dysplasia and possible carcinoma.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client smoked for 40 years and now has cells described as 'severe dysplasia' on a bronchial biopsy. Which statements about dysplasia are accurate? Select all that apply.","a":["Cells vary in size, shape, and organization","Dysplasia is considered a precancerous change","Dysplasia may regress if the trigger is removed","Dysplastic cells are by definition invading nearby tissue","Dysplasia is the same as encapsulated tumor growth"],"c":0,"t":"Cellular Adaptations","fb":"Dysplastic cells are disorganized and pleomorphic but still confined to their site. Dysplasia is precancerous and may reverse if the offending stimulus is removed.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"A client asks how free radicals are generated. Which statement by the nurse is correct?","a":["Free radicals form during normal metabolism and increase with carcinogen exposure","Free radicals are produced only by tobacco smoke","Free radicals are stabilized by oxygen","Free radicals only appear after viral infections"],"c":0,"t":"Free Radicals","fb":"Free radicals are an unavoidable byproduct of mitochondrial metabolism, but exposure to carcinogens (UV, radiation, tobacco, pollutants) markedly increases their formation.","d":"m"},{"q":"A nurse is teaching a women's health class. Which exposures are recognized carcinogens? Select all that apply.","a":["Long-term tobacco use","Excessive sun exposure without protection","Asbestos exposure in older buildings","Daily moderate water intake","Regular brisk walking"],"c":0,"t":"Carcinogens","fb":"Chemical (tobacco, asbestos), physical (UV, radiation), and biologic carcinogens are well established. Hydration and routine exercise are protective, not carcinogenic.","d":"e","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"A nurse is discussing oncogenic infections with a clinic client. Which pathogen is linked to gastric cancer?","a":["Helicobacter pylori","Influenza A virus","Rhinovirus","Staphylococcus aureus"],"c":0,"t":"Oncogenic Organisms","fb":"Chronic H. pylori infection causes gastritis and is a major risk factor for gastric adenocarcinoma and MALT lymphoma. The other organisms are not oncogenic.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client expresses confusion about which viruses can promote cancer. The nurse should include which virus as linked to hepatocellular carcinoma?","a":["Hepatitis B virus","Influenza B virus","Norovirus","Rhinovirus"],"c":0,"t":"Oncogenic Organisms","fb":"Hepatitis B and C viruses cause chronic hepatic inflammation that increases the risk of hepatocellular carcinoma. The other viruses do not have an oncogenic link.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client reports a persistent cough, a sore on the lip that has not healed in two months, and a recent change in bowel habits. The nurse should be most concerned that these findings:","a":["May represent CAUTION warning signs of cancer","Are expected with seasonal allergies","Suggest normal aging changes","Indicate a typical viral illness"],"c":0,"t":"Cancer Warning Signs","fb":"The classic CAUTION mnemonic includes Change in bowel/bladder, A sore that does not heal, Unusual bleeding, Thickening/lump, Indigestion, Obvious change in mole, and Nagging cough. Multiple findings warrant evaluation.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which findings would the nurse consider possible early warning signs of cancer? Select all that apply.","a":["Unintentional weight loss","Persistent fatigue not relieved by rest","Recurrent low-grade fevers","Loss of appetite","Improved energy and stamina"],"c":0,"t":"Cancer Warning Signs","fb":"Constitutional symptoms (weight loss, fatigue, anorexia, fever, night sweats) often appear early in cancer. Improved energy is not a warning sign.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,3]},{"q":"A nurse is teaching about the most common type of cancer. Which statement is accurate?","a":["Carcinomas arise from epithelial tissue and account for the majority of cancers","Sarcomas are the most common cancer type in adults","Leukemias arise from epithelial tissue","Lymphomas arise from connective tissue"],"c":0,"t":"Cancer Types by Tissue","fb":"Carcinomas (e.g., lung, breast, colon, prostate, skin) make up roughly 80-90% of cancers and arise from epithelium. Sarcomas arise from connective tissue and are less common.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client is diagnosed with multiple myeloma. The nurse understands that this cancer arises from which tissue?","a":["Plasma cells in bone marrow","Skeletal muscle","Skin epithelium","Glial cells of the CNS"],"c":0,"t":"Cancer Types by Tissue","fb":"Myelomas are malignancies of plasma cells in the bone marrow that produce monoclonal immunoglobulins. They are distinct from leukemias, lymphomas, and carcinomas.","d":"m"},{"q":"A nurse is reviewing pathology terms. Which cancer types arise from connective tissue? Select all that apply.","a":["Osteosarcoma","Leiomyosarcoma","Liposarcoma","Adenocarcinoma","Squamous cell carcinoma"],"c":0,"t":"Cancer Types by Tissue","fb":"Sarcomas (osteo-, leiomyo-, lipo-, chondro-) arise from connective tissues such as bone, smooth muscle, fat, and cartilage. Adeno- and squamous carcinomas arise from epithelium.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"A client's tumor report uses the TNM system. Which statement best explains what this system describes?","a":["Tumor size, lymph node involvement, and presence of metastasis","Cellular differentiation only","Genetic mutations only","Likelihood of treatment side effects"],"c":0,"t":"Cancer Staging","fb":"TNM stands for Tumor size (T), regional lymph Node involvement (N), and distant Metastasis (M). It is the most common staging system.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client asks why their tumor was called 'well-differentiated' on the report. The nurse explains this means:","a":["The cells closely resemble the normal tissue of origin","The cells have spread to distant organs","The tumor is encapsulated and benign","The tumor is in Stage IV"],"c":0,"t":"Cancer Grading","fb":"Well-differentiated tumors look much like their tissue of origin and are typically lower grade and less aggressive. Poorly differentiated tumors look chaotic and are higher grade.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client is diagnosed with breast cancer that is confined to the breast tissue with no node involvement and no distant spread. The nurse expects this to be:","a":["Stage I","Stage II","Stage III","Stage IV"],"c":0,"t":"Cancer Staging","fb":"Stage I disease is limited to the original tissue with no nodal or distant involvement. Stage II usually means small spread to nearby nodes; III, larger nodal spread; IV, distant metastasis.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with prostate cancer is told the tumor has metastasized. Which sites are most commonly involved in distant spread of solid tumors? Select all that apply.","a":["Bone","Lung","Liver","Brain","Hair follicles"],"c":0,"t":"Metastasis","fb":"The most common metastatic sites for solid tumors are bone, lung, liver, and brain. Hair follicles are not typical metastatic targets.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,3]},{"q":"A client asks the nurse to explain how a single tumor can spread to a distant organ. Which step represents the entry of tumor cells into a blood or lymphatic vessel?","a":["Intravasation","Extravasation","Angiogenesis","Apoptosis"],"c":0,"t":"Metastasis","fb":"Metastasis requires local invasion, intravasation (entry into vessels), travel, extravasation (exit), and growth at the distant site with new blood vessel formation (angiogenesis).","d":"m"},{"q":"A client is reviewing imaging that shows a slow-growing tumor with a smooth, well-defined capsule and no invasion of nearby tissues. Which features are most consistent with a benign tumor? Select all that apply.","a":["Slow growth","Encapsulated borders","Cells closely resemble normal tissue","Frequent distant metastasis","Highly disorganized cells"],"c":0,"t":"Benign vs Malignant","fb":"Benign tumors are typically slow-growing, encapsulated, well-differentiated, and do not metastasize. Disorganization and metastasis suggest malignancy.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"A client is scheduled for a colonoscopy at age 50. The nurse explains this screening evaluates for:","a":["Colorectal cancer","Stomach cancer","Liver cancer","Pancreatic cancer"],"c":0,"t":"Cancer Screening","fb":"Routine colonoscopy detects precancerous polyps and early colorectal cancer and is recommended starting at age 45-50 for average-risk adults.","d":"e"},{"q":"A 45-year-old client asks about cancer screening. Which screenings are commonly recommended for average-risk adults? Select all that apply.","a":["Mammography for breast cancer","Pap test for cervical cancer","Colonoscopy for colorectal cancer","Routine annual full-body CT for any cancer","Daily home tumor marker self-tests"],"c":0,"t":"Cancer Screening","fb":"Mammography, Pap testing, and colonoscopy are established population screening tools. Routine whole-body CT and home tumor marker tests are not standard screening.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"During inflammation, a client experiences swelling at the injury site. The nurse understands which vascular change directly causes the edema?","a":["Increased capillary permeability allowing plasma proteins into tissue","Vasoconstriction reducing blood flow","Decreased capillary hydrostatic pressure","Activation of the sodium-potassium pump"],"c":0,"t":"Histamine","fb":"Histamine and other mediators increase capillary permeability, letting fluid and plasma proteins enter the interstitium. The resulting accumulation is inflammatory edema.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client experiences a wasp sting and develops a wheal-and-flare reaction within minutes. The nurse identifies the most likely mediator as:","a":["Histamine released from mast cells","Insulin from pancreatic beta cells","Glucagon from pancreatic alpha cells","Thyroxine from the thyroid gland"],"c":0,"t":"Histamine","fb":"Mast cell degranulation releases histamine immediately on antigen contact, producing vasodilation, edema, and itching that look like a wheal-and-flare reaction.","d":"m"},{"q":"The nurse recognizes which of the following as cardinal signs of acute inflammation? Select all that apply.","a":["Redness","Heat","Swelling","Pain","Pallor and coolness"],"c":0,"t":"Inflammation","fb":"The five cardinal signs are redness, heat, swelling, pain, and loss of function. Pallor and coolness suggest poor perfusion, not inflammation.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,3]},{"q":"A client asks why acute inflammation can be considered beneficial. Which response by the nurse is best?","a":["It limits damage, clears injured cells, and supports healing","It permanently scars all damaged tissue","It always leads to autoimmune disease","It blocks the immune response so cells can rest"],"c":0,"t":"Inflammation","fb":"Acute inflammation walls off and removes the offending agent, clears damaged cells, and releases growth factors that drive tissue repair.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with rheumatoid arthritis has had joint inflammation for several years. Which features distinguish chronic from acute inflammation? Select all that apply.","a":["Persistence beyond several weeks","Predominance of lymphocytes and macrophages","Tissue destruction with fibrosis","Resolves within a few days","Initial neutrophil wave only"],"c":0,"t":"Inflammation","fb":"Chronic inflammation lasts weeks to years, with lymphocytes, macrophages, and plasma cells; it produces ongoing damage and fibrosis. Acute inflammation is brief and neutrophil-led.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"A client recovering from a small wound has neutrophils arriving within hours, followed by macrophages. The nurse identifies the macrophage role as:","a":["Clearing debris and bacteria and orchestrating later inflammatory phases","Producing immunoglobulin in response to antigens","Releasing histamine at injury sites","Forming the fibrin clot during hemostasis"],"c":0,"t":"Neutrophils","fb":"Macrophages are the 'clean-up crew' of inflammation, phagocytosing debris and pathogens and releasing cytokines that orchestrate healing. B cells produce antibodies.","d":"m"},{"q":"During inflammation, neutrophils squeeze through capillary walls to reach injured tissue. Which term describes this process?","a":["Diapedesis","Opsonization","Apoptosis","Modulation"],"c":0,"t":"Neutrophils","fb":"Diapedesis is the movement of white blood cells through capillary endothelial junctions into tissues. Opsonization tags pathogens for phagocytes.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client is suspected of developing SIRS. The nurse evaluates which findings as meeting SIRS criteria? Select all that apply.","a":["Temperature greater than 38 degrees C","Heart rate greater than 90 beats per minute","Respiratory rate greater than 20 breaths per minute","WBC less than 4,000 or greater than 12,000","Blood pressure greater than 140/90"],"c":0,"t":"SIRS/Sepsis","fb":"SIRS requires at least two of: temperature >38 or <36 C, HR >90, RR >20 or PaCO2 <32, WBC >12,000 or <4,000. Blood pressure is not a SIRS criterion.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,3]},{"q":"A client with sepsis develops new-onset confusion, oliguria, and elevated lactate. The nurse recognizes these findings as consistent with:","a":["Severe sepsis with organ dysfunction","SIRS without infection","Localized cellulitis","Anaphylaxis"],"c":0,"t":"SIRS/Sepsis","fb":"Severe sepsis is sepsis plus end-organ dysfunction (e.g., altered mental status, oliguria, elevated lactate). Septic shock adds refractory hypotension despite fluids.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with sepsis has a blood pressure of 78/40 mmHg that does not improve after adequate fluid resuscitation. The nurse identifies this as:","a":["Septic shock","Pure SIRS","Anaphylaxis","Localized infection"],"c":0,"t":"SIRS/Sepsis","fb":"Septic shock is severe sepsis with persistent hypotension requiring vasopressors despite adequate fluid resuscitation. It carries very high mortality.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with appendicitis describes a poorly localized, deep, cramping pain in the abdomen. The nurse classifies this as:","a":["Visceral pain","Somatic pain","Neuropathic pain","Phantom pain"],"c":0,"t":"Pain","fb":"Visceral pain arises from internal organs and is diffuse, deep, cramping, and often referred to other body areas. Somatic pain from skin or muscle is sharper and well-localized.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client who had a leg amputation six months ago reports burning, tingling pain that feels as if it is coming from the missing limb. The nurse documents this as:","a":["Phantom pain","Somatic pain","Visceral pain","Acute nociceptive pain"],"c":0,"t":"Pain","fb":"Phantom limb pain is a form of neuropathic pain experienced in the missing body part after amputation, due to altered central nervous system processing.","d":"m"},{"q":"Two postoperative clients with identical surgeries report very different pain scores. The nurse explains pain is subjective in part because:","a":["Individuals vary in substance P, glutamate, and endorphin levels","Pain receptors only function in certain body regions","Only neuropathic pain is real pain","Subjective pain scores are not clinically relevant"],"c":0,"t":"Pain","fb":"Genetic and biochemical differences in neurotransmitters (substance P, glutamate) and modulators (endorphins, serotonin) affect each person's pain experience. The patient's report is the gold standard.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client steps on a tack. The nurse identifies the very first step in the pain pathway, when free nerve endings convert the noxious stimulus to an electrical signal, as:","a":["Transduction","Transmission","Modulation","Perception"],"c":0,"t":"Pain","fb":"Transduction is the conversion of a noxious stimulus into a nerve impulse at the nociceptor. Transmission carries it to the cord; modulation tunes it; perception is conscious awareness.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client describes severe right shoulder pain after gallstone passage, with no shoulder injury. The nurse explains this as:","a":["Referred pain from a visceral source","Somatic pain from the shoulder muscle","Phantom pain","Neuropathic pain"],"c":0,"t":"Pain","fb":"Visceral pain often refers to a distant body region because of shared spinal cord pathways. Diaphragmatic and gallbladder irritation classically refer to the right shoulder.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which features are characteristic of acute pain? Select all that apply.","a":["Protective in nature","Short duration related to identifiable injury","Resolves with healing of underlying tissue","Has no identifiable cause","Lasts longer than 6 months by definition"],"c":0,"t":"Pain","fb":"Acute pain is protective, time-limited, and tied to a discrete injury. Pain lasting beyond expected healing time (often >3-6 months) is classified as chronic.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"A client has a laceration that is bleeding when the nurse begins to assess it. Which phase of wound healing is currently active?","a":["Hemostasis","Inflammation","Proliferation","Remodeling"],"c":0,"t":"Wound Healing","fb":"Hemostasis is the first phase: vasoconstriction, platelet plug, and fibrin clot stop bleeding within minutes of injury. Inflammation, proliferation, and remodeling follow.","d":"e"},{"q":"A client returns 12 months after an abdominal surgery and the surgical scar is now pale, flat, and softer. The nurse identifies this as which phase of wound healing?","a":["Remodeling phase","Inflammatory phase","Proliferative phase","Hemostasis phase"],"c":0,"t":"Wound Healing","fb":"Remodeling can continue for up to 1-2 years, reorganizing collagen and producing a paler, flatter, more flexible scar. Scar tensile strength reaches about 80% of original tissue.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with diabetes is at increased risk for delayed wound healing. Which factors most directly impair healing in this client population? Select all that apply.","a":["Microvascular disease reducing tissue perfusion","Hyperglycemia impairing neutrophil function","Peripheral neuropathy delaying injury detection","Increased collagen production","Improved oxygen delivery"],"c":0,"t":"Wound Healing","fb":"Diabetic clients have impaired microvascular perfusion, dysfunctional neutrophils, and reduced sensation, all of which delay healing. Collagen production is actually impaired.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"A client returns four weeks after surgery with a raised, thickened scar that stays within the original wound margins. The nurse documents this as:","a":["Hypertrophic scar","Keloid scar","Dehiscence","Evisceration"],"c":0,"t":"Wound Healing","fb":"Hypertrophic scars are raised but stay within the original wound border, unlike keloids, which overgrow it. Dehiscence is separation; evisceration is organ protrusion.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with Parkinson disease asks the nurse what symptoms to expect. Which symptoms are characteristic? Select all that apply.","a":["Resting 'pill-rolling' tremor","Bradykinesia","Cogwheel rigidity","Postural instability and shuffling gait","Ascending paralysis"],"c":0,"t":"Parkinson Disease","fb":"The cardinal motor features of Parkinson disease are tremor, rigidity, akinesia/bradykinesia, and postural instability (TRAP). Ascending paralysis suggests Guillain-Barre.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,3]},{"q":"A client with Alzheimer disease can no longer recognize the face of a longtime friend. The nurse documents this finding as which 'A' of Alzheimer's?","a":["Agnosia","Amnesia","Aphasia","Apraxia"],"c":0,"t":"Alzheimer Disease","fb":"Agnosia is the loss of ability to recognize familiar people, objects, or sensations. Amnesia is memory loss; aphasia is language loss; apraxia is loss of learned motor skills.","d":"m"},{"q":"A nurse is teaching a family about Alzheimer disease pathology. Which statement is accurate?","a":["Amyloid plaques and neurofibrillary tau tangles accumulate, killing cortical neurons","Dopamine neurons degenerate in the substantia nigra","Myelin sheaths in the CNS are autoimmune-destroyed","Motor neurons in the spinal cord are selectively lost"],"c":0,"t":"Alzheimer Disease","fb":"Alzheimer disease is characterized by beta-amyloid plaques between neurons and intracellular tau neurofibrillary tangles, leading to neuronal loss and cortical atrophy. The others describe Parkinson, MS, and ALS.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with ALS is asking the nurse about cognitive changes. Which response by the nurse is most accurate?","a":["Cognition and sensation are usually preserved despite progressive motor weakness","Memory and personality usually decline before motor symptoms","Sensation is lost early in the disease","ALS only affects voluntary cognition"],"c":0,"t":"ALS","fb":"ALS selectively destroys upper and lower motor neurons; cognition, sensation, and sphincter control are typically spared until very late, which can make the disease especially difficult.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with Guillain-Barre syndrome is hospitalized. Which assessments are the most important priority for the nurse?","a":["Respiratory function and ability to swallow","Skin turgor and capillary refill only","Cognition and orientation only","Bowel sounds only"],"c":0,"t":"Guillain-Barré","fb":"Guillain-Barre causes ascending paralysis that can reach the diaphragm and bulbar muscles, leading to respiratory failure and aspiration. Vital capacity and swallow safety are critical to monitor.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with a family history of Huntington disease asks about genetic testing. Which statement by the nurse is most accurate?","a":["Huntington disease is autosomal dominant; each child of an affected parent has a 50% chance of inheriting it","Huntington disease is autosomal recessive and requires two affected parents","Huntington disease is X-linked and only affects males","Huntington disease is not heritable"],"c":0,"t":"Huntington Disease","fb":"Huntington disease is caused by a CAG-repeat expansion in the HTT gene with autosomal dominant inheritance. Each child of an affected parent has a 50% chance of carrying the mutation.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with gout asks how diet may have contributed. Which foods would the nurse advise limiting? Select all that apply.","a":["Organ meats","Shellfish","Beer and other alcohol","Sugary sodas with high-fructose corn syrup","Plain water"],"c":0,"t":"Gout","fb":"Purine-rich foods (organ meats, certain seafood), alcohol (especially beer), and fructose-sweetened drinks raise serum urate. Hydration with water actually helps lower urate.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,3]},{"q":"A client diagnosed with rheumatoid arthritis asks the nurse about disease progression. Which features distinguish RA from osteoarthritis? Select all that apply.","a":["Symmetric joint involvement","Systemic symptoms such as fatigue and low-grade fever","Autoimmune attack on the synovium","Pain that worsens only with activity","Single weight-bearing joint affected"],"c":0,"t":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","fb":"RA is a symmetric, systemic autoimmune polyarthritis with synovitis, morning stiffness, and constitutional symptoms. Osteoarthritis is asymmetric, mechanical, and often single-joint.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"A client with SLE is asking why their disease affects so many organs. The nurse explains:","a":["Autoantibodies target nuclear material found in cells throughout the body","Only the skin produces the affected antigens","SLE only damages joint tissue","SLE is limited to the kidneys"],"c":0,"t":"SLE","fb":"SLE produces antinuclear antibodies (ANA) and immune complexes that deposit in many tissues, explaining the rash, arthritis, nephritis, anemia, serositis, and CNS involvement.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with new myasthenia gravis asks why their symptoms worsen later in the day. The nurse's best response is:","a":["Repeated muscle use depletes available acetylcholine receptors, worsening weakness","Acetylcholine release is highest in the morning","Cortisol levels block ACh release in the evening","Sodium-potassium pumps shut down at night"],"c":0,"t":"Myasthenia Gravis","fb":"Autoantibodies block and destroy acetylcholine receptors at the NMJ. With repeated use during the day, fewer working receptors are available, producing fatigable weakness that improves with rest.","d":"m"}]},"3":{"title":"THE BREAKING POINT — Ch 5 Mental Health/Brain/Stroke · Ch 6 MSK, Skin, Burns","questions":[{"q":"A client recovering from a high thoracic spinal cord injury suddenly develops a severe headache and BP of 210/110. Which vital sign pattern is most consistent with autonomic dysreflexia?","a":["Hypertension with bradycardia","Hypotension with tachycardia","Hypertension with tachycardia","Hypotension with bradycardia"],"c":0,"t":"Autonomic Dysreflexia","fb":"AD presents with severe HTN above the lesion and reflex bradycardia from baroreceptor stimulation of the intact vagus. Common triggers include bladder distention or kinked catheters.","d":"m"},{"q":"A 62-year-old with a long history of poorly controlled hypertension collapses with sudden one-sided weakness and a thunderclap headache. Which stroke type is most likely?","a":["Hemorrhagic stroke from a ruptured cerebral artery","Embolic ischemic stroke from atrial fibrillation","Thrombotic ischemic stroke from atherosclerosis","Transient ischemic attack"],"c":0,"t":"Stroke","fb":"Chronic hypertension weakens arterial walls and is the leading cause of hemorrhagic stroke; the sudden severe headache reflects bleeding into brain tissue.","d":"m"},{"q":"A nurse is teaching a couple planning pregnancy how to lower the risk of a neural tube defect such as spina bifida. Which recommendation is most important?","a":["Begin a prenatal vitamin containing folic acid before conception","Avoid all dairy products during early pregnancy","Increase intake of vitamin A supplements","Start iron supplementation in the third trimester"],"c":0,"t":"Spina Bifida","fb":"Adequate folate intake before and during early pregnancy is the single most effective measure to prevent neural tube defects.","d":"e"},{"q":"A nurse monitoring a client with a head injury observes these new findings. Select all that indicate LATE signs of severely increased intracranial pressure.","a":["Fixed, dilated pupils","Decreasing heart rate","Slow, irregular respirations","Widening pulse pressure","Restlessness and mild confusion"],"c":0,"t":"Increased ICP","fb":"Cushing's triad (HTN with widened pulse pressure, bradycardia, abnormal respirations) plus fixed pupils are late, ominous findings. Restlessness/mild confusion is an EARLY change.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,3]},{"q":"A client with a spinal cord injury at T3 has a BP of 78/50 and a heart rate of 52. The provider diagnoses neurogenic shock. Which vital sign pattern matches this diagnosis?","a":["Bradycardia with hypotension","Tachycardia with hypotension","Bradycardia with hypertension","Tachycardia with hypertension"],"c":0,"t":"Shock","fb":"Neurogenic shock from a high SCI causes loss of sympathetic tone, producing widespread vasodilation (hypotension) and unopposed vagal tone (bradycardia).","d":"m"},{"q":"An 82-year-old who fell three weeks ago is brought in by family because she has slowly become more confused and unsteady. Which type of intracranial bleed is most consistent with this presentation?","a":["Subdural hematoma","Epidural hematoma","Subarachnoid hemorrhage","Intracerebral hemorrhage"],"c":0,"t":"Hematomas","fb":"Subdural bleeds are venous and often develop slowly, especially in older adults with brain atrophy. Subtle, progressive symptoms over days to weeks are classic.","d":"m"},{"q":"A 7-year-old client is being evaluated for ADHD. Which neurotransmitter is typically deficient in this disorder?","a":["Dopamine","Acetylcholine","GABA","Histamine"],"c":0,"t":"ADHD","fb":"ADHD involves low dopamine (and norepinephrine) signaling in attention/executive circuits, which is why stimulants that boost these transmitters help.","d":"e"},{"q":"A school-aged child presents with golden-yellow, crusted lesions around the nose and mouth. The nurse recognizes this as impetigo. How is this skin condition best described?","a":["A superficial bacterial infection of the skin","A deep dermal infection caused by Streptococcus","An allergic reaction to environmental irritants","A viral infection that produces vesicles"],"c":0,"t":"Impetigo","fb":"Impetigo is a contagious, SUPERFICIAL bacterial infection (often staph or strep) producing classic honey-colored crusts.","d":"m"},{"q":"An older adult with osteoporosis develops an exaggerated outward curvature of the upper back. The nurse documents this finding as:","a":["Kyphosis","Lordosis","Scoliosis","Spondylolisthesis"],"c":0,"t":"Kyphosis","fb":"Kyphosis is an abnormal forward rounding of the thoracic spine, frequently caused by vertebral compression as bones weaken.","d":"e"},{"q":"A nurse is reviewing the purpose of the primary survey in trauma care. Select all statements that are TRUE.","a":["It evaluates airway, breathing, and circulation first","It includes a brief neurologic disability assessment","It is performed on unresponsive trauma clients to find life-threatening problems","It examines tissue perfusion distal to a fracture","It focuses on long-term rehabilitation planning"],"c":0,"t":"Primary Survey","fb":"The primary survey is the ABCDE life-threat sweep. Checking pulses/sensation distal to a fracture belongs to the priority (focused) assessment, not the primary survey.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"A client arrives in the ED with a throbbing, unilateral headache, nausea, and intolerance of light and sound. Which type of headache is most likely?","a":["Migraine","Cluster headache","Tension headache","Sinus headache"],"c":0,"t":"Headaches","fb":"Migraines are typically unilateral and throbbing with photophobia, phonophobia, and nausea.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client presents with sudden one-sided facial droop, drooling, and inability to close one eye. Imaging is negative for stroke. The nurse anticipates a diagnosis of Bell's palsy. Which pathophysiology explains the symptoms?","a":["Inflammation and paralysis of cranial nerve VII","Ischemia of the middle cerebral artery","Demyelination of cranial nerve V","Compression of cranial nerve III"],"c":0,"t":"Bell's Palsy","fb":"Bell's palsy is an inflammatory paralysis of the facial nerve (CN VII). It mimics stroke but has no associated limb weakness or speech changes.","d":"m"},{"q":"After casting a client's tibial fracture, the nurse performs the priority (neurovascular) assessment of the affected leg. Which two parameters are included?","a":["Pulses and ability to move (paralysis)","Bowel sounds and skin turgor","Lung sounds and respiratory rate","Reflexes and pupillary response"],"c":0,"t":"Priority Assessment","fb":"The 'six P's' of the priority neurovascular assessment include pulses, pain, pallor, paresthesia, paralysis, and poikilothermia — used to detect compromised tissue perfusion.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with an open femur fracture spikes a fever and reports deep, throbbing bone pain at the wound site. Cultures from the bone grow Staphylococcus aureus. Which condition has developed?","a":["Osteomyelitis","Cellulitis","Compartment syndrome","Osteoporosis"],"c":0,"t":"Osteomyelitis","fb":"Osteomyelitis is a bacterial infection of the bone, classically following open fractures or surgical hardware.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with a tibia fracture reports pain out of proportion to the injury, pain on passive stretch, and a tense, swollen calf. Which statement best explains the pathophysiology of compartment syndrome?","a":["Pressure inside the fascial compartment collapses arteries, leading to ischemia","Bacteria spread through the bloodstream to the bone","Fat globules occlude small pulmonary vessels","A ligament has been overstretched beyond its capacity"],"c":0,"t":"Compartment Syndrome","fb":"As compartment pressure rises, small arteries and capillaries are compressed, causing ischemia and irreversible tissue damage if not promptly relieved.","d":"m"},{"q":"A volleyball player rolls her ankle and is told she has a sprain. How should the nurse describe a sprain?","a":["Overstretching or tearing of a ligament","Overstretching or tearing of a muscle or tendon","Disruption of cartilage in a joint","Separation of two bones at a joint"],"c":0,"t":"Sprain vs Strain","fb":"A SPRAIN injures a LIGAMENT (bone-to-bone). A STRAIN injures a MUSCLE or TENDON (muscle-to-bone).","d":"m"},{"q":"A young athlete tears the anterior cruciate ligament during a soccer match. Years later, which long-term joint condition is she at increased risk to develop?","a":["Osteoarthritis","Osteoporosis","Osteomalacia","Osteomyelitis"],"c":0,"t":"ACL Injury","fb":"ACL injuries destabilize the knee, accelerating cartilage wear and increasing the risk of post-traumatic osteoarthritis.","d":"m"},{"q":"A 60-year-old client says his knee pain worsens by evening after activity and improves with rest. Imaging shows joint space narrowing. Which pathologic mechanism best describes his osteoarthritis?","a":["Wearing down of cartilage so bone rubs against bone","Autoimmune destruction of the synovial membrane","Crystal deposition inside the joint space","Bacterial seeding of the joint capsule"],"c":0,"t":"Osteoarthritis","fb":"OA is a 'wear-and-tear' disease in which cartilage degrades, allowing bone-on-bone contact, osteophytes, and pain worse with activity.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client reports left shoulder pain that worsens when reaching overhead and weakness when raising the arm out to the side. Which finding most supports a rotator cuff injury?","a":["Pain and weakness with abduction","Sharp pain only with elbow flexion","Numbness in the fingertips","Locking of the wrist joint"],"c":0,"t":"Rotator Cuff Injury","fb":"The rotator cuff (supraspinatus especially) stabilizes the shoulder during abduction; pain and weakness with abduction are hallmark findings.","d":"m"},{"q":"A nurse teaches a community group about osteoporosis. Select all factors that increase the risk of developing osteoporosis.","a":["Chronic alcohol use","Low dietary calcium intake","Postmenopausal low estrogen","Weight-bearing exercise","Adequate vitamin D status"],"c":0,"t":"Osteoporosis","fb":"Alcohol, low calcium intake, and estrogen loss accelerate osteoclast activity and bone loss. Weight-bearing exercise and adequate vitamin D are PROTECTIVE.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"A teenager asks the nurse what is causing his acne. Which pathophysiology best explains acne vulgaris?","a":["Excess sebum production with follicular plugging and inflammation","An IgE-mediated allergic reaction","Autoimmune attack on melanocytes","Bacterial invasion of the deep dermis"],"c":0,"t":"Acne","fb":"Increased sebum, hyperkeratinization, Cutibacterium acnes colonization, and inflammation combine to produce acne lesions.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with generalized anxiety disorder is admitted with palpitations and feelings of dread. Which set of physical signs is consistent with an anxiety response?","a":["Tachycardia and elevated blood pressure","Bradycardia and hypotension","Hypothermia and bradypnea","Pinpoint pupils and warm dry skin"],"c":0,"t":"Anxiety","fb":"Anxiety activates the sympathetic nervous system, producing tachycardia, hypertension, diaphoresis, and dilated pupils.","d":"m"},{"q":"A nurse is teaching about depression. Select all factors that are recognized contributors to depression.","a":["Reduced sunlight exposure (seasonal pattern)","Genetic predisposition","Traumatic life events","Unresolved grief","Excessive intake of caffeine alone"],"c":0,"t":"Depression","fb":"Depression is multifactorial — biology, genetics, environment, and life stressors all contribute. Caffeine alone is not a recognized cause.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,3]},{"q":"A nurse explains that an ischemic stroke caused permanent damage to a region of the client's brain. Which term best defines this permanent tissue death from lack of oxygen?","a":["Infarction","Ischemia","Edema","Atrophy"],"c":0,"t":"Infarction","fb":"Ischemia is the lack of perfusion; infarction is the resulting permanent tissue death once oxygen deprivation is prolonged.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client has had two episodes of facial droop and arm weakness that resolved within an hour. Imaging is negative. Which statement about TIA is most accurate?","a":["It is a temporary event that increases the risk of a future stroke","It always causes permanent neurologic deficits","It rules out the possibility of future stroke","It is a type of seizure disorder"],"c":0,"t":"TIA","fb":"A TIA is a transient, fully reversible ischemic event but is a strong warning sign that a completed stroke may follow.","d":"m"},{"q":"A football player struck in the head is evaluated for a concussion. Select all findings that are consistent with this diffuse brain injury.","a":["Brief confusion or memory loss","Headache and dizziness","Impaired coordination","Fixed, dilated pupils","Persistent dense hemiparesis"],"c":0,"t":"Concussion","fb":"Concussions cause diffuse (not focal) injury — transient confusion, headache, dizziness, and impaired balance. Fixed pupils or dense hemiparesis suggest a more severe focal lesion.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"A client with a large epidural hematoma develops one fixed, dilated pupil. Which mechanism explains the pupil change as the brain begins to herniate?","a":["Compression of cranial nerve III by displaced brain tissue","Direct injury to the optic nerve from the original trauma","Ischemic damage to the retina","Sympathetic overstimulation of the pupil"],"c":0,"t":"Herniation","fb":"As ICP rises and brain tissue shifts, CN III is compressed against the tentorium, producing an ipsilateral fixed, dilated pupil.","d":"m"},{"q":"A nurse caring for a client with a large epidural bleed observes BP 198/72, heart rate 48, and irregular slow respirations. The nurse recognizes Cushing's triad. What vital-sign pattern defines this triad?","a":["Hypertension with widened pulse pressure, bradycardia, and irregular respirations","Hypotension, tachycardia, and tachypnea","Hypertension, tachycardia, and Kussmaul respirations","Hypotension, bradycardia, and bradypnea"],"c":0,"t":"Cushing's Triad","fb":"Cushing's triad reflects brainstem compression from severely elevated ICP and is a late, ominous finding.","d":"m"},{"q":"A nurse is teaching about spinal cord injury mechanisms. Select all statements that are TRUE.","a":["The cervical and lumbar regions are most mobile and most often injured","A hyperextension mechanism occurs when the head and neck are forced backward","A flexion injury occurs when the head is forced forward toward the chest","Compression injuries result from axial loading down the spine","The thoracic spine is the most flexible region and therefore the most frequently injured"],"c":0,"t":"Spinal Cord Injury","fb":"The cervical and lumbar regions are the most mobile/most-injured. Flexion, hyperextension, compression, and rotation/subluxation are the major mechanism categories.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,3]},{"q":"A nurse caring for a client with rising intracranial pressure recognizes the most feared complication. Which complication poses the greatest threat to life?","a":["Uncal herniation with brainstem compression","Mild peripheral edema","Transient hypoglycemia","Self-limited fever"],"c":0,"t":"Herniation","fb":"Herniation shifts brain tissue across rigid dural structures, compressing the brainstem and causing respiratory and cardiac collapse.","d":"m"},{"q":"A confused college student is brought to the ED with possible meningitis. Select all findings that support this diagnosis.","a":["High fever","Severe headache","Nuchal rigidity (stiff neck)","Photophobia","Painless, unilateral arm weakness only"],"c":0,"t":"Meningitis","fb":"Fever, headache, nuchal rigidity, photophobia, and altered mental status are classic meningitis features. Isolated painless arm weakness suggests a stroke instead.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,3]},{"q":"A diabetic client has a warm, red, tender, swollen area extending past the foot wound up the lower leg. The provider diagnoses cellulitis. Which layers of skin are involved?","a":["The dermis and subcutaneous tissue","Only the stratum corneum","The hair follicle alone","Only the epidermis"],"c":0,"t":"Cellulitis","fb":"Cellulitis is a deep bacterial skin infection reaching the dermis and subcutaneous tissue, with risk of progression to bacteremia.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client is being taught about preventing fungal skin infections. The nurse explains that fungi thrive in:","a":["Warm, moist areas of the body","Cool, dry skin","Areas exposed to direct sunlight","Heavily calloused skin"],"c":0,"t":"Fungal Infections","fb":"Candida and tinea favor warm, moist environments such as skin folds, the groin, and between toes.","d":"e"},{"q":"A client reports a tingling sensation on the lip a day before painful, fluid-filled blisters appear. The nurse recognizes this as herpes simplex. Which description fits this infection?","a":["A painful vesicular rash often preceded by a prodromal tingling","A painless honey-crusted patch","A scaly, ring-shaped lesion","A widespread maculopapular rash with Koplik spots"],"c":0,"t":"Herpes Simplex","fb":"HSV produces grouped painful vesicles, classically preceded by burning or tingling at the site.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client develops raised, intensely itchy welts shortly after eating shellfish. The provider documents urticaria. This rash is most often associated with:","a":["An allergic (histamine-mediated) reaction","A bacterial deep skin infection","A chronic autoimmune cartilage disease","An obstruction of sebaceous glands"],"c":0,"t":"Urticaria","fb":"Urticaria (hives) reflects mast cell degranulation releasing histamine; it is a hallmark of allergic reactions.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with long-standing varicose veins develops a shallow, weeping ulcer on the medial lower leg. Which pathophysiology best explains a venous stasis ulcer?","a":["Damaged venous valves let fluid and waste accumulate in the tissues","Atherosclerosis blocks arterial inflow to the foot","Bacteria invade the bone of the lower leg","Excess sebum plugs hair follicles"],"c":0,"t":"Venous Stasis Ulcers","fb":"Incompetent venous valves cause chronic venous hypertension; tissues become edematous, poorly oxygenated, and ulcerate.","d":"m"},{"q":"A bedbound nursing-home resident develops a stage 2 wound over the sacrum. Which underlying mechanism best describes the cause of a pressure ulcer?","a":["Sustained pressure reduces blood flow to the skin, causing ischemia","An allergic reaction to bed linens","A primary bacterial invasion of the dermis","An autoimmune destruction of melanocytes"],"c":0,"t":"Pressure Ulcers","fb":"Unrelieved pressure over a bony prominence compresses capillaries, causing ischemia and tissue breakdown.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with extensive burns is at risk for many systemic complications. Select all that are common complications of severe burns.","a":["Hypovolemia leading to acute kidney injury","Wound infection that may progress to sepsis","Impaired thermoregulation","Increased metabolic demand for healing","Spontaneous remyelination of damaged nerves"],"c":0,"t":"Burns","fb":"Fluid loss, infection, heat loss, and hypermetabolic state are all major burn complications. Remyelination is unrelated.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,3]},{"q":"A nurse uses the ABCDE rule to screen pigmented skin lesions. Select all findings that raise concern for melanoma.","a":["Asymmetry between halves of the lesion","Irregular or notched borders","Varied colors within the lesion","Diameter greater than 6 mm","A perfectly round mole that has been stable for years"],"c":0,"t":"Melanoma","fb":"ABCDE: Asymmetry, Border irregularity, Color variation, Diameter >6 mm, Evolution. A stable, symmetric, single-color mole is reassuring.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,3]},{"q":"A client presents with fever, headache, photophobia, and altered mental status. Imaging shows inflammation of brain tissue itself. Which diagnosis is most likely?","a":["Encephalitis","Meningitis","TIA","Cluster headache"],"c":0,"t":"Encephalitis","fb":"Encephalitis is inflammation of the brain parenchyma (often viral). Meningitis inflames the meninges; encephalitis affects the brain tissue.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client arrives after a shallow-water diving injury and is found to be in spinal shock. Which clinical finding is most consistent with this state?","a":["Absent deep tendon reflexes below the injury","Hyperreflexia with clonus below the injury","Severe hypertension and bradycardia","Bounding peripheral pulses"],"c":0,"t":"Shock","fb":"Spinal shock is a temporary loss of all reflex activity below the lesion — flaccid paralysis, absent reflexes, and loss of sensation.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client crushed under fallen debris develops dark tea-colored urine, severe muscle pain, and a serum potassium of 6.2 mEq/L. The nurse recognizes rhabdomyolysis. Which finding pattern fits this condition?","a":["Muscle pain with hyperkalemia and myoglobinuria","Hypokalemia with muscle cramping only","Hypocalcemia with brisk reflexes only","Joint pain with pyuria"],"c":0,"t":"Rhabdomyolysis","fb":"Skeletal muscle breakdown releases potassium and myoglobin into the bloodstream, producing hyperkalemia, dark urine, and risk of acute kidney injury.","d":"m"},{"q":"A nurse responds to a client who is convulsing. The seizure activity has continued for over five minutes without recovery. Which term best describes this emergency?","a":["Status epilepticus","Aura phase","Postictal state","Absence seizure"],"c":0,"t":"Status Epilepticus","fb":"A seizure lasting 5 minutes or more (or repeated seizures without recovery between) is status epilepticus and requires emergent treatment.","d":"e"},{"q":"An adult sustains a femur fracture in a motor vehicle crash. Within 48 hours she develops shortness of breath, confusion, and petechiae over the chest. Which pathophysiology best explains her symptoms?","a":["Fat droplets enter the bloodstream and occlude small vessels","Bacterial infection has reached the bone marrow","A ligament has overstretched","Skin breakdown over a pressure point"],"c":0,"t":"Fat Embolism","fb":"Long-bone fractures release marrow fat into circulation; the triad of respiratory distress, neurologic changes, and petechiae is classic for fat embolism syndrome.","d":"m"},{"q":"A nurse is teaching a parent of a child with eczema. Which statement is correct about this condition?","a":["Eczema often begins in childhood and produces dry, itchy patches, classically behind the knees and inside the elbows","Eczema is a contagious bacterial rash that needs antibiotics","Eczema produces honey-colored crusts on the face","Eczema is best treated by drying out the skin"],"c":0,"t":"Eczema","fb":"Atopic dermatitis (eczema) is a chronic inflammatory skin disorder of dry, pruritic patches in flexural areas; moisturization is a cornerstone of care.","d":"m"},{"q":"A child presents with cough, runny nose, conjunctivitis, high fever, and a red maculopapular rash that began on the face and spread downward. The nurse suspects measles. Which finding is consistent with measles?","a":["Maculopapular rash with fever and respiratory symptoms","Honey-crusted lesions around the nose","A painful single vesicular cluster on the lip","An asymmetric brown growing mole"],"c":0,"t":"Measles","fb":"Measles is a viral illness producing the 3 C's (cough, coryza, conjunctivitis), high fever, Koplik spots, and a descending maculopapular rash.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client trips and strikes her chin on a table edge; cervical imaging shows torn anterior ligaments and instability of the cervical spine. Which mechanism of spinal cord injury does this represent?","a":["Hyperextension injury","Flexion injury","Compression injury","Subluxation injury"],"c":0,"t":"Spinal Cord Injury","fb":"A forceful backward snap of the neck (chin striking forward and head whipping back) overstretches anterior structures — a hyperextension mechanism.","d":"m"},{"q":"A nurse assesses an infant with congenital hydrocephalus. Which finding would the nurse expect?","a":["Increasing head circumference with bulging fontanels","Sunken anterior fontanel","Microcephaly with overlapping sutures","Decreased respiratory rate from birth"],"c":0,"t":"Hydrocephalus","fb":"Infant cranial sutures haven't fused, so excess CSF expands the skull — increased head circumference, bulging fontanels, and 'sunset' eyes are classic.","d":"e"},{"q":"A client with vitamin D deficiency is diagnosed with osteomalacia. Which pathologic process best describes this disease?","a":["Impaired bone mineralization weakening the bone matrix","Autoimmune destruction of synovial joints","Bacterial infection of bone tissue","Crystal deposition in the joint space"],"c":0,"t":"Osteomalacia","fb":"Osteomalacia is softening of bone from defective mineralization, most commonly due to vitamin D deficiency. (In children the same process causes rickets.)","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with a history of disorganized thinking now presents with auditory hallucinations and incoherent speech. Which signs are most consistent with schizophrenia?","a":["Hallucinations and disorganized speech","Episodic facial droop and slurred speech","Sudden unilateral throbbing headache with nausea","Pounding headache with severe hypertension"],"c":0,"t":"Schizophrenia","fb":"Schizophrenia is characterized by positive symptoms (hallucinations, delusions, disorganized speech/behavior) and negative symptoms (flat affect, avolition).","d":"m"},{"q":"A nurse on a rehabilitation unit is caring for a client with a C5 spinal cord injury who suddenly reports a pounding headache, blurred vision, and a flushed face. BP is 198/104. Which action should the nurse take FIRST?","a":["Raise the head of the bed and check for a kinked urinary catheter","Lay the client flat and give an oral antihypertensive","Administer a PRN dose of acetaminophen for the headache","Call the family to provide reassurance"],"c":0,"t":"Autonomic Dysreflexia","fb":"In autonomic dysreflexia, raise the head of bed immediately to lower BP using gravity, then find and remove the trigger (most often bladder distention from a kinked or full catheter).","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with a known C6 spinal cord injury suddenly becomes diaphoretic above the level of injury, with skin that is dry and pale below the lesion. Which statement BEST explains the differential sweating pattern?","a":["Sympathetic discharge below the lesion is reflexive while parasympathetic response dominates above","Parasympathetic discharge below the lesion overrides the sympathetic response","The injury has caused total autonomic shutdown bilaterally","Peripheral vasodilation is occurring uniformly across the body"],"c":0,"t":"Autonomic Dysreflexia","fb":"In AD, a noxious stimulus below the injury triggers massive sympathetic outflow (pallor, piloerection BELOW); the intact parasympathetic response above the lesion produces flushing and diaphoresis ABOVE.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client arrives in the ED with sudden left arm weakness and slurred speech that began 90 minutes ago. CT shows no bleed. The client has atrial fibrillation. Which type of stroke is most likely?","a":["Embolic ischemic stroke","Hemorrhagic stroke","Lacunar hemorrhagic stroke","Aneurysmal subarachnoid hemorrhage"],"c":0,"t":"Stroke","fb":"Atrial fibrillation allows clots to form in the left atrium that can embolize to cerebral arteries — a classic embolic ischemic stroke mechanism.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client is admitted for evaluation after a suspected stroke. Select all manifestations the nurse would expect with a LEFT hemispheric stroke.","a":["Expressive or receptive aphasia","Right-sided hemiparesis","Slow, cautious behavior","Difficulty with reading and writing","Left visual field neglect"],"c":0,"t":"Stroke","fb":"Left-hemisphere strokes typically cause right-sided weakness, language deficits (aphasia), and slow/cautious behavior. Left-sided neglect is a RIGHT-hemisphere finding.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,3]},{"q":"A pregnant client asks how much folic acid she should take before conception to reduce risk of spina bifida. Which response is most accurate?","a":["At least 400 mcg of folic acid daily starting before conception","No supplementation is needed if she eats vegetables","2,000 mcg of folic acid weekly only","Folic acid should begin in the third trimester"],"c":0,"t":"Spina Bifida","fb":"Current guidelines recommend at least 400 mcg of folic acid daily for women of childbearing age, started before conception, to reduce neural tube defects.","d":"e"},{"q":"A nurse caring for a client with a closed head injury notes that the client's MAP is 80 and ICP is 25. What is the client's cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP)?","a":["55 mm Hg","105 mm Hg","45 mm Hg","75 mm Hg"],"c":0,"t":"Increased ICP","fb":"CPP = MAP - ICP. 80 - 25 = 55 mm Hg. CPP below 60-70 indicates inadequate brain perfusion.","d":"m"},{"q":"A nurse is positioning a client with severely increased intracranial pressure. Select all interventions that help LOWER ICP.","a":["Elevate the head of bed 30 degrees","Keep the head and neck in neutral midline alignment","Cluster nursing care to minimize stimulation","Maintain a quiet, dim environment","Encourage Valsalva maneuvers during repositioning"],"c":0,"t":"Increased ICP","fb":"HOB at 30 degrees and a neutral neck promote venous drainage; quiet/dim environments reduce stimulation. Clustering care can actually SPIKE ICP — SPACE care instead. Valsalva raises ICP and must be avoided.","d":"m","m":[0,1,3]},{"q":"A client with a complete T2 spinal cord injury 12 hours ago has BP 80/48, HR 50, and warm, dry, pink skin. The nurse recognizes neurogenic shock. Which IV intervention is most appropriate to address the underlying problem?","a":["Vasopressors after volume resuscitation","Large-volume fluid bolus alone","Diuretics to reduce vascular volume","Beta-blockers to control reflex tachycardia"],"c":0,"t":"Shock","fb":"Neurogenic shock is from loss of sympathetic tone, not hypovolemia. Fluids alone won't fix the vasodilation — vasopressors are needed to restore vascular tone.","d":"m"},{"q":"A young man fell from a ladder and struck his temple. He briefly lost consciousness, then was alert and talking, then rapidly deteriorated to unresponsive. Which type of intracranial bleed is most consistent with this 'lucid interval' presentation?","a":["Epidural hematoma","Subdural hematoma","Subarachnoid hemorrhage","Concussion only"],"c":0,"t":"Hematomas","fb":"An epidural hematoma is arterial (often middle meningeal artery from a temporal-bone fracture). The classic 'lucid interval' precedes rapid decompensation as the bleed expands.","d":"m"},{"q":"A nurse is teaching parents of a 9-year-old newly diagnosed with ADHD. Select all statements that are accurate about ADHD.","a":["It involves dysregulation of dopamine and norepinephrine","Symptoms include inattention, hyperactivity, and impulsivity","Stimulant medications can help focus and attention","Symptoms typically appear before age 12","ADHD is caused by poor parenting alone"],"c":0,"t":"ADHD","fb":"ADHD is a neurodevelopmental disorder with biologic dopamine/norepinephrine dysregulation — not caused by parenting style.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,3]},{"q":"A school nurse evaluates a child with golden-crusted lesions around the mouth. Which teaching point about impetigo is most important to prevent spread?","a":["Keep the child home from school until lesions are no longer draining or until 24 hours of antibiotic therapy","Apply heat to the lesions to dry them faster","Cover lesions with a wool cloth","Share towels only with immediate family members"],"c":0,"t":"Impetigo","fb":"Impetigo is highly contagious. The child should stay home until at least 24 hours after starting antibiotics or until lesions stop draining; do not share towels or linens.","d":"m"},{"q":"A 78-year-old client with osteoporosis presents with new mid-back pain after lifting a grocery bag. Imaging confirms a thoracic vertebral compression fracture. The nurse expects what postural change to develop over time?","a":["Kyphosis","Lordosis","Scoliosis","Pectus excavatum"],"c":0,"t":"Kyphosis","fb":"Vertebral compression fractures in osteoporosis collapse anteriorly, producing the progressive thoracic kyphosis (\"dowager's hump\").","d":"m"},{"q":"A trauma client arrives in the ED unresponsive. The team begins the primary survey. Which assessment is the very FIRST priority?","a":["Airway patency with cervical spine protection","Pupillary response","Pulses distal to extremities","Skin color and temperature"],"c":0,"t":"Primary Survey","fb":"The 'A' in ABCDE is airway — the first priority in any trauma is securing the airway while protecting the cervical spine.","d":"e"},{"q":"A client describes a severe one-sided headache located behind the right eye with tearing and nasal congestion on the same side. Attacks last about an hour and occur in clusters several times a day. Which headache type does this best describe?","a":["Cluster headache","Migraine without aura","Tension headache","Sinus headache"],"c":0,"t":"Headaches","fb":"Cluster headaches are unilateral, periorbital, and accompanied by autonomic features (tearing, rhinorrhea, ptosis); they occur in clusters lasting weeks.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client presents with bilateral pressure-like band around the head that worsens with stress and improves with rest. There is no nausea or aura. Which headache type best fits this presentation?","a":["Tension headache","Migraine with aura","Cluster headache","Sinus headache"],"c":0,"t":"Headaches","fb":"Tension headaches are typically bilateral, band-like, dull, and triggered by stress, with no aura, nausea, or autonomic features.","d":"e"},{"q":"A client with new-onset Bell's palsy on the right side asks why she cannot close her right eye. Which nursing intervention is the priority to prevent complications?","a":["Use artificial tears and an eye patch at night to protect the cornea","Apply ice packs to the affected side continuously","Encourage chewing only on the affected side","Restrict fluids to reduce facial swelling"],"c":0,"t":"Bell's Palsy","fb":"Inability to close the eye (CN VII palsy) puts the cornea at risk of drying and abrasion — artificial tears, lubrication, and overnight eye protection are critical.","d":"m"},{"q":"A nurse performs a focused neurovascular check on a casted forearm. Select all findings that would suggest compromised perfusion and the need to notify the provider.","a":["Pallor of the fingers","Paresthesia (numbness or tingling)","Pain unrelieved by analgesia","Pulselessness in the radial pulse","Warm pink fingers with brisk capillary refill"],"c":0,"t":"Priority Assessment","fb":"The six P's — Pain, Pallor, Paresthesia, Paralysis, Pulselessness, Poikilothermia — flag impaired perfusion. Warm pink fingers with brisk refill are reassuring.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,3]},{"q":"A nurse is reviewing risk factors for osteomyelitis. Select all clients at INCREASED risk.","a":["A client with an open tibia fracture","A client with diabetes and a chronic foot ulcer","A client with hardware from a recent joint replacement","A client receiving IV drug use","A teenager taking a daily multivitamin"],"c":0,"t":"Osteomyelitis","fb":"Open fractures, chronic ulcers, indwelling hardware, and IV drug use all introduce or enable bacterial seeding of bone.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,3]},{"q":"A client with a long-leg cast applied two hours ago reports severe deep pain not relieved by morphine, along with numbness in the toes. The nurse should suspect:","a":["Compartment syndrome","Routine post-cast soreness","Cellulitis","Mild bruising under the cast"],"c":0,"t":"Compartment Syndrome","fb":"Pain out of proportion to the injury, especially unrelieved by opioids, plus paresthesia, is the cardinal early warning of compartment syndrome — an emergency.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with a fresh casted leg develops compartment syndrome. Which action by the nurse is the priority?","a":["Bivalve or remove the cast and notify the provider immediately","Elevate the leg above heart level","Apply ice to reduce swelling","Encourage the client to wiggle the toes"],"c":0,"t":"Compartment Syndrome","fb":"Removing the constricting cast relieves pressure; elevating ABOVE heart level can actually WORSEN perfusion in compartment syndrome — keep at heart level only.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client asks the nurse to explain the difference between a sprain and a strain. Which explanation is correct?","a":["A sprain injures a ligament; a strain injures a muscle or tendon","A sprain injures a tendon; a strain injures a ligament","A sprain involves a bone; a strain involves cartilage","Both terms refer to the same injury"],"c":0,"t":"Sprain vs Strain","fb":"Memory aid: spraIn = lIgament; strAIN = muscle/tendon (Always Includes Nuisance). Same general management with RICE.","d":"e"},{"q":"A soccer player twists her knee and reports hearing a 'pop' followed by immediate swelling and instability when bearing weight. Which injury is most likely?","a":["ACL tear","Lateral malleolus fracture","Calcaneal stress fracture","Plantar fasciitis flare"],"c":0,"t":"ACL Injury","fb":"A loud pop with immediate swelling and instability after a pivoting or twisting maneuver is the classic ACL tear presentation.","d":"m"},{"q":"A 68-year-old with osteoarthritis of both knees reports morning stiffness lasting about 15 minutes that improves with movement. Which best distinguishes osteoarthritis from rheumatoid arthritis?","a":["OA has brief morning stiffness while RA has prolonged morning stiffness over an hour","OA is autoimmune and RA is degenerative","OA involves only the small joints","OA is always bilateral and symmetric"],"c":0,"t":"Osteoarthritis","fb":"OA stiffness is brief (under 30 minutes) and improves with movement; RA stiffness lasts longer than an hour and is autoimmune and symmetric.","d":"m"},{"q":"A construction worker reports right shoulder pain that wakes him at night and weakness when lifting his arm above his head. Imaging confirms a rotator cuff tear. Which muscle of the rotator cuff is most often involved?","a":["Supraspinatus","Pectoralis major","Latissimus dorsi","Biceps brachii"],"c":0,"t":"Rotator Cuff Injury","fb":"The supraspinatus is most commonly torn because it passes under the acromion and is most subject to impingement.","d":"m"},{"q":"A nurse is teaching a community group about osteoporosis prevention. Which statement should be included?","a":["Weight-bearing exercise like walking helps maintain bone density","Smoking has no effect on bone strength","Calcium supplements alone replace the need for vitamin D","Bedrest is the best way to protect fragile bones"],"c":0,"t":"Osteoporosis","fb":"Weight-bearing and resistance exercise stimulate osteoblast activity. Smoking, immobility, and low vitamin D worsen bone loss.","d":"m"},{"q":"A 16-year-old with severe nodulocystic acne asks why his skin is so oily. Which structures in the skin produce the oil involved in acne?","a":["Sebaceous glands","Eccrine sweat glands","Apocrine glands","Melanocytes"],"c":0,"t":"Acne","fb":"Sebaceous glands secrete sebum. Excess sebum combined with hyperkeratinization plugs follicles and allows Cutibacterium acnes to proliferate.","d":"e"},{"q":"A nurse is screening clients for anxiety disorders. Select all symptoms that are consistent with generalized anxiety disorder.","a":["Persistent worry that is difficult to control","Muscle tension","Sleep disturbance","Irritability","Flat affect with poverty of speech"],"c":0,"t":"Anxiety","fb":"GAD features include excessive worry, restlessness, fatigue, muscle tension, irritability, and sleep disturbance. Flat affect is a NEGATIVE symptom of schizophrenia.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,3]},{"q":"A client with depression is started on a selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor. The nurse explains the medication targets which neurotransmitter imbalance?","a":["Low serotonin activity in synapses","Excessive dopamine release","Low acetylcholine in muscle","Excessive GABA inhibition"],"c":0,"t":"Depression","fb":"The monoamine hypothesis links depression with reduced synaptic serotonin (and norepinephrine). SSRIs block serotonin reuptake, raising synaptic levels.","d":"m"},{"q":"A nurse is teaching a client recovering from an ischemic stroke about secondary prevention. Select all modifiable risk factors the nurse should emphasize.","a":["Hypertension","Smoking","High cholesterol","Diabetes management","Family history"],"c":0,"t":"Stroke","fb":"HTN, smoking, hyperlipidemia, and uncontrolled diabetes are all modifiable. Family history is non-modifiable.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,3]},{"q":"A client had a sudden episode of right arm numbness and slurred speech that resolved within 30 minutes. CT is unremarkable. What is the nurse's priority teaching point?","a":["A TIA is a warning that places the client at high risk for a future stroke","A TIA always rules out future cerebrovascular events","No further follow-up is needed once symptoms resolve","TIAs cause permanent neurologic deficits"],"c":0,"t":"TIA","fb":"A TIA carries roughly a 10-15% short-term stroke risk; aggressive workup and risk-factor modification are essential.","d":"m"},{"q":"A high school athlete is evaluated after a sideline head impact. He is alert and oriented but reports headache and feeling 'foggy.' Which return-to-play recommendation is most appropriate?","a":["No return to play that day and gradual reintroduction once symptom-free","Return to play immediately if he can name the date","Return after a 10-minute rest if helmet is intact","Return after a single dose of acetaminophen"],"c":0,"t":"Concussion","fb":"Current concussion guidelines mandate same-day removal from play and a stepwise return only once asymptomatic at rest and with exertion.","d":"m"},{"q":"A nurse caring for a client with rising ICP recognizes which sign as the EARLIEST indicator of neurologic deterioration?","a":["A change in level of consciousness","Fixed and dilated pupils","Cushing's triad","Decerebrate posturing"],"c":0,"t":"Increased ICP","fb":"A subtle decrease in level of consciousness or increased restlessness is the earliest and most sensitive sign of rising ICP. The others are LATE.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with a large epidural hematoma has unilateral pupil dilation. Which type of brain herniation is most often associated with this finding?","a":["Uncal herniation","Cingulate herniation","Tonsillar herniation","Upward herniation"],"c":0,"t":"Herniation","fb":"In uncal herniation, the medial temporal lobe (uncus) herniates over the tentorial edge and compresses CN III, producing an ipsilateral fixed, dilated pupil.","d":"m"},{"q":"A nurse caring for a client with a large intracranial bleed observes BP 200/68, HR 44, and irregular respirations. Which intervention takes priority?","a":["Notify the provider and prepare to manage rising ICP","Administer an IV beta-blocker to lower the heart rate","Restrict head-of-bed elevation to flat","Begin oral antihypertensives"],"c":0,"t":"Cushing's Triad","fb":"Cushing's triad signals imminent herniation. Emergent intervention is required — the provider must be notified and ICP-lowering measures begun immediately.","d":"m"},{"q":"A nurse is reviewing precautions for a client with a possible cervical spinal cord injury at the scene of an accident. Which action is most appropriate?","a":["Maintain in-line cervical stabilization until the spine is cleared","Quickly turn the head to assess for ear bleeding","Position the client in high Fowler's","Allow the client to walk to the stretcher"],"c":0,"t":"Spinal Cord Injury","fb":"Any suspected cervical injury requires immediate manual in-line stabilization to prevent secondary cord damage from movement.","d":"m"},{"q":"A nurse is teaching a parent of a child with bacterial meningitis about isolation. Which type of precautions should be initiated?","a":["Droplet precautions until at least 24 hours of effective antibiotic therapy","Airborne precautions for the duration of illness","Contact precautions only","Standard precautions are sufficient"],"c":0,"t":"Meningitis","fb":"Bacterial meningitis (e.g., meningococcal) is spread by respiratory droplets; droplet precautions are maintained for at least 24 hours of appropriate antibiotic treatment.","d":"m"},{"q":"A nurse assesses a client suspected of meningitis. Which positive finding indicates meningeal irritation?","a":["Pain and flexion of the hips and knees when the neck is flexed (Brudzinski sign)","Babinski reflex","Hyperactive bowel sounds","A positive Homans sign"],"c":0,"t":"Meningitis","fb":"Brudzinski (involuntary hip/knee flexion with neck flexion) and Kernig (resistance to knee extension when hip is flexed) both indicate meningeal irritation.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with diabetes has a warm, red, swollen lower leg with poorly defined borders. The provider diagnoses cellulitis. Which finding would alert the nurse to systemic spread?","a":["New fever and rising white blood cell count","Decreasing redness at the site","Stable blood pressure","Reduced pain at the lesion"],"c":0,"t":"Cellulitis","fb":"Fever, leukocytosis, lymphangitis (red streaking), and hemodynamic changes signal systemic infection or bacteremia — a serious complication of cellulitis.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client is being treated for a tinea pedis infection. Which teaching point will best help prevent recurrence?","a":["Keep feet clean and dry; change socks frequently","Wear closed, damp shoes after showering","Share towels with household members","Apply heavy occlusive ointment all day"],"c":0,"t":"Fungal Infections","fb":"Tinea fungi thrive in warm, damp environments. Dry skin, breathable footwear, dedicated towels, and topical antifungals reduce recurrence.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client reports recurrent painful clusters of vesicles on the lip during periods of stress. Which best describes the pathophysiology of these herpes simplex outbreaks?","a":["The virus stays latent in sensory ganglia and reactivates with triggers","The bacteria reinfect the lip each episode","An autoimmune attack on the lip recurs","Fungal spores germinate during stress"],"c":0,"t":"Herpes Simplex","fb":"After primary HSV infection, the virus remains dormant in the trigeminal ganglion and reactivates with triggers (stress, illness, sunlight, fever).","d":"m"},{"q":"A client develops a widespread itchy rash with raised wheals after taking a new antibiotic. Which treatment addresses the underlying pathophysiology of urticaria?","a":["Antihistamines to block H1 receptors","Topical antifungals","Oral antiviral therapy","Topical antibiotics"],"c":0,"t":"Urticaria","fb":"Urticaria is histamine-mediated; H1 blockers (e.g., diphenhydramine, cetirizine) directly counter the mast cell response.","d":"m"},{"q":"A nurse caring for a client with chronic venous insufficiency provides teaching about preventing venous stasis ulcers. Select all appropriate recommendations.","a":["Wear graduated compression stockings during the day","Elevate the legs above heart level when sitting","Perform regular ankle pumps and walking","Inspect feet and legs daily","Apply tight, knee-high garters to support circulation"],"c":0,"t":"Venous Stasis Ulcers","fb":"Compression, elevation, mobility, and skin inspection are the cornerstones. Garters constrict the venous return and worsen stasis.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,3]},{"q":"A bedbound nursing-home resident has nonblanchable redness over the sacrum that is intact. The nurse correctly classifies this pressure injury as:","a":["Stage 1","Stage 2","Stage 3","Stage 4"],"c":0,"t":"Pressure Ulcers","fb":"Stage 1 = intact skin with NONBLANCHABLE redness. Stage 2 = partial-thickness with shallow open ulcer or blister; Stage 3 = full-thickness with visible fat; Stage 4 = exposed bone/tendon/muscle.","d":"m"},{"q":"A nurse caring for a client with full-thickness burns over 35% body surface area is most concerned in the first 24 hours about:","a":["Hypovolemic shock from massive fluid shifts","Wound contractures","Hypertrophic scar formation","Itching during healing"],"c":0,"t":"Burns","fb":"In the resuscitative phase (first 24-48 hours), fluid shifts into the interstitium cause hypovolemia and burn shock — the immediate life threat.","d":"m"},{"q":"A nurse is screening pigmented lesions. Which lesion description is MOST suspicious for melanoma?","a":["A 9 mm asymmetric mole with notched borders and three different colors","A symmetric 3 mm tan mole present since childhood","A round, evenly pigmented brown freckle","A 4 mm raised, soft, skin-colored papule"],"c":0,"t":"Melanoma","fb":"ABCDE — Asymmetry, Border irregularity, Color variation, Diameter >6 mm, Evolution. A 9 mm mole meeting multiple criteria is highly suspicious.","d":"m"},{"q":"A college student arrives in the ED febrile, photophobic, and confused. The provider performs a lumbar puncture. Which CSF profile is most consistent with VIRAL encephalitis?","a":["Lymphocytic pleocytosis with normal glucose","Neutrophilic pleocytosis with low glucose","Bloody fluid with xanthochromia","Clear fluid with normal cell count and high protein only"],"c":0,"t":"Encephalitis","fb":"Viral encephalitis typically shows lymphocyte-predominant pleocytosis with normal glucose. Bacterial meningitis shows neutrophilic pleocytosis with LOW glucose.","d":"m"},{"q":"A nurse is caring for a client with a thoracic spinal cord injury one week ago. Reflexes are absent and the bladder is flaccid. Which condition best explains these findings?","a":["Spinal shock","Autonomic dysreflexia","Neurogenic shock","Cauda equina syndrome"],"c":0,"t":"Shock","fb":"Spinal shock is the acute, temporary loss of all reflex activity below the lesion immediately after SCI; flaccid paralysis and areflexic bladder are classic.","d":"m"},{"q":"A crush-injury client is admitted with rhabdomyolysis. Which laboratory result is the nurse most concerned about because of its immediate cardiac risk?","a":["Potassium 6.8 mEq/L","Calcium 8.4 mg/dL","Sodium 138 mEq/L","Magnesium 1.9 mg/dL"],"c":0,"t":"Rhabdomyolysis","fb":"Hyperkalemia from cellular lysis can trigger lethal dysrhythmias. Aggressive IV fluids and management of potassium are top priorities.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client in the ED is convulsing and has been actively seizing for 7 minutes. The nurse recognizes status epilepticus and prepares to administer:","a":["IV lorazepam","Oral phenytoin","IM diphenhydramine","Subcutaneous insulin"],"c":0,"t":"Status Epilepticus","fb":"First-line treatment for status epilepticus is a benzodiazepine (IV lorazepam or midazolam) to abort the seizure activity.","d":"m"},{"q":"A 24-year-old with a closed femur fracture develops sudden dyspnea, confusion, and petechiae across the chest and axillae 36 hours after injury. Which intervention is most likely needed urgently?","a":["Supplemental oxygen and respiratory support","Topical antibiotic ointment to the rash","Oral analgesia and reassurance","Sleep restriction and stimulants"],"c":0,"t":"Fat Embolism","fb":"Fat embolism syndrome (the triad: respiratory distress, neurologic changes, petechiae) is an emergency requiring oxygenation and supportive care.","d":"m"},{"q":"A child has dry, intensely itchy patches in the antecubital and popliteal folds. Which teaching point will the nurse emphasize to the caregiver about eczema?","a":["Moisturize the skin generously after short, lukewarm baths","Take long, hot baths twice a day","Use heavily scented soaps to keep the skin fresh","Allow the skin to air-dry without moisturizer"],"c":0,"t":"Eczema","fb":"Eczema management centers on hydration and lipid replacement — short lukewarm baths followed by liberal moisturizer; harsh soaps and prolonged hot water worsen it.","d":"m"},{"q":"A nurse is teaching about measles. Which preventive measure is most effective?","a":["MMR vaccination per the immunization schedule","Vitamin C supplementation","Avoiding cold beverages","Daily handwashing alone"],"c":0,"t":"Measles","fb":"Measles is highly contagious; the MMR vaccine is the cornerstone of prevention with proven population immunity above ~95%.","d":"e"},{"q":"A diver hits the bottom of a shallow pool head-first. Which mechanism of spinal cord injury is most consistent with this scenario?","a":["Axial compression","Hyperextension","Flexion","Rotation"],"c":0,"t":"Spinal Cord Injury","fb":"Striking the top of the head drives the skull straight down onto the cervical spine — axial compression, the classic shallow-water-diving SCI mechanism.","d":"m"},{"q":"A nurse is caring for an infant with hydrocephalus who had a ventriculoperitoneal shunt placed. Which finding requires immediate notification of the provider?","a":["Bulging fontanel and high-pitched cry","Brisk capillary refill","Mild abdominal distention that resolves with burping","Sleeping 16 hours in a 24-hour period"],"c":0,"t":"Hydrocephalus","fb":"Bulging fontanel and high-pitched cry suggest shunt malfunction with rising ICP — a neurosurgical emergency in infants.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with schizophrenia is hospitalized. The nurse documents flat affect, social withdrawal, and lack of motivation. These manifestations are best categorized as:","a":["Negative symptoms","Positive symptoms","Cognitive symptoms only","Mood symptoms"],"c":0,"t":"Schizophrenia","fb":"Positive symptoms ADD to normal experience (hallucinations, delusions). Negative symptoms SUBTRACT (flat affect, avolition, anhedonia, alogia).","d":"m"}]},"4":{"title":"CODE & CASCADE — Ch 7 Genetics · Ch 8 Endocrine","questions":[{"q":"A nursing student asks what the term \"allele\" actually refers to. Which response by the instructor is most accurate?","a":["A specific variant of a gene that influences how the trait is expressed","The fixed location of a gene on a chromosome","An image showing every chromosome a person carries","A segment of DNA that has no protein-coding function"],"c":0,"t":"Alleles","fb":"An allele is one of the alternative versions of a gene at a given locus. The location itself is the locus, and the picture of all the chromosomes is a karyotype.","d":"e"},{"q":"A trait controlled by a single gene typically results in how many possible phenotypes in the offspring?","a":["Two","Three","Four","It varies depending on the number of chromosomes"],"c":0,"t":"Genotype/Phenotype","fb":"Single-gene traits present as one of two phenotypes (the trait is either expressed or it is not), unlike polygenic traits which produce a wide spectrum.","d":"e"},{"q":"Adult height, skin tone, and overall cardiovascular risk are influenced by many different genes working together. The nurse recognizes this type of inheritance is best described as which of the following?","a":["Polygenic","Single gene dominant","X-linked recessive","Mitochondrial"],"c":0,"t":"Polygenic Traits","fb":"Polygenic traits result from the combined effect of many genes, producing a continuous range of phenotypes rather than a simple yes/no outcome.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client newly diagnosed with cystic fibrosis asks the nurse what \"autosomal recessive\" means. Which explanation is most appropriate?","a":["Two copies of the mutated allele are required and unaffected individuals can still be carriers","Only one copy of the mutated allele is needed and there are no carriers","The disorder is passed only from the mother to her sons","The condition skips every other generation without exception"],"c":0,"t":"Autosomal Recessive","fb":"Autosomal recessive disorders such as cystic fibrosis require two mutated alleles for expression. Heterozygous individuals are unaffected carriers.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which of the following inheritance patterns has no carrier state and requires only one mutated allele for the disorder to be expressed?","a":["Autosomal dominant","Autosomal recessive","X-linked recessive","Mitochondrial inheritance"],"c":0,"t":"Autosomal Dominant","fb":"Autosomal dominant disorders manifest with a single dominant allele. There is no carrier state because expressing one allele is enough to produce the phenotype.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which of the following are considered autosomal dominant disorders? Select all that apply.","a":["Huntington's disease","Marfan syndrome","Cystic fibrosis","Sickle cell disease","Tay-Sachs disease"],"c":0,"t":"Autosomal Dominant","fb":"Huntington's disease and Marfan syndrome are autosomal dominant. Cystic fibrosis, sickle cell, and Tay-Sachs are autosomal recessive.","d":"m","m":[0,1]},{"q":"Which of the following disorders are inherited in an autosomal recessive pattern? Select all that apply.","a":["Cystic fibrosis","Sickle cell anemia","Tay-Sachs disease","Huntington's disease","Marfan syndrome"],"c":0,"t":"Autosomal Recessive","fb":"Cystic fibrosis, sickle cell anemia, and Tay-Sachs are classic autosomal recessive disorders. Huntington's and Marfan syndrome are autosomal dominant.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"Brown eye color is a dominant trait and blue is recessive. If a client is homozygous recessive at this locus, the nurse would expect the client's eye color (phenotype) to be:","a":["Blue","Brown","Green","It cannot be determined without parental data"],"c":0,"t":"Genotype/Phenotype","fb":"Homozygous recessive (bb) means both alleles are the recessive form, so the recessive phenotype (blue eyes) is expressed.","d":"e"},{"q":"A client is a known carrier of cystic fibrosis. Using F for the unaffected dominant allele and f for the recessive allele, which notation correctly represents the client's genotype?","a":["Ff","FF","ff","fY"],"c":0,"t":"Genotype/Phenotype","fb":"Carriers of an autosomal recessive disorder are heterozygous: one dominant and one recessive allele (Ff).","d":"m"},{"q":"A nurse is reviewing X-linked recessive disorders with a colleague. Which conditions should be included? Select all that apply.","a":["Hemophilia A","Red-green color blindness","Duchenne muscular dystrophy","Huntington's disease","Marfan syndrome"],"c":0,"t":"X-Linked","fb":"Hemophilia A, red-green color blindness, and Duchenne muscular dystrophy follow X-linked recessive inheritance. Huntington's and Marfan are autosomal dominant.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"Which statement about X-linked recessive disorders is accurate?","a":["Individuals with two X chromosomes can be unaffected carriers","Both parents must be affected for the child to express the disorder","The disorder is transmitted only along the Y chromosome","Carriers always show full symptoms of the disease"],"c":0,"t":"X-Linked","fb":"In X-linked recessive inheritance, individuals with XX can carry one mutated allele without expressing the disease, while individuals with XY express it with a single mutated X.","d":"m"},{"q":"Both reproductive donors are heterozygous (Aa) for an autosomal dominant disorder. Using a Punnett square, what is the probability that any given offspring will express the disorder?","a":["75%","25%","50%","100%"],"c":0,"t":"Punnett Squares","fb":"Aa x Aa produces 1 AA : 2 Aa : 1 aa. Three of four offspring carry at least one dominant allele, so the chance of expressing the dominant disorder is 75%.","d":"m"},{"q":"A sperm donor is homozygous dominant (AA) and an egg donor is heterozygous (Aa) for an autosomal recessive disorder. What percentage of offspring would be expected to express the recessive disease?","a":["0%","25%","50%","100%"],"c":0,"t":"Punnett Squares","fb":"AA x Aa produces only AA and Aa offspring. None will be homozygous recessive, so none will express the recessive disease.","d":"m"},{"q":"An egg donor is a carrier (X^A X^a) for an X-linked recessive condition and the sperm donor is affected (X^a Y). What is the probability that any offspring will be affected by the condition?","a":["50%","25%","75%","100%"],"c":0,"t":"Punnett Squares","fb":"The cross produces X^A X^a (carrier), X^a X^a (affected), X^A Y (unaffected), and X^a Y (affected). Half of the offspring are affected.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client receives genetic results showing a complete image of all 46 chromosomes arranged by number. Which test does the nurse recognize this as?","a":["Karyotype","Pharmacogenetic panel","Gram stain","Anion gap"],"c":0,"t":"Karyotype","fb":"A karyotype is a visual display of an individual's complete set of chromosomes, used to identify numerical or structural chromosomal abnormalities.","d":"e"},{"q":"Which statement best describes a nondisjunction error during meiosis?","a":["Chromosomes fail to separate properly, producing gametes with extra or missing genetic material","A gene rearranges itself on the same chromosome without losing material","Only the X chromosome is duplicated, never the autosomes","DNA is copied twice rather than once before cell division"],"c":0,"t":"Nondisjunction","fb":"Nondisjunction is the failure of chromosomes to separate during meiosis, resulting in gametes with one too many or one too few chromosomes.","d":"m"},{"q":"A teenager with Klinefelter syndrome asks the nurse how he developed the condition. Which response by the nurse is most accurate?","a":["A nondisjunction error produced an extra sex chromosome, giving you a 47,XXY karyotype","You are missing one of your X chromosomes (45,XO)","You inherited a dominant mutation from one parent","There is an autoimmune attack against your testicular tissue"],"c":0,"t":"Klinefelter Syndrome","fb":"Klinefelter syndrome results from a nondisjunction error producing an XXY karyotype. The individual is phenotypically male with an extra X chromosome.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client recently diagnosed with Turner syndrome asks the nurse to explain the chromosomal basis. Which response is correct?","a":["You have only one X chromosome instead of the typical two (45,XO)","You have three sex chromosomes (47,XXY)","You have three copies of chromosome 21","You inherited a dominant mutation from each parent"],"c":0,"t":"Turner Syndrome","fb":"Turner syndrome is a monosomy involving a single X chromosome (45,XO) and occurs in phenotypic females.","d":"m"},{"q":"A pregnant client at her first prenatal visit asks the nurse what \"teratogen\" means. Which response is most accurate?","a":["Any agent capable of causing malformations in a developing fetus","A genetic test ordered during the first trimester","A type of antibody passed across the placenta","A normal hormone that supports embryonic development"],"c":0,"t":"Teratogens","fb":"Teratogens are substances, infections, or other agents (alcohol, certain medications, TORCH infections) that can cause fetal malformations.","d":"e"},{"q":"The nurse is teaching about teratogens during pregnancy. Which of the following should be identified as known teratogenic exposures? Select all that apply.","a":["Alcohol consumption","Rubella infection","Cytomegalovirus infection","Standard prenatal vitamins","Adequate hydration"],"c":0,"t":"Teratogens","fb":"Alcohol and TORCH infections such as rubella and CMV are well-known teratogens. Prenatal vitamins and hydration are not teratogenic.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"Which best describes the role of negative feedback in the endocrine system?","a":["It turns off the original stimulus once homeostasis is reached","It amplifies the stimulus until an external event ends it","It bypasses the hypothalamus completely","It only operates during fight-or-flight responses"],"c":0,"t":"Feedback Loops","fb":"Negative feedback shuts down hormone release once the desired physiologic state is restored, allowing the system to self-regulate.","d":"m"},{"q":"Labor and delivery involves uterine contractions that intensify until the baby is delivered. Which feedback mechanism best describes this process?","a":["Positive feedback loop","Negative feedback loop","Tertiary feedback loop","Autonomic dysreflexia"],"c":0,"t":"Feedback Loops","fb":"Positive feedback enhances the original stimulus. Oxytocin release during labor intensifies contractions until birth ends the stimulus.","d":"m"},{"q":"Lab results show a client with hypothyroidism has low T3 and T4 levels. What TSH value would the nurse expect to find?","a":["TSH will be elevated","TSH will be low","TSH will be undetectable","TSH will be unchanged"],"c":0,"t":"Hypothyroidism","fb":"In primary hypothyroidism, low T3/T4 removes negative feedback on the anterior pituitary, so TSH rises as the body tries to stimulate the thyroid.","d":"m"},{"q":"The nurse reviews labs for a client with Hashimoto's thyroiditis showing low T3 and T4, elevated TSH, and elevated TRH. Which level of endocrine dysfunction does this represent?","a":["Primary","Secondary","Tertiary","Quaternary"],"c":0,"t":"Endocrine Dysfunction Levels","fb":"Primary dysfunction means the target gland (thyroid) is the problem. The pituitary and hypothalamus respond appropriately by raising TSH and TRH.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client has hyperthyroidism with elevated T4, elevated TSH, and low TRH. Which level of endocrine dysfunction does this finding represent?","a":["Secondary","Primary","Tertiary","Compensated"],"c":0,"t":"Endocrine Dysfunction Levels","fb":"Secondary endocrine dysfunction originates at the pituitary. Excess TSH from the pituitary drives high T4 while the hypothalamus appropriately suppresses TRH.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client arrives at the clinic reporting heat intolerance, recent weight loss, and a resting heart rate of 118. Which condition does the nurse suspect?","a":["Hyperthyroidism","Hypothyroidism","Addison's disease","SIADH"],"c":0,"t":"Hyperthyroidism","fb":"Heat intolerance, weight loss, and tachycardia reflect the accelerated metabolism of hyperthyroidism.","d":"m"},{"q":"What is the most common cause of hypothyroidism in the United States?","a":["Autoimmune destruction of the thyroid gland (Hashimoto's thyroiditis)","Iodine excess in the diet","Pituitary tumor secreting TSH","Adrenal insufficiency"],"c":0,"t":"Hypothyroidism","fb":"Hashimoto's thyroiditis, an autoimmune attack on the thyroid, is the leading cause of hypothyroidism in iodine-sufficient populations like the U.S.","d":"m"},{"q":"A nurse is teaching about goiter formation. Which conditions may present with a goiter? Select all that apply.","a":["Hypothyroidism","Graves' disease","Hyperthyroidism","Iodine deficiency","Type 1 diabetes mellitus"],"c":0,"t":"Goiter","fb":"A goiter is enlargement of the thyroid gland and may occur with hypothyroidism, hyperthyroidism (including Graves'), or iodine deficiency. Type 1 DM is unrelated.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,3]},{"q":"Following a thyroidectomy, the parathyroid glands are inadvertently damaged. Which electrolyte imbalance is the nurse most concerned about?","a":["Hypocalcemia","Hyperkalemia","Hyponatremia","Hyperphosphatemia leading to alkalosis"],"c":0,"t":"Hypoparathyroidism","fb":"PTH regulates calcium. Hypoparathyroidism causes hypocalcemia, which can present with Chvostek's and Trousseau's signs and tetany.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client is diagnosed with pheochromocytoma. Which pathophysiologic process underlies this condition?","a":["Excessive release of catecholamines triggering sympathetic stimulation","Autoimmune destruction of the adrenal cortex","Hypersecretion of growth hormone from the anterior pituitary","Decreased ADH production by the posterior pituitary"],"c":0,"t":"Pheochromocytoma","fb":"Pheochromocytoma is usually a benign tumor of the adrenal medulla that secretes excess epinephrine, norepinephrine, and dopamine, producing paroxysmal hypertension, headaches, and palpitations.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client is newly diagnosed with Cushing's disease. Which underlying pathophysiology is most likely responsible?","a":["A pituitary adenoma releasing excessive ACTH that drives cortisol production","Autoimmune destruction of the adrenal cortex","A deficiency of growth hormone in adulthood","Excessive ADH from the posterior pituitary"],"c":0,"t":"Cushing's Syndrome","fb":"Cushing's disease specifically refers to ACTH-secreting pituitary adenomas. Excess ACTH stimulates the adrenal cortex to overproduce cortisol.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client newly diagnosed with Cushing's disease asks what signs and symptoms to expect. Which findings should the nurse describe? Select all that apply.","a":["Weight gain centrally","Hypertension","Moon face and buffalo hump","Bronze skin discoloration","Profound weight loss"],"c":0,"t":"Cushing's Syndrome","fb":"Cushing's classically presents with central obesity, hypertension, moon face, and buffalo hump. Bronze skin and weight loss are seen in Addison's disease.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"A pituitary adenoma is most often associated with excessive secretion of anterior pituitary hormones. Hypersecretion of which hormone leads to acromegaly?","a":["Growth hormone","Antidiuretic hormone","Cortisol","Aldosterone"],"c":0,"t":"Growth Hormone Disorders","fb":"Acromegaly results from excess growth hormone in adulthood, most commonly from a pituitary adenoma.","d":"m"},{"q":"A 6-year-old is found to have excessive levels of growth hormone before fusion of the epiphyseal growth plates. Which condition is most likely?","a":["Gigantism","Acromegaly","Dwarfism","Addison's disease"],"c":0,"t":"Gigantism","fb":"Excessive growth hormone BEFORE epiphyseal closure causes gigantism. After closure, it produces acromegaly instead.","d":"e"},{"q":"An adult client with a pituitary adenoma presents with enlargement of the jaw, hands, feet, and hypertension. Which condition is most consistent with these findings?","a":["Acromegaly","Gigantism","Dwarfism","Cushing's syndrome"],"c":0,"t":"Acromegaly","fb":"Acromegaly produces enlargement of acral structures (jaw, hands, feet) and is associated with hypertension and cardiomegaly because the growth plates are fused in adulthood.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which statement best describes the typical features of growth hormone deficiency in childhood (dwarfism)?","a":["The client has short stature with proportionally sized body parts","The client has short stature with disproportionately large extremities","The client experiences rapid weight gain with central obesity","The client develops bronze skin and hyperpigmented gums"],"c":0,"t":"Dwarfism","fb":"Growth hormone deficiency dwarfism produces short stature with proportional body parts. Disproportionate features suggest other forms such as achondroplasia.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client is diagnosed with SIADH. Which electrolyte imbalance is the nurse most concerned about?","a":["Hyponatremia","Hypernatremia","Hypercalcemia","Hypokalemia"],"c":0,"t":"SIADH","fb":"SIADH causes excessive water reabsorption, diluting serum sodium and producing hyponatremia (with risk of seizures and cerebral edema).","d":"m"},{"q":"A client newly diagnosed with diabetes insipidus would most likely present with which combination of findings?","a":["Hypernatremia and large volumes of dilute urine","Hyponatremia and concentrated urine","Hyperglycemia and ketonuria","Hyperkalemia and metabolic acidosis"],"c":0,"t":"Diabetes Insipidus","fb":"Without ADH, water is lost in the urine. Plasma sodium rises (hypernatremia) while urine becomes copious and dilute.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with Addison's disease develops patches of bronze-colored skin. What is the underlying reason for this discoloration?","a":["Elevated ACTH levels stimulate melanocytes as the pituitary tries to compensate for low cortisol","Excess cortisol deposits pigment in the skin","Iron overload from the adrenal cortex damages keratinocytes","Increased sympathetic activity dilates skin vessels"],"c":0,"t":"Addison's Disease","fb":"In primary adrenal insufficiency, low cortisol leads to a compensatory rise in ACTH. ACTH shares a precursor with melanocyte-stimulating hormone, producing hyperpigmentation.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client has been taking high-dose corticosteroids for three weeks and abruptly stops the medication while on vacation. The nurse is most concerned that the client will develop which life-threatening complication?","a":["Addisonian crisis","DKA","Thyroid storm","SIADH"],"c":0,"t":"Addison's Disease","fb":"Prolonged steroid use suppresses the HPA axis. Abrupt discontinuation can precipitate an Addisonian crisis with severe hypotension and hypoglycemia.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which statement best describes the pathophysiology of Addison's disease?","a":["Autoimmune destruction of the adrenal cortex producing low cortisol levels","A pituitary adenoma producing excessive ACTH","Resistance of peripheral cells to insulin","Excessive release of antidiuretic hormone"],"c":0,"t":"Addison's Disease","fb":"Addison's disease is primary adrenal insufficiency, typically caused by autoimmune destruction of the adrenal cortex, resulting in low cortisol and often low aldosterone.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which statement best describes the pathophysiology of type 1 diabetes mellitus?","a":["Autoimmune destruction of pancreatic beta cells with absolute insulin deficiency","Peripheral cellular resistance to insulin with adequate insulin production","Excess insulin secretion from a pancreatic tumor","Suppression of glucagon release leading to hypoglycemia"],"c":0,"t":"Diabetes Mellitus","fb":"Type 1 DM results from autoimmune destruction of the insulin-producing beta cells of the pancreas, producing complete insulin deficiency.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which statement best describes the pathophysiology of type 2 diabetes mellitus?","a":["Peripheral cells become resistant to insulin even though insulin is still produced","Autoimmune destruction of beta cells eliminates insulin","Excessive ACTH leads to elevated blood glucose","Lack of ADH produces hyperglycemia"],"c":0,"t":"Diabetes Mellitus","fb":"In type 2 DM, the pancreas continues to make insulin, but peripheral cells (especially muscle and adipose) fail to respond appropriately, producing hyperglycemia.","d":"m"},{"q":"A nurse is reviewing risk factors for type 2 diabetes mellitus. Which of the following increase a client's risk? Select all that apply.","a":["Sedentary lifestyle","History of gestational diabetes","Advancing age","Autoimmune destruction of pancreatic beta cells","Routine moderate exercise"],"c":0,"t":"Diabetes Mellitus","fb":"Sedentary lifestyle, prior gestational diabetes, and advancing age are major risk factors. Autoimmune beta-cell destruction is the mechanism of type 1, not a risk factor for type 2.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"A client presents with a blood glucose of 320 mg/dL and an arterial blood gas showing metabolic acidosis. The nurse suspects diabetic ketoacidosis. Which type of diabetes is most consistent with this presentation?","a":["Type 1 diabetes mellitus","Type 2 diabetes mellitus","Gestational diabetes","Diabetes insipidus"],"c":0,"t":"DKA","fb":"DKA most commonly occurs in type 1 diabetes because the absolute lack of insulin forces the body to burn fat, generating ketones and metabolic acidosis.","d":"m"},{"q":"The nurse is teaching a nursing student about hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state (HHS). Which statements about HHS are accurate? Select all that apply.","a":["Blood glucose is typically greater than 600 mg/dL","It occurs more often in type 2 diabetes","It causes osmotic diuresis and polyuria leading to profound dehydration","Significant ketosis and acidosis are hallmark features","Insulin levels are completely absent"],"c":0,"t":"HHS","fb":"HHS is characterized by extreme hyperglycemia (>600), severe dehydration from osmotic diuresis, and minimal ketosis because some residual insulin is present (unlike DKA).","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"Diabetes insipidus and diabetes mellitus have very different pathophysiology but share several clinical features. Which manifestations would the nurse expect in both conditions?","a":["Polyuria and polydipsia","Ketosis and metabolic acidosis","Hypoglycemia and confusion","Bronze skin and weight gain"],"c":0,"t":"Diabetes Insipidus","fb":"Both diabetes mellitus (osmotic diuresis from hyperglycemia) and diabetes insipidus (lack of ADH) produce large urine volumes and increased thirst.","d":"m"},{"q":"Beyond moving glucose into cells, insulin has several other functions. Which additional action of insulin is accurate?","a":["It moves potassium into cells and inhibits fat breakdown","It releases glucose from hepatic glycogen stores","It stimulates lipolysis to provide ketones for fuel","It increases ACTH release from the pituitary"],"c":0,"t":"Insulin","fb":"Insulin shifts potassium intracellularly and suppresses lipolysis. Glucagon, not insulin, mobilizes stored glucose and lipids.","d":"m"},{"q":"A nurse is counseling a pregnant client about complications most likely to occur with gestational diabetes. Which complications should be emphasized?","a":["Preterm labor and macrosomia (increased birth weight)","Severe maternal weight loss and ketosis","Permanent maternal type 1 diabetes after delivery","Decreased amniotic fluid leading to oligohydramnios"],"c":0,"t":"Gestational Diabetes","fb":"Gestational diabetes commonly leads to preterm labor and macrosomia (large for gestational age newborn) due to increased fetal exposure to maternal glucose.","d":"m"},{"q":"A nurse is reviewing long-term complications of uncontrolled diabetes mellitus. Which disease state is consistent with increased risk of stroke, peripheral vascular disease, and renal damage?","a":["Diabetes mellitus","Diabetes insipidus","Addison's disease","Hypoparathyroidism"],"c":0,"t":"Diabetes Mellitus","fb":"Chronic hyperglycemia damages vascular endothelium, accelerating atherosclerosis. This leads to increased risk of strokes, peripheral vascular disease, and nephropathy.","d":"m"},{"q":"The nurse is reviewing inheritance patterns with a nursing student. Which statement about an autosomal dominant condition is most accurate?","a":["An affected parent has roughly a 50% chance of passing the condition to each child","Both parents must carry the mutation for a child to be affected","Only male offspring inherit the condition","The disease consistently skips every other generation"],"c":0,"t":"Autosomal Dominant","fb":"Autosomal dominant disorders typically show up in every generation, and each child of an affected heterozygous parent has about a 50% chance of inheriting the disease allele.","d":"m"},{"q":"A couple is concerned because both partners are carriers of an autosomal recessive metabolic disorder. The nurse should explain that with each pregnancy, there is what probability of having an affected child?","a":["25%","50%","75%","100%"],"c":0,"t":"Punnett Squares","fb":"Heterozygous x heterozygous (Aa x Aa) produces 1 AA : 2 Aa : 1 aa. Only the homozygous recessive (aa) offspring express the disease, giving a 25% risk per pregnancy.","d":"e"},{"q":"A man with Huntington's disease (heterozygous, Hh) and an unaffected woman (hh) are planning a family. Using a Punnett square, the nurse calculates the probability that any single child will inherit Huntington's disease as:","a":["50%","25%","75%","0%"],"c":0,"t":"Punnett Squares","fb":"Hh x hh yields Hh, Hh, hh, hh. Two of four offspring (50%) inherit the dominant disease allele and will develop Huntington's disease.","d":"m"},{"q":"Two parents who are both heterozygous for cystic fibrosis (Ff x Ff) ask the nurse about the chance that a child will be an unaffected carrier. Which response is most accurate?","a":["About 50% of children will be unaffected carriers","About 25% of children will be unaffected carriers","All offspring will be unaffected carriers","No offspring will be unaffected carriers"],"c":0,"t":"Punnett Squares","fb":"Ff x Ff produces 1 FF : 2 Ff : 1 ff. The two heterozygous (Ff) offspring represent 50% of the cross and are unaffected carriers.","d":"m"},{"q":"A woman who is a known carrier of hemophilia A (X^H X^h) has a child with an unaffected partner (X^H Y). What is the probability that a son will be affected?","a":["50% of sons will be affected","25% of sons will be affected","All sons will be affected","No sons will be affected"],"c":0,"t":"Punnett Squares","fb":"The cross yields X^H X^H, X^H X^h, X^H Y, and X^h Y. Half the sons (X^h Y) are affected, while the other half are unaffected.","d":"m"},{"q":"A pediatric nurse counsels the family of a boy newly diagnosed with Duchenne muscular dystrophy. Which statement reflects accurate teaching about this inheritance pattern?","a":["The mother is likely a carrier because the gene is located on the X chromosome","The father almost certainly passed the disease allele to his son","Both parents must be affected for a son to develop the disease","The disease will be passed to all of the boy's future children"],"c":0,"t":"X-Linked","fb":"Duchenne is X-linked recessive. Affected males receive the mutated X from their mother (typically a carrier). Fathers do not pass an X chromosome to their sons.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which finding by the nurse most strongly suggests that a condition is X-linked recessive rather than autosomal recessive?","a":["Most of the affected family members are males","Both parents must be affected to produce affected offspring","The condition appears in every generation","Both sexes are affected with equal frequency"],"c":0,"t":"X-Linked","fb":"X-linked recessive disorders disproportionately affect males because they only need one mutated X allele. Females require two and are usually carriers.","d":"m"},{"q":"The nurse explains to a couple that traits such as adult height, skin color, and the risk of type 2 diabetes do not follow a simple dominant or recessive pattern. This is most accurately described as which type of inheritance?","a":["Polygenic inheritance involving many genes and environmental factors","Autosomal recessive inheritance with carriers","Sex-linked inheritance from the X chromosome","Single-gene inheritance with two possible phenotypes"],"c":0,"t":"Polygenic Traits","fb":"Polygenic traits arise from the combined effect of many genes (often with environmental influence) and produce a continuous spectrum of phenotypes.","d":"m"},{"q":"A nurse is reviewing the difference between genotype and phenotype with a student. Which scenario best illustrates the phenotype?","a":["A client with curly brown hair and blue eyes","A client who is heterozygous (Aa) at a particular gene","A client's pair of chromosomes 7 displayed on a karyotype","A client's allele combination for the ABO blood group"],"c":0,"t":"Genotype/Phenotype","fb":"Phenotype is the observable expression of the genes (hair, eye color, height). Genotype is the underlying allele combination.","d":"e"},{"q":"Which genotype represents a homozygous dominant individual at a single gene locus?","a":["BB","Bb","bb","bY"],"c":0,"t":"Genotype/Phenotype","fb":"Two identical capital (dominant) alleles, such as BB, denote a homozygous dominant genotype. Bb is heterozygous and bb is homozygous recessive.","d":"e"},{"q":"A genetic counselor reviews a karyotype with the nurse showing 47 chromosomes including three copies of chromosome 21. Which condition does this karyotype represent?","a":["Down syndrome (Trisomy 21)","Turner syndrome","Klinefelter syndrome","Cystic fibrosis"],"c":0,"t":"Karyotype","fb":"An extra chromosome 21 (trisomy 21) on karyotype is diagnostic of Down syndrome, the most common autosomal trisomy in live births.","d":"m"},{"q":"A young woman with Turner syndrome asks the nurse what features she should expect. Which findings are commonly associated with this condition? Select all that apply.","a":["Short stature","Webbed neck","Ovarian dysgenesis with infertility","Tall stature with gynecomastia","Trisomy 21 facial features"],"c":0,"t":"Turner Syndrome","fb":"Turner syndrome (45,XO) classically presents with short stature, a webbed neck, and ovarian failure. Tall stature with gynecomastia suggests Klinefelter, and trisomy 21 features are Down syndrome.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"Which statement by a teenage client with Klinefelter syndrome would indicate correct understanding of the condition?","a":["\"My body has an extra X chromosome, so my karyotype is 47,XXY.\"","\"I am missing one of my X chromosomes.\"","\"I have an extra copy of chromosome 21.\"","\"I inherited a dominant mutation from my father.\""],"c":0,"t":"Klinefelter Syndrome","fb":"Klinefelter syndrome is the result of nondisjunction during meiosis producing a 47,XXY karyotype in a phenotypic male.","d":"m"},{"q":"The nurse is teaching about teratogen exposure during pregnancy. Which of the following are recognized teratogens? Select all that apply.","a":["Isotretinoin (acne medication)","Warfarin","ACE inhibitors","Folic acid supplements","Prenatal yoga"],"c":0,"t":"Teratogens","fb":"Isotretinoin, warfarin, and ACE inhibitors are well-known human teratogens. Folic acid supplements reduce neural tube defects, and exercise during pregnancy is generally safe.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"A pregnant client at 8 weeks gestation asks why she should avoid alcohol. The nurse's best response is:","a":["\"Alcohol is a teratogen that can cause fetal alcohol syndrome and lifelong cognitive deficits.\"","\"Alcohol primarily affects the mother's liver during pregnancy.\"","\"Alcohol only causes harm if consumed during the third trimester.\"","\"A small amount of alcohol can help relax the uterus.\""],"c":0,"t":"Teratogens","fb":"Prenatal alcohol exposure causes fetal alcohol spectrum disorders, including growth restriction, characteristic facial features, and neurodevelopmental impairment. No safe amount has been established.","d":"m"},{"q":"A nondisjunction event during meiosis results in which of the following outcomes?","a":["A gamete that contains either an extra chromosome or one fewer chromosome than normal","A chromosome that has rearranged its genes but contains all the original material","A gamete that contains only one set of chromosomes as expected","A duplicated DNA strand prior to mitosis"],"c":0,"t":"Nondisjunction","fb":"Nondisjunction occurs when sister chromatids or homologous chromosomes fail to separate, leaving daughter cells with an extra or missing chromosome.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which scenario best demonstrates a positive feedback loop in human physiology?","a":["Oxytocin release intensifying uterine contractions during labor","Insulin secretion lowering blood glucose after a meal","Thyroid hormone shutting off TSH release","Cortisol suppressing further ACTH release"],"c":0,"t":"Feedback Loops","fb":"Positive feedback amplifies an effect until an end event resolves it. Labor contractions and oxytocin form the textbook example.","d":"m"},{"q":"After eating a high-carbohydrate meal, the pancreas secretes insulin, which lowers blood glucose and turns off further insulin release. The nurse identifies this regulatory process as:","a":["A negative feedback loop","A positive feedback loop","A tertiary feedback loop","An autonomic reflex arc"],"c":0,"t":"Feedback Loops","fb":"Negative feedback turns off the stimulus once homeostasis is achieved. Blood-glucose regulation by insulin is a classic example.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client has labs revealing low T4, low TSH, and low TRH. The nurse recognizes this pattern as which level of endocrine dysfunction?","a":["Tertiary","Secondary","Primary","Compensated"],"c":0,"t":"Endocrine Dysfunction Levels","fb":"When all three hormones are low, the problem originates at the hypothalamus (tertiary dysfunction) - the pituitary and thyroid are responding appropriately to absent stimulation.","d":"m"},{"q":"Lab results show a client has low T4 with elevated TSH. The nurse interprets this as:","a":["Primary hypothyroidism","Secondary hypothyroidism","Subclinical hyperthyroidism","Tertiary hyperthyroidism"],"c":0,"t":"Endocrine Dysfunction Levels","fb":"Low T4 with elevated TSH means the thyroid is failing to respond, and the pituitary is appropriately compensating - classic primary hypothyroidism.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which clinical findings are most consistent with hypothyroidism? Select all that apply.","a":["Fatigue and cold intolerance","Weight gain despite stable intake","Bradycardia","Heat intolerance and tachycardia","Diarrhea and tremor"],"c":0,"t":"Hypothyroidism","fb":"Hypothyroidism slows metabolism, causing fatigue, cold intolerance, weight gain, and bradycardia. Heat intolerance, tachycardia, diarrhea, and tremor reflect hyperthyroidism.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"Which clinical findings would the nurse expect in a client with hyperthyroidism? Select all that apply.","a":["Tachycardia at rest","Unintentional weight loss","Heat intolerance and diaphoresis","Constipation and bradycardia","Coarse hair and edema"],"c":0,"t":"Hyperthyroidism","fb":"Hyperthyroidism accelerates metabolism, producing tachycardia, weight loss, heat intolerance, and diaphoresis. Constipation, bradycardia, coarse hair, and edema are hypothyroid features.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"A client with longstanding iodine deficiency develops a visible enlargement at the base of the neck. Which underlying mechanism best explains the formation of this goiter?","a":["Persistent TSH stimulation in the absence of adequate iodine causes thyroid hyperplasia","Excess thyroid hormone is being stored as a visible mass","Calcium is being deposited around the thyroid gland","Lymphatic tissue surrounding the thyroid becomes inflamed"],"c":0,"t":"Goiter","fb":"Without iodine, the thyroid cannot make T3/T4. Falling levels remove negative feedback, TSH rises, and the chronically stimulated gland grows into a goiter.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client returns from a total thyroidectomy and reports tingling around the mouth and muscle cramps. Which finding by the nurse is the highest priority?","a":["A positive Chvostek's sign suggesting hypocalcemia","Mild incisional pain rated 3/10","Hoarseness from intubation","Oral temperature of 99.1 degrees F"],"c":0,"t":"Hypoparathyroidism","fb":"Damage to the parathyroid glands during thyroidectomy causes hypocalcemia. Perioral tingling, cramping, and Chvostek's sign signal a need for urgent calcium replacement.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which manifestation should the nurse anticipate in a client with hypoparathyroidism?","a":["Muscle tetany and laryngospasm","Bone pain with elevated calcium","Polyuria from hypercalcemia","Bronze skin pigmentation"],"c":0,"t":"Hypoparathyroidism","fb":"Hypoparathyroidism produces hypocalcemia. Manifestations include neuromuscular irritability such as tetany, perioral tingling, and laryngospasm.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client presents to the ED with paroxysmal headaches, palpitations, profound diaphoresis, and a blood pressure of 220/130. Which endocrine disorder should the nurse suspect first?","a":["Pheochromocytoma","Cushing's disease","Addison's disease","Hypothyroidism"],"c":0,"t":"Pheochromocytoma","fb":"The triad of episodic headache, palpitations, and diaphoresis with severe hypertension is classic for pheochromocytoma - an adrenal medullary tumor secreting catecholamines.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with Cushing's syndrome asks why she bruises so easily and develops purple stretch marks. Which explanation by the nurse is most accurate?","a":["Excess cortisol thins the skin and weakens connective tissue","Chronic hypoglycemia impairs platelet function","ADH excess produces fluid overload that stretches the skin","Iodine deficiency impairs collagen synthesis"],"c":0,"t":"Cushing's Syndrome","fb":"High cortisol degrades collagen and elastin, leading to thin, fragile skin, easy bruising, and characteristic purple striae over the abdomen, thighs, and breasts.","d":"m"},{"q":"A nurse is monitoring a client with Cushing's syndrome. Which laboratory findings would the nurse expect? Select all that apply.","a":["Hyperglycemia","Hypernatremia","Hypokalemia","Hypocalcemia and hyponatremia","Profound hypoglycemia"],"c":0,"t":"Cushing's Syndrome","fb":"Cortisol promotes gluconeogenesis (hyperglycemia), sodium retention (hypernatremia), and potassium wasting (hypokalemia). Hypoglycemia and hyponatremia are hallmarks of Addison's, the opposite condition.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"An adult client with a pituitary adenoma reports that his wedding ring no longer fits, his shoe size has increased, and he is developing prominent brow ridges. Which condition does the nurse suspect?","a":["Acromegaly","Gigantism","Dwarfism","Hypothyroidism"],"c":0,"t":"Acromegaly","fb":"After epiphyseal closure, excess growth hormone causes acral enlargement (hands, feet, jaw, brow) - the defining picture of acromegaly.","d":"m"},{"q":"A 9-year-old client is referred to endocrinology because of accelerated linear growth, surpassing the 99th percentile in height. The nurse anticipates that further workup will most likely reveal which condition?","a":["Pituitary gigantism due to excess growth hormone before epiphyseal closure","Acromegaly due to excess growth hormone after epiphyseal closure","Cushing's disease due to excessive cortisol","Growth hormone deficiency"],"c":0,"t":"Gigantism","fb":"Excess growth hormone before the growth plates close results in pituitary gigantism - dramatically increased linear height with proportional features.","d":"m"},{"q":"Parents of a 4-year-old who is well below the growth curve ask the nurse what dwarfism means. Which response best describes growth-hormone deficiency dwarfism?","a":["The child is short, but the body proportions are normal","The child has a short trunk with disproportionately long limbs","The child has shortened limbs but a normal-sized trunk and head","The child develops bone deformities with normal stature"],"c":0,"t":"Dwarfism","fb":"GH deficiency in childhood produces proportionate short stature - body parts are uniformly small. Disproportionate short stature suggests other skeletal disorders such as achondroplasia.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client diagnosed with SIADH is being monitored for complications. Which clinical findings should the nurse anticipate? Select all that apply.","a":["Concentrated, low-volume urine","Serum sodium below 135 mEq/L","Weight gain without obvious edema","Hypernatremia and dehydration","Copious dilute urine output"],"c":0,"t":"SIADH","fb":"SIADH retains free water, producing concentrated urine, dilutional hyponatremia, and water weight gain. Hypernatremia with copious dilute urine is the opposite picture (diabetes insipidus).","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"Which intervention is most appropriate for a client with mild SIADH and a sodium of 128 mEq/L?","a":["Strict fluid restriction","Encouraging at least 3 liters of free water daily","Administering a hypotonic IV fluid bolus","Withholding all sodium-containing foods"],"c":0,"t":"SIADH","fb":"SIADH is treated by limiting water intake to correct dilutional hyponatremia. Encouraging water or giving hypotonic fluids worsens the imbalance.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with diabetes insipidus is most likely to demonstrate which combination of laboratory and clinical findings?","a":["Serum sodium of 152 mEq/L with urine specific gravity of 1.001","Serum sodium of 124 mEq/L with urine specific gravity of 1.030","Blood glucose of 480 mg/dL with ketones in the urine","Serum potassium of 6.2 mEq/L with metabolic acidosis"],"c":0,"t":"Diabetes Insipidus","fb":"Without ADH, the client loses copious dilute urine (low specific gravity), and serum sodium climbs as free water is lost - the classic DI lab pattern.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which assessment finding in a client with diabetes insipidus would prompt the nurse to intervene first?","a":["Urine output of 8 liters in the past 24 hours with serum sodium of 158 mEq/L","Weight gain of 1 kilogram overnight","Urine specific gravity of 1.030","Reports of intermittent thirst"],"c":0,"t":"Diabetes Insipidus","fb":"Massive urine output with marked hypernatremia indicates significant free-water loss and risk of hypovolemic shock and neurologic injury - immediate intervention is needed.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client presenting with fatigue, weight loss, salt craving, hyperpigmentation of the palmar creases, and a blood pressure of 86/52 most likely has which condition?","a":["Addison's disease","Cushing's syndrome","Hyperthyroidism","SIADH"],"c":0,"t":"Addison's Disease","fb":"Primary adrenal insufficiency presents with fatigue, weight loss, hypotension, salt craving (low aldosterone), and bronze hyperpigmentation from elevated ACTH.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with Addison's disease is hospitalized with vomiting, severe hypotension, and serum glucose of 48 mg/dL. The nurse anticipates which priority intervention?","a":["IV hydrocortisone and fluid resuscitation","Subcutaneous insulin","Initiating a low-sodium diet","Administering oral levothyroxine"],"c":0,"t":"Addison's Disease","fb":"Addisonian crisis is treated with immediate IV glucocorticoids (hydrocortisone), isotonic fluids, and glucose. Delay can be fatal.","d":"m"},{"q":"A nurse provides discharge teaching to a client newly started on long-term prednisone. Which statement by the client indicates correct understanding?","a":["\"I should never stop this medication suddenly because my body may not make enough cortisol.\"","\"If I feel better, it is safe to discontinue this medication.\"","\"I will take a double dose if I miss one to catch up.\"","\"This medication should be taken at bedtime to avoid stomach upset.\""],"c":0,"t":"Addison's Disease","fb":"Chronic corticosteroid use suppresses the HPA axis. Abrupt discontinuation can precipitate Addisonian crisis. Steroids must be tapered slowly.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which statement best explains why hyperpigmentation is a hallmark sign of Addison's disease?","a":["Elevated ACTH shares a precursor with melanocyte-stimulating hormone and stimulates skin melanocytes","Cortisol directly deposits brown pigment in the skin","Iron overload from failing adrenal glands stains the skin","Excess aldosterone darkens the dermal layer"],"c":0,"t":"Addison's Disease","fb":"ACTH is cleaved from a precursor (POMC) that also yields MSH. When ACTH rises in primary adrenal failure, melanocyte stimulation produces bronze skin.","d":"m"},{"q":"A nurse is teaching a newly diagnosed type 1 diabetic about insulin therapy. Which client statement requires correction?","a":["\"My pancreas just needs a few weeks to start working again on its own.\"","\"I will need insulin for the rest of my life.\"","\"Type 1 diabetes is an autoimmune condition.\"","\"I am at risk for diabetic ketoacidosis if I skip my insulin doses.\""],"c":0,"t":"Diabetes Mellitus","fb":"Type 1 DM destroys the insulin-producing beta cells. Insulin replacement is required for life - the pancreas will not recover.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which of the following clinical signs would the nurse expect in a client experiencing diabetic ketoacidosis? Select all that apply.","a":["Kussmaul respirations","Fruity (acetone) breath odor","Serum bicarbonate of 12 mEq/L","Blood glucose of 75 mg/dL","Negative urine ketones"],"c":0,"t":"DKA","fb":"DKA produces Kussmaul respirations to blow off CO2, fruity breath from acetone, and low serum bicarbonate from metabolic acidosis. Glucose is usually >250, and urine ketones are markedly positive.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"A client arrives in the ED with a glucose of 880 mg/dL, serum osmolality of 360 mOsm/kg, minimal ketones, and altered mental status. Which condition is most consistent with these findings?","a":["Hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state (HHS)","Diabetic ketoacidosis","Severe hypoglycemia","Acute kidney injury"],"c":0,"t":"HHS","fb":"HHS features extreme hyperglycemia, marked hyperosmolality, and minimal ketosis (some residual insulin suppresses lipolysis). Mental status changes reflect cellular dehydration.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which of the following statements correctly distinguishes DKA from HHS? Select all that apply.","a":["DKA is associated with significant ketoacidosis; HHS is not","HHS is more common in type 2 diabetes","Blood glucose tends to be higher in HHS than in DKA","DKA presents with a serum pH above 7.45","HHS typically produces a fruity-smelling breath"],"c":0,"t":"DKA","fb":"DKA produces ketoacidosis (low pH) and usually occurs in type 1 DM. HHS occurs in type 2, has minimal ketones, and shows even higher glucose. Fruity breath is a DKA finding.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"A client with type 2 diabetes asks the nurse why oral medications often work even though insulin is still being produced. Which explanation is most accurate?","a":["\"In type 2 diabetes the body still makes insulin, but the cells do not respond well; many oral drugs help your tissues use insulin more effectively.\"","\"Type 2 diabetes is caused by autoimmune destruction of the pancreas, so the pills regenerate beta cells.\"","\"The pills replace the insulin your pancreas is no longer producing.\"","\"Type 2 diabetes is caused by lack of ADH, which the pills correct.\""],"c":0,"t":"Diabetes Mellitus","fb":"Type 2 DM is characterized by insulin resistance. Many oral agents (metformin, thiazolidinediones) sensitize tissues to the insulin the pancreas already produces.","d":"m"},{"q":"A nurse is reviewing risk factors for gestational diabetes. Which client is at highest risk?","a":["A 38-year-old with a BMI of 34 and a prior macrosomic infant","A 22-year-old at a healthy weight with no medical history","A 28-year-old marathon runner expecting her first child","A 20-year-old with a family history of Marfan syndrome"],"c":0,"t":"Gestational Diabetes","fb":"Advanced maternal age, obesity, and a prior macrosomic infant are major risk factors for gestational diabetes.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client diagnosed with gestational diabetes asks the nurse what risks her newborn faces. Which response is most accurate?","a":["\"Your baby is at increased risk for hypoglycemia shortly after birth.\"","\"Your baby is at high risk for being underweight.\"","\"Your baby will likely be born with type 1 diabetes.\"","\"Your baby will require lifelong insulin therapy.\""],"c":0,"t":"Gestational Diabetes","fb":"Maternal hyperglycemia drives fetal hyperinsulinemia. After delivery the glucose source is removed, but high insulin persists - newborns are at risk for hypoglycemia.","d":"m"},{"q":"Insulin is best described as having which combination of effects on cellular metabolism?","a":["Drives glucose and potassium into cells and inhibits fat breakdown","Increases hepatic glycogen breakdown and promotes lipolysis","Stimulates ketone production and proteolysis","Raises blood glucose and serum potassium simultaneously"],"c":0,"t":"Insulin","fb":"Insulin is an anabolic hormone: it shifts glucose and potassium into cells, builds glycogen, and inhibits lipolysis. The opposite actions describe glucagon and stress hormones.","d":"m"},{"q":"A nurse is observing the long-term complications of poorly controlled diabetes mellitus. Which complications should the nurse anticipate? Select all that apply.","a":["Peripheral neuropathy","Diabetic nephropathy","Retinopathy with vision loss","Increased bone density","Reduced cardiovascular risk"],"c":0,"t":"Diabetes Mellitus","fb":"Chronic hyperglycemia damages microvasculature and nerves, producing neuropathy, nephropathy, and retinopathy. Cardiovascular risk is increased, not decreased.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"A client newly diagnosed with type 1 diabetes asks why she has been so thirsty and tired. The nurse's best explanation is:","a":["\"High blood sugar causes you to lose water in the urine, which leads to dehydration and fatigue.\"","\"Your antidiuretic hormone has become inactive.\"","\"Your thyroid is producing too much hormone, causing dehydration.\"","\"Excess cortisol is making you tired and thirsty.\""],"c":0,"t":"Diabetes Mellitus","fb":"Hyperglycemia exceeds the renal threshold; glucose spills into the urine and pulls water with it (osmotic diuresis), producing polyuria, dehydration, and fatigue.","d":"m"},{"q":"A primary care nurse reviews a client's labs showing T3 and T4 within normal limits but elevated TSH. The client is currently asymptomatic. Which condition is most consistent with these findings?","a":["Subclinical hypothyroidism","Subclinical hyperthyroidism","Secondary hyperthyroidism","Pituitary infarction"],"c":0,"t":"Hypothyroidism","fb":"Subclinical hypothyroidism is defined as elevated TSH with still-normal T3/T4. The pituitary is working hard to keep the thyroid producing hormone; symptoms may be absent or subtle.","d":"m"},{"q":"The nurse is teaching a client with Graves' disease. Which finding would the nurse expect on assessment?","a":["Exophthalmos and a visibly enlarged thyroid","Periorbital edema and dry coarse skin","Cold intolerance and bradycardia","Bronze pigmentation of skin creases"],"c":0,"t":"Hyperthyroidism","fb":"Graves' disease is an autoimmune cause of hyperthyroidism. Bulging eyes (exophthalmos), goiter, tachycardia, and heat intolerance are classic findings.","d":"m"},{"q":"Two parents are heterozygous (Bb) for an autosomal recessive disorder. The Punnett square indicates approximately what percentage of offspring will be unaffected (no carrier status, no disease)?","a":["25%","50%","75%","0%"],"c":0,"t":"Punnett Squares","fb":"Bb x Bb produces 1 BB : 2 Bb : 1 bb. Only the single BB offspring is fully unaffected (no carrier status, no disease), representing 25%.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client newly diagnosed with type 2 diabetes asks the nurse what lifestyle changes will help. Which interventions should be recommended? Select all that apply.","a":["Regular aerobic exercise","Weight loss if overweight","A diet with controlled carbohydrate intake","Strict avoidance of all carbohydrates for life","Daily insulin injections as the first-line therapy"],"c":0,"t":"Diabetes Mellitus","fb":"Exercise, weight loss, and a controlled-carbohydrate diet improve insulin sensitivity. Complete carb avoidance is unrealistic, and insulin is not the first-line therapy for type 2 DM.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"The nurse is reviewing the HPA axis with a student and explains that ACTH from the anterior pituitary acts on which target gland?","a":["The adrenal cortex to stimulate cortisol release","The adrenal medulla to release catecholamines","The thyroid gland to release T3 and T4","The pancreas to release insulin"],"c":0,"t":"ACTH/Cortisol Axis","fb":"ACTH stimulates the adrenal cortex (specifically zona fasciculata) to produce cortisol. The medulla makes catecholamines under autonomic, not ACTH, control.","d":"e"},{"q":"A client with a pituitary adenoma has labs showing elevated ACTH and elevated cortisol. The nurse interprets this as which level of endocrine dysfunction?","a":["Secondary - the pituitary is the source of excess hormone driving the adrenal gland","Primary - the adrenal gland is the source of excess hormone","Tertiary - the hypothalamus is the source of dysfunction","Compensated - no true dysfunction exists"],"c":0,"t":"Endocrine Dysfunction Levels","fb":"When the pituitary inappropriately oversecretes ACTH (driving high cortisol), the disorder is secondary - the pituitary is the originating problem.","d":"m"}]},"5":{"title":"PUMP & FLOW — Ch 9 Hematology · Ch 10 Cardiovascular","questions":[{"q":"A client presents to clinic with fatigue, shortness of breath on mild exertion, and unusually pale skin. Routine labs show low hemoglobin and low hematocrit. Which condition should the nurse suspect first?","a":["Anemia","Polycythemia vera","Acute leukemia","Dehydration"],"c":0,"t":"Anemia","fb":"Fatigue, dyspnea, and pallor with low Hgb/Hct is the classic presentation of anemia — the body is short of oxygen-carrying capacity.","d":"e"},{"q":"Which findings would the nurse expect in a client with moderate anemia? Select all that apply.","a":["Pallor of the skin and conjunctiva","Dyspnea on exertion","Tachycardia","Bounding peripheral pulses with hypertension","Fatigue and weakness"],"c":0,"t":"Anemia","fb":"Anemia signs reflect low O2 delivery and compensatory cardiac effort: pallor, fatigue, dyspnea, tachycardia. Hypertension with bounding pulses is not typical.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,4]},{"q":"A vegan client with heavy menstrual periods reports months of fatigue. Labs show low hemoglobin, low MCV, and low ferritin. Which type of anemia is most consistent with this picture?","a":["Iron deficiency anemia","Pernicious anemia","Aplastic anemia","Polycythemia vera"],"c":0,"t":"Iron Deficiency Anemia","fb":"Low iron stores, low intake, plus chronic blood loss point to iron deficiency anemia — microcytic and hypochromic on smear.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which factors are common causes of iron deficiency anemia? Select all that apply.","a":["Heavy menstrual bleeding","Pregnancy and increased demand","A diet very low in iron-containing foods","Chronic GI bleeding","Living at high altitude"],"c":0,"t":"Iron Deficiency Anemia","fb":"Iron deficiency is driven by blood loss, increased demand (pregnancy, growth), or poor intake/absorption. High altitude actually drives RBC production up.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,3]},{"q":"A client with anemia reports tingling in the feet, balance problems, and a sore, beefy red tongue. Which nutrient deficiency is most likely?","a":["Vitamin B12","Iron","Folate alone","Vitamin K"],"c":0,"t":"B12 Deficiency","fb":"Neurologic symptoms (peripheral neuropathy, ataxia, glossitis) distinguish B12 deficiency from other anemias.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with pernicious anemia asks why their body cannot use the B12 they eat. The nurse's best explanation is:","a":["The stomach's parietal cells aren't producing intrinsic factor, so B12 can't be absorbed in the ileum","The liver destroys B12 before it can be used","The kidneys excrete all dietary B12","The bone marrow rejects B12-containing food"],"c":0,"t":"Pernicious Anemia","fb":"Pernicious anemia is an autoimmune loss of parietal cells/intrinsic factor — without IF, dietary B12 isn't absorbed at the terminal ileum.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which pathophysiologic statement best describes aplastic anemia?","a":["Bone marrow stops producing red cells, white cells, and platelets","Only red cell production is affected; WBCs and platelets remain normal","Red cells are produced normally but destroyed in the spleen","Red cells have abnormal hemoglobin and sickle under stress"],"c":0,"t":"Aplastic Anemia","fb":"Aplastic anemia is pancytopenia from bone marrow failure — usually triggered by drugs, infection, autoimmune attack, or radiation.","d":"m"},{"q":"Sickle cell anemia is best described by which of the following?","a":["An inherited disorder in which abnormal hemoglobin causes RBCs to sickle, occlude vessels, and cause ischemic pain","An autoimmune destruction of RBCs by the spleen","A nutritional deficiency of B12 and folate","A reactive overproduction of RBCs in response to hypoxia"],"c":0,"t":"Sickle Cell Anemia","fb":"Sickle cell is an inherited (autosomal recessive) hemoglobinopathy — under stress, RBCs sickle, clog microvasculature, and cause vaso-occlusive crises.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with sickle cell disease arrives in the ED in vaso-occlusive crisis. Which factors can trigger a crisis? Select all that apply.","a":["Dehydration","Infection","High-altitude exposure or hypoxia","Cold exposure","A diet high in iron-rich foods"],"c":0,"t":"Sickle Cell Anemia","fb":"Triggers all stress the RBC: dehydration, hypoxia, infection, cold, acidosis. Iron-rich foods don't trigger sickling.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,3]},{"q":"Thalassemia is best described as:","a":["An inherited disorder producing abnormal or insufficient hemoglobin chains that results in anemia","An acquired marrow failure caused by chemotherapy","A B12 absorption disorder","An autoimmune destruction of platelets"],"c":0,"t":"Thalassemia","fb":"Thalassemia is a genetic defect in alpha or beta hemoglobin chain production, leading to ineffective erythropoiesis and chronic anemia.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which symptoms would the nurse expect in a client with polycythemia vera?","a":["Itching after a warm shower, ruddy complexion, and increased risk of clots","Pale skin, bleeding gums, and easy bruising","Cyanotic extremities and low oxygen saturation","Low blood pressure and low hematocrit"],"c":0,"t":"Polycythemia Vera","fb":"PV produces too many RBCs — blood becomes thick: pruritus (especially post-shower), plethora, headaches, and high thrombosis risk.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which statement best explains why clients with von Willebrand disease bleed more easily?","a":["They lack von Willebrand factor, so platelets cannot adhere properly at the site of vessel injury","They have too many platelets, which clog the vessels","They cannot absorb vitamin K","They produce excessive thrombin"],"c":0,"t":"Von Willebrand","fb":"vWF helps platelets stick to damaged endothelium and stabilizes factor VIII. Without it, primary hemostasis fails — easy bruising, nosebleeds, prolonged bleeding.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which condition is characterized by widespread inappropriate clotting that consumes clotting factors, leading to simultaneous thrombosis and severe bleeding?","a":["Disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC)","Von Willebrand disease","Polycythemia vera","Pernicious anemia"],"c":0,"t":"DIC","fb":"DIC is a paradox — the body clots everywhere at once, uses up its clotting factors and platelets, and then bleeds uncontrollably.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client receiving a unit of packed red blood cells suddenly develops fever, flank pain, hypotension, and red-tinged urine within minutes. The nurse recognizes this as:","a":["An acute hemolytic transfusion reaction due to ABO incompatibility","A mild allergic reaction to plasma proteins","Transfusion-associated circulatory overload","An air embolism"],"c":0,"t":"Acute Hemolytic Reaction","fb":"Acute hemolytic reactions occur when recipient antibodies destroy donor RBCs — almost always ABO-mismatched blood. Stop the transfusion immediately.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with new-onset atrial fibrillation asks why they have been started on an anticoagulant. The nurse's best response is:","a":["Atrial fibrillation lets blood pool in the atria, where clots can form and then travel — most worrisome is stroke","Anticoagulants slow the heart rate","The medication will convert your rhythm back to normal","It prevents the heart muscle from getting weak"],"c":0,"t":"Atrial Fibrillation","fb":"A-fib's quivering atria fail to empty efficiently — blood stagnates (often in the left atrial appendage), clots form, and embolize → ischemic stroke.","d":"m"},{"q":"On the ECG of a client with atrial fibrillation, the nurse expects to see:","a":["An irregularly irregular rhythm with absent P waves","A regular rhythm with widened QRS complexes","Pronounced ST elevation in the inferior leads","A regular rhythm with peaked T waves"],"c":0,"t":"Atrial Fibrillation","fb":"A-fib: the atria fire chaotically — no organized P wave, and the ventricular response is irregularly irregular.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which complications are associated with sustained tachyarrhythmias such as uncontrolled atrial fibrillation? Select all that apply.","a":["Ischemic stroke from embolic clots","Heart failure from poor ventricular filling","Hypotension and decreased cardiac output","Worsening myocardial ischemia","Improved coronary perfusion"],"c":0,"t":"Arrhythmias","fb":"Fast or chaotic rhythms shorten diastole, drop cardiac output, increase O2 demand, and promote clotting. They do not improve coronary perfusion.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,3]},{"q":"Which factors contribute to an elevated blood pressure? Select all that apply.","a":["Increased peripheral vascular resistance","Increased cardiac output","Activation of the RAAS system","Increased sodium and water retention","Loss of all sympathetic tone"],"c":0,"t":"Hypertension","fb":"BP = CO x PVR. Anything raising CO, PVR, or fluid volume (RAAS, salt/water retention) raises BP. Loss of sympathetic tone lowers BP.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,3]},{"q":"A client with poorly controlled chronic hypertension is most at risk for which complications? Select all that apply.","a":["Coronary artery disease","Left ventricular hypertrophy","Aortic aneurysm","Chronic kidney disease and stroke","Acute pernicious anemia"],"c":0,"t":"Hypertension","fb":"Long-standing HTN damages vessels and overworks the heart — CAD, LVH, aneurysm, CKD, and stroke are classic sequelae. Pernicious anemia is unrelated.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,3]},{"q":"A client's lipid panel shows high LDL and high triglycerides. The nurse explains they are at increased risk for which disease process?","a":["Atherosclerosis","Pernicious anemia","Aplastic anemia","Pericardial effusion"],"c":0,"t":"Atherosclerosis","fb":"Elevated LDL ('bad cholesterol') and triglycerides drive lipid deposition in vessel walls — the foundation of atherosclerosis.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with known atherosclerosis reports cramping pain in the left calf when walking that resolves with rest. The nurse documents this as:","a":["Intermittent claudication","Stable angina","Venous insufficiency","Raynaud's phenomenon"],"c":0,"t":"Intermittent Claudication","fb":"Intermittent claudication is exercise-induced leg pain from arterial insufficiency — muscle O2 demand outstrips a narrowed vessel's supply. It resolves with rest.","d":"e"},{"q":"A client with worsening peripheral artery disease is most likely to exhibit which findings on assessment of the lower extremities?","a":["Cool, pale skin with diminished pedal pulses","Warm, reddened legs with bounding pulses","Brown discoloration and weeping ulcers around the ankles","Edema and a positive Homan's sign"],"c":0,"t":"Peripheral Artery Disease","fb":"PAD = poor arterial inflow → cool, pale, shiny limbs with thin hair, weak pulses, and pain with activity. Brown ankles and edema fit venous, not arterial, disease.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with chronic venous insufficiency develops a non-healing wound near the medial ankle. The nurse recognizes this as which expected complication?","a":["A venous stasis ulcer","An arterial ulcer","A pressure ulcer","A diabetic neuropathic ulcer"],"c":0,"t":"Venous Insufficiency","fb":"Damaged venous valves let blood pool — chronic edema, hemosiderin staining, and venous ulcers (usually medial malleolus) result.","d":"e"},{"q":"A client comes to the ED with chest pain occurring even at rest, lasting longer than typical. Troponin is negative and the ECG shows no ST elevation. Which condition is most likely?","a":["Unstable angina","STEMI","Pericarditis","Stable angina"],"c":0,"t":"Stable vs Unstable Angina","fb":"Unstable angina = chest pain at rest or with minimal exertion and no cardiac biomarker elevation. It signals an unstable plaque and a high near-term MI risk.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with coronary atherosclerosis develops chest tightness only during brisk walking, which subsides within a few minutes of rest. The nurse identifies this pattern as:","a":["Stable angina","Unstable angina","NSTEMI","Pericarditis"],"c":0,"t":"Stable vs Unstable Angina","fb":"Stable angina is predictable — brought on by a known level of exertion and relieved by rest or nitroglycerin. There is no cell death yet.","d":"m"},{"q":"Why is a STEMI considered more severe than an NSTEMI?","a":["A STEMI represents a full-thickness, completely occluded coronary artery and full-thickness myocardial death","A STEMI never causes troponin to rise","A STEMI is only seen in young clients","A STEMI affects only the pericardium"],"c":0,"t":"STEMI vs NSTEMI","fb":"STEMI = total coronary occlusion → transmural (full-thickness) infarction. NSTEMI = partial occlusion → subendocardial damage.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client presents with chest pain similar to unstable angina but troponin is elevated. The ECG shows no ST elevation. The nurse recognizes this finding is consistent with:","a":["NSTEMI — cells are dying despite no ST elevation","Stable angina","Pericardial effusion","Aplastic anemia"],"c":0,"t":"STEMI vs NSTEMI","fb":"Elevated troponin without ST elevation = NSTEMI. Damage is occurring even if the ECG doesn't show ST changes.","d":"m"},{"q":"Why does troponin rise in a myocardial infarction but not in stable or unstable angina?","a":["Troponin is released only when cardiac muscle cells die","Troponin is released anytime the heart rate rises","Angina causes more cell death than an MI","Troponin reflects platelet activity"],"c":0,"t":"Troponin","fb":"Troponin is an intracellular cardiac muscle protein — it leaks into the bloodstream only when myocytes die, which happens in MI but not in angina.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which conditions are major modifiable risk factors for coronary artery disease? Select all that apply.","a":["Hypertension","High LDL cholesterol","Diabetes mellitus","Obesity and physical inactivity","Smoking"],"c":0,"t":"Coronary Artery Disease","fb":"All of these can be modified through medication and lifestyle. Non-modifiable risks include age, sex, and genetics.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,3,4]},{"q":"A client with a long history of systemic hypertension and coronary artery disease develops dyspnea, orthopnea, frothy pink sputum, and a low ejection fraction. The nurse suspects:","a":["Left-sided heart failure","Right-sided heart failure","Pericardial tamponade","Polycythemia vera"],"c":0,"t":"Heart Failure Left vs Right","fb":"Left HF backs up into the pulmonary circulation — dyspnea, crackles, orthopnea, pink frothy sputum, and a fallen ejection fraction.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which findings would the nurse expect in a client with right-sided heart failure? Select all that apply.","a":["Peripheral edema","Jugular venous distention","Hepatomegaly","Ascites","Pink, frothy sputum"],"c":0,"t":"Heart Failure Left vs Right","fb":"Right HF backs up into the systemic venous system — JVD, edema, congested liver, ascites. Pink frothy sputum is a LEFT-sided sign.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,3]},{"q":"What is the most common cause of right-sided heart failure?","a":["Left-sided heart failure or pulmonary hypertension","Iron deficiency anemia","Mitral valve prolapse alone","Acute pericarditis"],"c":0,"t":"Heart Failure Left vs Right","fb":"When the left side fails, pressure backs up into the lungs and eventually overworks the right ventricle. Primary pulmonary hypertension does the same.","d":"m"},{"q":"Systolic heart failure is best described as:","a":["A weakened, dilated ventricle that cannot contract effectively, producing a low ejection fraction","A thickened, stiff ventricle that cannot relax to fill","A torn valve allowing backflow","Loss of all sympathetic tone"],"c":0,"t":"Heart Failure Diastolic vs Systolic","fb":"Systolic HF = HFrEF — the ventricle is dilated and weak (squeeze problem). EF is reduced.","d":"m"},{"q":"Diastolic heart failure is best described as:","a":["A thickened, non-compliant ventricle that cannot relax and fill adequately, even though EF appears preserved","A weakened, floppy ventricle that cannot contract","An infection of the inner lining of the heart","A complete blockage of a coronary artery"],"c":0,"t":"Heart Failure Diastolic vs Systolic","fb":"Diastolic HF = HFpEF — the ventricle is stiff and small. EF can look normal, but filling is impaired (fill problem).","d":"m"},{"q":"A client is admitted with severe blood loss after a motor vehicle collision. Which compensatory mechanisms help maintain blood pressure and cardiac output? Select all that apply.","a":["Activation of the sympathetic nervous system (tachycardia, vasoconstriction)","Activation of the RAAS system","Activation of the coagulation cascade","Release of ADH to retain water","Massive vasodilation to improve flow"],"c":0,"t":"Blood Loss Compensation","fb":"Hemorrhage triggers SNS, RAAS, ADH, and clotting cascades — all working to preserve perfusion. Vasodilation would worsen shock, not compensate.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,3]},{"q":"Afterload is the resistance the ventricles must overcome to eject blood. Which conditions INCREASE afterload?","a":["Systemic hypertension and atherosclerosis","Hemorrhage and dehydration","Mitral stenosis and pulmonary embolism","Hyponatremia and bradycardia"],"c":0,"t":"Preload/Afterload","fb":"Afterload rises whenever the vessels the heart pumps into are narrowed or stiff — HTN and atherosclerosis are classic causes.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which scenario would DECREASE preload and reduce cardiac output?","a":["Hemorrhage with decreased venous return","Fluid overload with peripheral edema","Bradycardia with normal volume","Mitral regurgitation"],"c":0,"t":"Preload/Afterload","fb":"Preload = volume returning to the ventricle just before contraction. Hemorrhage drops venous return, so preload (and stroke volume) fall.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client has volume overload and an overstretched myocardium. Which natural compensatory response helps offload fluid?","a":["Release of ANP and BNP from the stretched atria and ventricles, promoting diuresis and vasodilation","Release of aldosterone to retain more sodium","Activation of ADH to retain water","Massive SNS activation"],"c":0,"t":"ANP/BNP","fb":"ANP/BNP are released by stretched heart chambers — they oppose RAAS by promoting sodium/water loss and vasodilation.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client recovering from a recent streptococcal pharyngitis several weeks ago now presents with new heart murmurs and joint pain. The nurse suspects:","a":["Rheumatic heart disease from immune-mediated valve inflammation","Acute pericardial tamponade","Polycythemia vera","Aplastic anemia"],"c":0,"t":"Rheumatic Heart Disease","fb":"Rheumatic heart disease follows untreated group A strep — antibodies cross-react with heart tissue, especially the mitral valve.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with a history of IV drug use presents with fever, a new murmur, and positive blood cultures showing bacterial growth and damage to the mitral valve. The nurse suspects:","a":["Infectious endocarditis","Pericarditis","Stable angina","Raynaud's disease"],"c":0,"t":"Infectious Endocarditis","fb":"Fever, new murmur, and positive blood cultures = infectious endocarditis until proven otherwise — especially with IV drug use, central lines, or recent dental work.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client presents with intermittent sharp chest pain that worsens with deep inspiration and improves when leaning forward. Echocardiogram shows inflammation of the outer heart lining. Which condition is most likely?","a":["Pericarditis","Stable angina","Aortic stenosis","Right-sided heart failure"],"c":0,"t":"Pericarditis","fb":"Pericarditis: sharp, pleuritic chest pain that worsens lying flat and improves leaning forward; a friction rub may be heard.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client is asymptomatic but echocardiogram reveals excess fluid in the pericardial sac. There is no hemodynamic compromise. The nurse documents this as:","a":["Pericardial effusion","Pericardial tamponade","Heart failure","Aortic aneurysm"],"c":0,"t":"Pericardial Effusion","fb":"Pericardial effusion = fluid in the pericardial sac. If it builds up fast or compresses the heart, it becomes tamponade.","d":"e"},{"q":"A client is admitted with suspected pericardial tamponade. Which findings would the nurse most likely observe?","a":["Muffled heart sounds, hypotension, and jugular venous distention","Loud S1 and S2, hypertension, and warm extremities","Bounding pulses and bradycardia","Crackles in the lungs and pink frothy sputum"],"c":0,"t":"Pericardial Tamponade","fb":"Beck's triad: muffled heart sounds, hypotension, and JVD. Fluid compressing the heart prevents filling and drops cardiac output.","d":"m"},{"q":"Why is pericardial tamponade considered a medical emergency?","a":["Fluid compresses the heart so it cannot fill, dropping cardiac output and causing shock","It always causes a heart attack within minutes","It rapidly destroys the heart valves","It causes immediate respiratory failure from airway obstruction"],"c":0,"t":"Pericardial Tamponade","fb":"Tamponade physiology = a heart that can't fill. Even with normal contraction, output collapses and shock follows.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client who had a viral upper respiratory infection two weeks ago now has new chest discomfort, palpitations, and a new arrhythmia. Echocardiogram shows inflammation of the heart muscle itself. Which condition is most likely?","a":["Myocarditis","Pericarditis","Stable angina","Aplastic anemia"],"c":0,"t":"Myocarditis","fb":"Myocarditis: viral or autoimmune inflammation of the myocardium — can cause arrhythmias, heart failure, and sudden death.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client suffered a large STEMI two days ago and suddenly collapses without warning. Which mechanism most likely caused death?","a":["A lethal ventricular arrhythmia such as ventricular fibrillation","An acute pernicious anemia event","A massive iron deficiency","An acute hemolytic transfusion reaction"],"c":0,"t":"Sudden Cardiac Death","fb":"Sudden cardiac death after MI is most often caused by ventricular tachycardia or ventricular fibrillation arising from scarred, irritable myocardium.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with mitral valve regurgitation asks why their cardiac output is declining. The nurse's best response is:","a":["Some of the blood that should leave the heart leaks backward into the left atrium during contraction, so less is delivered to the body","The valve is too narrow for blood to pass through","The valve is fused shut and won't open","The valve only affects the right side of the heart"],"c":0,"t":"Valvular Disorders","fb":"Regurgitation = leaky valve. Blood sloshes back into the atrium during systole, reducing forward output and dilating the chamber over time.","d":"m"},{"q":"A newborn is diagnosed with a congenital heart defect that shunts blood from left to right, so they remain pink at rest. Which defects fit this acyanotic, left-to-right pattern? Select all that apply.","a":["Ventricular septal defect (VSD)","Atrial septal defect (ASD)","Patent ductus arteriosus (PDA)","Tetralogy of Fallot","Transposition of the great arteries"],"c":0,"t":"Congenital Heart Defects","fb":"VSD, ASD, and PDA shunt oxygenated blood from the higher-pressure left side back to the right — they are acyanotic. ToF and transposition are cyanotic defects.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"A newborn with Tetralogy of Fallot is most likely to demonstrate which finding?","a":["Bluish (cyanotic) skin, especially during feeding or crying","Bounding peripheral pulses with hypertension","Profound peripheral edema in the lower extremities","Loud, productive cough with green sputum"],"c":0,"t":"Tetralogy of Fallot","fb":"ToF causes right-to-left shunting of deoxygenated blood — cyanotic episodes (tet spells) appear with exertion like crying or feeding.","d":"m"},{"q":"A young woman with an autoimmune condition reports that in cold weather her fingers turn white, then blue, then red, with severe pain. The nurse suspects:","a":["Raynaud's phenomenon — vasospasm of the arterioles","Acute arterial occlusion requiring surgery","Deep vein thrombosis","Venous insufficiency"],"c":0,"t":"Raynaud's","fb":"Raynaud's = arteriolar vasospasm in fingers/toes triggered by cold or stress. Color changes (white → blue → red) and pain are classic.","d":"m"},{"q":"A 72-year-old client reports increasing fatigue and being short of breath when carrying groceries up one flight of stairs. The nurse notes pale conjunctiva. Which laboratory result would most support a diagnosis of anemia?","a":["Hemoglobin 8.4 g/dL","White blood cell count 7,200/mm3","Platelet count 230,000/mm3","Sodium 138 mEq/L"],"c":0,"t":"Anemia","fb":"Anemia is defined by a low hemoglobin and/or hematocrit. WBC, platelet, and sodium values reflect other systems.","d":"e"},{"q":"The nurse is teaching a client newly diagnosed with anemia why they feel so tired. Which explanation is most accurate?","a":["Red blood cells carry oxygen, and when there are fewer of them, tissues do not get enough oxygen to make energy","Anemia causes the kidneys to make too much urine, depleting energy","Anemia makes the blood too thick to circulate efficiently","Anemia means the heart valves are leaking and oxygen escapes"],"c":0,"t":"Anemia","fb":"Anemia drops oxygen-carrying capacity. Tissues become hypoxic, metabolism suffers, and fatigue results.","d":"e"},{"q":"A toddler is being evaluated for fatigue and poor appetite. The mother reports the child drinks 'lots of cow's milk all day long.' Labs reveal microcytic, hypochromic anemia. The nurse recognizes this picture as most consistent with:","a":["Iron deficiency anemia","Sickle cell anemia","Aplastic anemia","Polycythemia vera"],"c":0,"t":"Iron Deficiency Anemia","fb":"Excessive cow's milk in toddlers displaces iron-rich foods and irritates the gut — classic cause of pediatric iron deficiency anemia.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client started on oral iron supplements asks how to maximize absorption. Which instruction is best?","a":["Take the iron with a source of vitamin C such as orange juice on an empty stomach if tolerated","Take the iron with a calcium-rich glass of milk","Take the iron with an antacid to prevent stomach upset","Take the iron only at bedtime with a large meal"],"c":0,"t":"Iron Deficiency Anemia","fb":"Vitamin C enhances non-heme iron absorption. Calcium, antacids, and large meals reduce absorption.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with long-standing alcohol use disorder and a poor diet presents with macrocytic anemia. Which deficiency is most likely contributing?","a":["Folate deficiency","Iron deficiency","Vitamin K deficiency","Calcium deficiency"],"c":0,"t":"B12 Deficiency","fb":"Chronic alcohol use depletes folate stores and interferes with absorption — macrocytic anemia results. B12 deficiency has a similar smear but adds neurologic findings.","d":"m"},{"q":"The nurse is teaching a client newly diagnosed with pernicious anemia why monthly B12 injections will be needed for life. Which explanation is correct?","a":["Without intrinsic factor, oral B12 cannot be absorbed, so it must be given by injection","The injections rebuild the parietal cells of the stomach","Oral B12 causes the autoimmune attack to worsen","The medication is given monthly to flush iron from the body"],"c":0,"t":"Pernicious Anemia","fb":"Pernicious anemia destroys parietal cells and intrinsic factor. Injectable B12 bypasses the gut, going straight into systemic circulation.","d":"m"},{"q":"The nurse caring for a client with aplastic anemia would expect which combination of laboratory findings?","a":["Low RBCs, low WBCs, and low platelets","High RBCs and high WBCs with normal platelets","Normal RBCs and high platelets","Low RBCs with high WBCs and high platelets"],"c":0,"t":"Aplastic Anemia","fb":"Aplastic anemia is pancytopenia — the marrow fails to make all three cell lines.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with aplastic anemia is at greatest risk for which of the following complications? Select all that apply.","a":["Life-threatening infection","Spontaneous bleeding","Severe fatigue and dyspnea","Acute pernicious anemia","Bruising from minor trauma"],"c":0,"t":"Aplastic Anemia","fb":"Pancytopenia leads to infection (low WBC), bleeding/bruising (low platelets), and fatigue/dyspnea (low RBC). Pernicious anemia is unrelated.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,4]},{"q":"A client with sickle cell disease is admitted in vaso-occlusive crisis. Which nursing intervention has the highest priority?","a":["Aggressive IV hydration and pain management","Restricting fluid intake","Encouraging vigorous physical activity","Withholding oxygen until O2 sat falls below 88%"],"c":0,"t":"Sickle Cell Anemia","fb":"Sickled cells obstruct microvasculature — fluids reduce viscosity, oxygen supports demand, and analgesia addresses ischemic pain.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which statement about sickle cell disease is correct?","a":["It is inherited in an autosomal recessive pattern","It is caused by a vitamin B12 deficiency","It develops only after age 50","It is contagious through saliva"],"c":0,"t":"Sickle Cell Anemia","fb":"Sickle cell anemia is an inherited autosomal recessive hemoglobinopathy — both parents must carry the trait for the child to express the disease.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client of Mediterranean descent has lifelong mild anemia and is told they have beta-thalassemia minor. Which statement about this condition is correct?","a":["It is an inherited hemoglobin chain production defect, not a nutritional deficiency","Iron supplements will cure the condition","It is caused by a recent viral infection","It develops only after age 65"],"c":0,"t":"Thalassemia","fb":"Thalassemia is genetic — supplemental iron will not help (and can cause iron overload). It is not nutritional or infectious.","d":"m"},{"q":"A 60-year-old client with a ruddy face, persistent headaches, dizziness, and itching after warm showers has a hematocrit of 60%. The nurse anticipates which condition?","a":["Polycythemia vera","Iron deficiency anemia","Sickle cell crisis","DIC"],"c":0,"t":"Polycythemia Vera","fb":"PV produces an overload of red cells. Symptoms include plethora, headache, hyperviscosity, pruritus (especially aquagenic), and clotting.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which therapeutic intervention is commonly used to manage polycythemia vera and reduce thrombotic risk?","a":["Therapeutic phlebotomy to reduce hematocrit","Daily iron supplementation","High-dose vitamin K","Restricting fluids to below 1 liter per day"],"c":0,"t":"Polycythemia Vera","fb":"Phlebotomy removes excess RBC mass and lowers viscosity — the cornerstone of PV management.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with von Willebrand disease asks why they tend to have heavy menstrual periods and frequent nosebleeds. The nurse's best explanation is:","a":["Without von Willebrand factor, platelets cannot stick to injured blood vessels and the bleeding doesn't stop quickly","The platelets become too sticky and clump too aggressively","The clotting factors work too well and exhaust themselves","The body produces too many red cells"],"c":0,"t":"Von Willebrand","fb":"vWF is the glue that helps platelets adhere to damaged endothelium. Without it, primary hemostasis fails — mucocutaneous bleeding (epistaxis, menorrhagia) results.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with septic shock develops oozing from IV sites along with new clots in the toes. Laboratory results show prolonged PT and PTT, low fibrinogen, low platelets, and elevated D-dimer. The nurse recognizes this as:","a":["Disseminated intravascular coagulation","Acute hemolytic transfusion reaction","Polycythemia vera","Aplastic anemia"],"c":0,"t":"DIC","fb":"DIC: simultaneous clotting and bleeding. Consumed factors and platelets give prolonged PT/PTT, low fibrinogen, low platelets, and high D-dimer.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which conditions are common triggers for disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC)? Select all that apply.","a":["Severe sepsis","Major trauma","Obstetric complications (e.g., amniotic fluid embolism)","Massive burns","Mild seasonal allergies"],"c":0,"t":"DIC","fb":"DIC is triggered by overwhelming tissue injury or infection — sepsis, trauma, OB emergencies, and severe burns top the list. Allergies do not trigger DIC.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,3]},{"q":"A client receiving a blood transfusion suddenly develops chills, low back pain, dyspnea, and dark urine. The nurse's FIRST action is to:","a":["Stop the transfusion immediately and keep the IV line open with normal saline","Slow the transfusion to half the rate","Give the next scheduled dose of antihypertensive","Document and continue the transfusion"],"c":0,"t":"Acute Hemolytic Reaction","fb":"Acute hemolytic transfusion reaction is life-threatening. Stop the blood first, keep saline running through a new line, and notify the provider/blood bank.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which precaution is most important in preventing an acute hemolytic transfusion reaction?","a":["Verifying ABO and Rh compatibility with two qualified staff before infusing","Warming the blood to body temperature in the microwave","Giving the unit over no more than 30 minutes","Routinely administering acetaminophen before every transfusion"],"c":0,"t":"Acute Hemolytic Reaction","fb":"Acute hemolytic reactions almost always come from ABO mismatch. Strict two-person verification at bedside is the most effective preventive measure.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with newly diagnosed atrial fibrillation has a heart rate of 132 beats per minute. Which complications are most concerning? Select all that apply.","a":["Embolic stroke","Heart failure from poor ventricular filling","Decreased cardiac output and hypotension","Increased myocardial oxygen demand","Acute iron deficiency"],"c":0,"t":"Atrial Fibrillation","fb":"Rapid A-fib drops filling time, fuels clot formation, and stresses the myocardium. Iron deficiency is unrelated to the arrhythmia.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,3]},{"q":"A client with atrial fibrillation is prescribed warfarin. Which teaching point is most important?","a":["Keep dietary vitamin K (leafy green) intake consistent and report any unusual bleeding","Avoid all green vegetables completely","Take an extra dose if you forget the morning dose","Stop the medication once your heart rate normalizes"],"c":0,"t":"Atrial Fibrillation","fb":"Warfarin works by antagonizing vitamin K. Wild swings in vitamin K intake destabilize INR — consistency is the goal.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which is a key pathophysiologic difference between supraventricular and ventricular arrhythmias?","a":["Ventricular arrhythmias arise below the AV node, often produce wide QRS complexes, and are more likely to be life-threatening","Supraventricular arrhythmias always cause sudden cardiac arrest","Ventricular arrhythmias produce only narrow, regular QRS complexes","All arrhythmias originate in the SA node"],"c":0,"t":"Arrhythmias","fb":"Ventricular arrhythmias (VT, VF) bypass normal conduction, create wide QRS, drop CO, and can degenerate into arrest.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client's blood pressure consistently reads 162/96 in clinic. The nurse explains that long-term uncontrolled hypertension can cause which of the following? Select all that apply.","a":["Damage to the kidneys leading to chronic kidney disease","Left ventricular hypertrophy","Increased risk of stroke","Increased risk of myocardial infarction","Improved blood vessel elasticity"],"c":0,"t":"Hypertension","fb":"HTN damages the endothelium of every organ — kidneys, brain, heart, retina. It does not improve vessel elasticity; it accelerates stiffening.","d":"e","m":[0,1,2,3]},{"q":"A client asks the nurse why hypertension is called the 'silent killer.' Which response is best?","a":["Hypertension often causes no symptoms until significant organ damage has already occurred","Hypertension makes people lose consciousness without warning","Hypertension causes silent heart attacks during sleep","Hypertension cannot be detected on routine blood pressure checks"],"c":0,"t":"Hypertension","fb":"HTN is typically asymptomatic until end-organ damage (stroke, MI, kidney failure) presents — hence the nickname.","d":"m"},{"q":"Atherosclerosis develops in stages. Place the following events in correct order from earliest to most advanced.","a":["Endothelial injury → fatty streak → fibrous plaque → complicated lesion with possible rupture","Plaque rupture → endothelial injury → fatty streak → calcification","Calcified plaque → fatty streak → endothelial healing → ulceration","Complicated lesion → fatty streak → endothelial injury → smooth plaque"],"c":0,"t":"Atherosclerosis","fb":"Atherosclerosis begins with endothelial injury, progresses through fatty streaks and fibrous plaques, and may end in a complicated, ruptured lesion that causes acute thrombosis.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which of the following are modifiable risk factors for atherosclerosis? Select all that apply.","a":["Smoking","Hypertension","High LDL cholesterol","Diabetes mellitus","Age over 60"],"c":0,"t":"Atherosclerosis","fb":"Smoking, BP, LDL, and diabetes can be modified. Age is non-modifiable.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,3]},{"q":"A client with peripheral artery disease asks the nurse why they have leg pain while walking but not at rest. Which response is best?","a":["Walking increases muscle oxygen demand, but narrowed arteries cannot deliver enough blood, so the muscles cramp until you rest","The pain is caused by varicose veins, which swell only when walking","Walking pinches a nerve in the spine that resolves at rest","The pain reflects too much blood flow to the legs"],"c":0,"t":"Intermittent Claudication","fb":"Intermittent claudication is exertional ischemic pain — narrowed arteries can't keep up with demand. Resting drops demand and the pain resolves.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with peripheral artery disease asks how to protect their feet at home. Which instructions are appropriate? Select all that apply.","a":["Inspect feet daily for any wounds or color changes","Avoid using heating pads on the legs","Wear well-fitting shoes and avoid going barefoot","Stop smoking","Apply tight elastic bandages to keep blood moving"],"c":0,"t":"Peripheral Artery Disease","fb":"PAD limbs are fragile and ischemic — protect from injury, never apply heat (poor sensation), avoid tight wraps (worsens flow), and stop smoking.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,3]},{"q":"A client diagnosed with chronic venous insufficiency would most likely present with which findings?","a":["Brown skin discoloration around the ankles with edema and an ulcer over the medial malleolus","Cool, pale, hairless lower legs with diminished pedal pulses","Sudden severe chest pain radiating to the jaw","Crackles in the lungs and pink frothy sputum"],"c":0,"t":"Venous Insufficiency","fb":"Venous insufficiency = pooled venous blood → edema, hemosiderin staining (brown), and medial malleolus ulcers. Cool/pale/no hair fits arterial disease.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which intervention is most appropriate for a client with venous insufficiency?","a":["Wearing prescribed graduated compression stockings during the day","Hanging the legs in a dependent position for several hours each day","Applying tight tourniquets to the thighs at night","Restricting fluid intake to less than 500 mL per day"],"c":0,"t":"Venous Insufficiency","fb":"Compression stockings counteract venous pooling and reduce edema. Dependency worsens it; tourniquets and fluid restriction are inappropriate.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client who has had stable angina for years now reports that their chest pain occurs with less activity, lasts longer, and is no longer fully relieved by nitroglycerin. The nurse recognizes this change as:","a":["Progression to unstable angina, which requires emergency evaluation","An expected age-related change","A sign that the antihypertensive needs to be discontinued","Improvement of the underlying coronary artery disease"],"c":0,"t":"Stable vs Unstable Angina","fb":"Crescendo pattern angina that worsens with less effort and lasts longer signals plaque instability — a true cardiac emergency.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which best describes the underlying mechanism of stable angina?","a":["A fixed atherosclerotic plaque that limits flow only when oxygen demand rises with exertion","Complete coronary occlusion causing full-thickness infarction","Vasospasm of a coronary artery at rest, unrelated to plaque","Inflammation of the pericardial sac"],"c":0,"t":"Stable vs Unstable Angina","fb":"Stable angina: predictable, exertional, transient ischemia from a fixed plaque. Demand rises beyond what the narrowed artery can supply.","d":"m"},{"q":"On a 12-lead ECG, a STEMI is most clearly identified by which finding?","a":["ST-segment elevation in two or more contiguous leads","Diffuse PR-segment depression","An irregularly irregular rhythm with no P waves","A widened QRS with peaked T waves"],"c":0,"t":"STEMI vs NSTEMI","fb":"STEMI = ST-elevation MI. Contiguous ST elevations localize the infarcted region and identify total occlusion.","d":"e"},{"q":"A client arrives with chest pain that radiates down the left arm. Initial troponin is normal. Two hours later, troponin is markedly elevated and ECG shows new T-wave inversion without ST elevation. The nurse interprets this as:","a":["NSTEMI","Stable angina","Pericarditis","Pericardial effusion"],"c":0,"t":"STEMI vs NSTEMI","fb":"Rising troponin without ST elevation = NSTEMI. Cells are dying despite the absence of ST elevation.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client's troponin is measured at 0.02 ng/mL initially and 8.4 ng/mL six hours later. Which interpretation is most accurate?","a":["A significant rise indicating myocardial cell death","A normal post-exercise variation","Lab error — troponin does not change this quickly","Evidence of platelet activation only"],"c":0,"t":"Troponin","fb":"Troponin rises 3-12 hours after cell injury and peaks at about 24 hours. A serial rise of this magnitude indicates acute myocardial necrosis.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client diagnosed with coronary artery disease asks why aspirin is being prescribed. The nurse explains:","a":["Aspirin reduces platelet aggregation, lowering the risk of clot formation in narrowed coronary arteries","Aspirin dissolves the cholesterol plaque","Aspirin slows the heart rate to reduce oxygen demand","Aspirin increases hemoglobin levels"],"c":0,"t":"Coronary Artery Disease","fb":"Low-dose aspirin is an antiplatelet — it prevents platelet plugging on unstable atherosclerotic plaques, reducing MI risk.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with congestive heart failure is admitted with shortness of breath, bilateral crackles, and orthopnea. Which is the most likely underlying problem?","a":["Left-sided heart failure causing pulmonary congestion","Right-sided heart failure with hepatomegaly","Acute pericarditis","Aplastic anemia"],"c":0,"t":"Heart Failure Left vs Right","fb":"Crackles and orthopnea = backup into the pulmonary system = left-sided HF.","d":"m"},{"q":"The nurse weighing a client with heart failure notes a 4-pound weight gain over two days. The best interpretation is:","a":["Fluid retention indicating worsening heart failure","Expected variation from dietary intake","Improvement of cardiac function","Lab equipment error"],"c":0,"t":"Heart Failure Left vs Right","fb":"In HF, a rapid weight gain (more than 2-3 lb in a day or 5 lb in a week) signals fluid retention — a key early warning sign of decompensation.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with heart failure has an ejection fraction of 55% but symptoms of shortness of breath and fatigue. Echocardiogram shows a thickened, stiff left ventricle. The nurse recognizes this as:","a":["Heart failure with preserved ejection fraction (diastolic heart failure)","Heart failure with reduced ejection fraction","Acute myocardial infarction","Pericardial tamponade"],"c":0,"t":"Heart Failure Diastolic vs Systolic","fb":"HFpEF (diastolic HF): EF looks normal, but the stiff ventricle cannot fill properly. Hypertension and aging are common causes.","d":"m"},{"q":"In systolic heart failure (HFrEF), which compensatory mechanism initially helps maintain cardiac output but eventually worsens the disease?","a":["Sustained activation of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS) with sodium and water retention","Continuous suppression of the sympathetic nervous system","Spontaneous regrowth of cardiac muscle","Permanent decrease in heart rate"],"c":0,"t":"Heart Failure Diastolic vs Systolic","fb":"RAAS initially boosts preload and BP — but chronic activation drives fluid overload, vasoconstriction, and remodeling, worsening HF over time.","d":"m"},{"q":"A trauma client has lost 1.5 liters of blood. Which findings would the nurse expect as the body compensates? Select all that apply.","a":["Tachycardia","Cool, clammy skin from peripheral vasoconstriction","Decreased urine output","Restlessness and anxiety","Bradycardia with warm, flushed skin"],"c":0,"t":"Blood Loss Compensation","fb":"Sympathetic activation: tachycardia, vasoconstriction (cool/clammy), reduced renal perfusion (low UOP), and early CNS irritability. Bradycardia/warm skin is the opposite picture.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,3]},{"q":"A client with severe systemic hypertension is at greatest risk for which type of heart failure initially?","a":["Left-sided heart failure due to high afterload","Right-sided heart failure due to high preload","Aplastic anemia","Polycythemia vera"],"c":0,"t":"Preload/Afterload","fb":"Systemic hypertension forces the left ventricle to pump against high pressure (afterload) — leading to LVH and eventually left-sided HF.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which intervention would DECREASE preload in a client with acute decompensated heart failure?","a":["Administering a loop diuretic such as furosemide","Giving a rapid IV fluid bolus","Encouraging high sodium intake","Lying flat with legs elevated"],"c":0,"t":"Preload/Afterload","fb":"Diuretics reduce circulating volume → less venous return → lower preload. Fluids, sodium, and leg elevation increase preload.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client's lab shows an elevated BNP. The nurse anticipates which condition?","a":["Heart failure with ventricular stretch","Iron deficiency anemia","Sickle cell crisis","Pernicious anemia"],"c":0,"t":"ANP/BNP","fb":"BNP is released by overstretched ventricles. Elevated BNP supports the diagnosis of heart failure and rises with its severity.","d":"e"},{"q":"A client recovering from rheumatic fever as a child now presents in adulthood with progressive shortness of breath and a diastolic murmur. Which valve is most commonly affected?","a":["Mitral valve","Pulmonic valve","Tricuspid valve only","Aortic valve only — never mitral"],"c":0,"t":"Rheumatic Heart Disease","fb":"Rheumatic heart disease most commonly damages the mitral valve, often producing mitral stenosis years later.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which client is at greatest risk for infectious endocarditis?","a":["A client with a prosthetic heart valve undergoing dental work without prophylactic antibiotics","A client with iron deficiency anemia","A client with mild allergic rhinitis","A client with chronic constipation"],"c":0,"t":"Infectious Endocarditis","fb":"Prosthetic valves, IV drug use, central venous lines, and invasive dental procedures expose the bloodstream to bacteria that can colonize cardiac surfaces.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with pericarditis describes the chest pain. Which characteristics are typical of pericarditis? Select all that apply.","a":["Sharp, pleuritic chest pain","Worse when lying flat, better when sitting up and leaning forward","Worsened by deep inspiration","Often follows a recent viral illness","Crushing pressure that radiates down the left arm and resolves at rest"],"c":0,"t":"Pericarditis","fb":"Pericarditis pain is sharp, positional (worse supine, better leaning forward), and pleuritic. Crushing substernal pressure suggests MI, not pericarditis.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,3]},{"q":"A pericardial effusion is most concerning to the nurse when it:","a":["Develops rapidly and begins to compress the heart, impairing filling","Develops slowly over months without symptoms","Is small and well tolerated","Is identified incidentally on imaging"],"c":0,"t":"Pericardial Effusion","fb":"Rapidly accumulating effusion has no time to stretch the pericardium and quickly causes tamponade physiology. Slow effusions are often tolerated.","d":"m"},{"q":"A nurse caring for a client with pericardial tamponade would expect to see which finding consistent with Beck's triad?","a":["Hypotension, muffled heart sounds, and jugular venous distention","Hypertension, bounding pulses, and bradycardia","Fever, rash, and joint pain","Pink frothy sputum, crackles, and dyspnea"],"c":0,"t":"Pericardial Tamponade","fb":"Beck's triad describes the classic tamponade picture: low BP, muffled heart tones (fluid muffles sound), and distended neck veins (back-up of venous blood).","d":"m"},{"q":"A young adult with a recent viral infection develops new-onset heart failure symptoms and an arrhythmia. Endomyocardial biopsy reveals lymphocytic infiltration of the heart muscle. The nurse recognizes this as:","a":["Myocarditis","Pericarditis","Stable angina","Polycythemia vera"],"c":0,"t":"Myocarditis","fb":"Viral myocarditis causes lymphocytic inflammation of the myocardium itself, presenting with HF symptoms, arrhythmias, and sometimes sudden cardiac death.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client survived a cardiac arrest and is now scheduled for an implantable cardioverter-defibrillator (ICD). The nurse explains the device works by:","a":["Sensing dangerous ventricular arrhythmias and delivering a shock to restore normal rhythm","Continuously pacing the atria at a fixed rate","Lowering the blood pressure by releasing medication","Improving coronary blood flow directly"],"c":0,"t":"Sudden Cardiac Death","fb":"ICDs detect life-threatening arrhythmias (VT/VF) and deliver defibrillation — preventing sudden cardiac death in high-risk clients.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with severe aortic stenosis would most likely present with which classic triad of symptoms?","a":["Angina, syncope, and dyspnea on exertion","Fever, rash, and joint pain","Polyuria, polydipsia, and polyphagia","Hematuria, oliguria, and edema"],"c":0,"t":"Valvular Disorders","fb":"The classic aortic stenosis triad is angina, syncope (especially with exertion), and exertional dyspnea. The stiff valve restricts forward flow.","d":"m"},{"q":"A neonate is diagnosed with patent ductus arteriosus (PDA). The nurse anticipates which finding?","a":["A continuous machine-like murmur and a left-to-right shunt","Profound cyanosis from a right-to-left shunt","Absent peripheral pulses","Bounding cranial bruits"],"c":0,"t":"Congenital Heart Defects","fb":"PDA is an acyanotic defect with a left-to-right shunt and a characteristic continuous 'machinery' murmur. It does not typically cause central cyanosis.","d":"m"},{"q":"Tetralogy of Fallot consists of four defects. Which combination is correct?","a":["Pulmonary stenosis, right ventricular hypertrophy, ventricular septal defect, and overriding aorta","Aortic stenosis, left ventricular hypertrophy, atrial septal defect, and pulmonary regurgitation","Mitral stenosis, atrial dilation, tricuspid stenosis, and pericardial effusion","Coarctation of the aorta, PDA, mitral regurgitation, and a thickened pericardium"],"c":0,"t":"Tetralogy of Fallot","fb":"Tetralogy = pulmonary stenosis, right ventricular hypertrophy, VSD, and an overriding aorta. The result is right-to-left shunting and cyanosis.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with Raynaud's phenomenon asks the nurse what lifestyle changes will help. Which instructions are appropriate? Select all that apply.","a":["Avoid cold exposure and wear warm gloves","Stop smoking","Manage emotional stress","Avoid medications that cause vasoconstriction such as decongestants","Take daily caffeine to improve circulation"],"c":0,"t":"Raynaud's","fb":"Raynaud's is provoked by cold, stress, nicotine, and vasoconstrictors. Caffeine is a vasoconstrictor and should be limited, not encouraged.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,3]},{"q":"A client is admitted with a suspected acute myocardial infarction. Which assessment findings would the nurse expect? Select all that apply.","a":["Crushing substernal chest pressure that may radiate to the jaw or left arm","Diaphoresis and nausea","Shortness of breath","Sense of impending doom","Pain that is reliably relieved by rest within 2 minutes"],"c":0,"t":"STEMI vs NSTEMI","fb":"MI pain is severe, often radiating, with diaphoresis, nausea, dyspnea, and anxiety. Unlike stable angina, it is NOT relieved by rest or nitroglycerin within a few minutes.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,3]}]},"6":{"title":"FILTER & BREATH — Ch 11 Renal · Ch 12 Respiratory","questions":[{"q":"A client newly diagnosed with kidney failure asks the nurse how creatinine and the glomerular filtration rate (GFR) relate to one another. Which response is most accurate?","a":["As serum creatinine rises, the GFR falls because the kidneys are clearing less waste","Creatinine and GFR rise together when filtration is normal","Creatinine reflects liver function while GFR reflects kidney function","GFR can only be measured directly with a 24-hour urine and does not relate to creatinine"],"c":0,"t":"Kidney Lab Values","fb":"Serum creatinine and GFR move in opposite directions: when filtration slows, creatinine accumulates and GFR drops.","d":"e"},{"q":"A 22-year-old client develops nephritic syndrome about two weeks after a strep throat infection and asks what happened inside the kidneys. Which explanation by the nurse is most accurate?","a":["Antibody complexes inflamed the glomerulus so it can no longer filter properly","Bacteria spread directly into the renal tubules and destroyed them","High blood pressure scarred the renal arteries","The kidneys lost the ability to make urine because of dehydration"],"c":0,"t":"Nephritic Syndrome","fb":"Post-streptococcal nephritic syndrome is an immune-complex inflammation of the glomerulus that disrupts filtration.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which assessment findings would the nurse expect in a client with nephritic syndrome?","a":["Hypertension and hematuria","Hypotension and clear, dilute urine","Hyperlipidemia and frothy urine","Hypoglycemia and oliguria with ketones"],"c":0,"t":"Nephritic Syndrome","fb":"Glomerular inflammation lets red cells leak into urine (hematuria) and fluid retention pushes blood pressure up.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with longstanding diabetes mellitus is told they have developed nephrotic syndrome. Which findings would the nurse expect on assessment? Select all that apply.","a":["Hyperlipidemia","Massive proteinuria","Peripheral edema","Hematuria with red cell casts","Hypertension as the dominant feature"],"c":0,"t":"Nephrotic Syndrome","fb":"Nephrotic syndrome: huge protein loss in urine, low albumin, edema, and compensatory rise in lipids. Hematuria is more typical of the nephritic picture.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"A nursing student asks why a pulmonary embolism (PE) is described as a V/Q mismatch. The best answer is:","a":["The lung segment is still being ventilated, but the clot blocks perfusion to it","The clot blocks airflow into the alveoli even though blood flow is normal","Both ventilation and perfusion are equally lost","Diffusion across the alveolar membrane is destroyed by the clot"],"c":0,"t":"V/Q Mismatch","fb":"A PE leaves ventilation intact but cuts off perfusion to that lung region, producing the classic V/Q mismatch.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client is admitted with a confirmed deep vein thrombosis (DVT) of the left calf. Which complication is the client at greatest risk of developing?","a":["Pulmonary embolism","Right-sided heart failure","Pneumothorax","Bladder cancer"],"c":0,"t":"DVT","fb":"A DVT can dislodge and travel through the venous circulation to the pulmonary arteries, becoming a PE.","d":"e"},{"q":"A client who had a femur fracture two days ago now reports sudden shortness of breath, sharp pleuritic chest pain, and is coughing up blood-tinged sputum. The nurse suspects:","a":["Pulmonary embolism","Acute bronchitis","Atelectasis","Pulmonary hypertension"],"c":0,"t":"Pulmonary Embolism","fb":"After a long-bone fracture, immobility plus possible fat/clot embolus presenting with pleuritic chest pain, dyspnea, and hemoptysis points to PE.","d":"m"},{"q":"Hypoxia is an insufficient amount of oxygen at the tissue level. Which assessment findings would the nurse expect in a client experiencing hypoxia?","a":["Tachycardia and cyanosis","Bradycardia and warm, flushed skin","Hypotension and bounding pulses","Bradypnea and pink mucous membranes"],"c":0,"t":"Hypoxia","fb":"Tissues short on oxygen drive the heart faster (tachycardia) and deoxygenated blood gives a bluish (cyanotic) appearance.","d":"e"},{"q":"A client recovering from a hip replacement develops a pulmonary embolism. The nurse explains that which of the following are known risk factors for PE? Select all that apply.","a":["Smoking","Obesity","Prolonged immobility","Deep vein thrombosis","Daily aerobic exercise"],"c":0,"t":"Pulmonary Embolism","fb":"Virchow-style risks (immobility, obesity, DVT) plus endothelial irritants like smoking raise PE risk; routine aerobic activity is protective.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,3]},{"q":"Asthma is a common inflammatory disease of the airways. Which features are characteristic of asthma? Select all that apply.","a":["Bronchoconstriction","Increased mucus production","Recurrent reversible airway obstruction","Permanent destruction of alveolar walls","Loss of surfactant"],"c":0,"t":"Asthma","fb":"Asthma = bronchoconstriction, mucus, and reversible airway narrowing. Alveolar destruction and surfactant loss fit emphysema, not asthma.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"A client arrives at the clinic with shortness of breath and a constant cough. On assessment the nurse notes prolonged expiration, bilateral wheezing, and low oxygen saturation. Which condition does the nurse most likely suspect?","a":["Asthma","Pneumonia","Pulmonary embolism","Tension pneumothorax"],"c":0,"t":"Asthma","fb":"Prolonged expiration with bilateral expiratory wheezing is the classic asthma exacerbation picture.","d":"m"},{"q":"Acute bronchitis is a self-limiting illness that produces an excessive cough and mucus. The most common cause is:","a":["A viral or bacterial infection","Chronic exposure to cigarette smoke","An inherited surfactant defect","Long-term occupational dust exposure"],"c":0,"t":"Bronchitis","fb":"Acute bronchitis is usually triggered by a viral (more common) or bacterial respiratory infection.","d":"e"},{"q":"A client with severe COPD has developed pulmonary hypertension. The nurse should monitor for which additional complication?","a":["Right-sided heart failure","Left-sided heart failure with pulmonary edema","Glomerulonephritis","Polycystic kidney disease"],"c":0,"t":"COPD","fb":"Chronic pulmonary hypertension forces the right ventricle to pump against high pressures, eventually causing right-sided failure (cor pulmonale).","d":"m"},{"q":"Chronic bronchitis is defined as a productive cough lasting more than three months in two consecutive years. Which is the most common underlying cause?","a":["Long-term exposure to a toxin or irritant such as tobacco smoke","A single viral respiratory infection","Inhalation of a foreign body","Autoimmune destruction of bronchial epithelium"],"c":0,"t":"Bronchitis","fb":"Chronic bronchitis is driven by chronic irritation of the airways, most often from cigarette smoke or other inhaled toxins.","d":"m"},{"q":"Emphysema destroys alveolar walls and impairs gas diffusion. What additional change occurs because of this destruction?","a":["Surfactant is lost, preventing complete exhalation and trapping air in the alveoli","Mucus production increases to fill the destroyed alveoli","The bronchi spasm and constrict reversibly","Pleural fluid accumulates in the alveolar sacs"],"c":0,"t":"Emphysema","fb":"Loss of alveolar surface area also means loss of surfactant; alveoli collapse on exhalation and air becomes trapped, producing the barrel chest.","d":"m"},{"q":"COPD is a combination of emphysema and chronic bronchitis. Which findings are most commonly seen with COPD? Select all that apply.","a":["Barrel chest","Hypoxemia","Hypercapnia","Hematuria","Tracheal deviation"],"c":0,"t":"COPD","fb":"Air trapping enlarges the chest (barrel chest); poor gas exchange produces low oxygen and rising CO2.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"A lower respiratory infection causes pus or fluid to fill the alveoli, impairing gas exchange. Which condition does this describe?","a":["Pneumonia","Asthma","Pulmonary embolism","Atelectasis"],"c":0,"t":"Pneumonia","fb":"Pneumonia consolidates the alveoli with exudate, blocking diffusion across the alveolar membrane.","d":"e"},{"q":"Cystic fibrosis is an inherited disorder that causes thick, sticky mucus production. Which two respiratory problems are most common in clients with cystic fibrosis?","a":["Hypoxia and recurrent pulmonary infections","Pulmonary embolism and hemothorax","Pneumothorax and flail chest","Pulmonary fibrosis and pneumoconiosis"],"c":0,"t":"Cystic Fibrosis","fb":"Thick mucus traps bacteria (frequent infections) and blocks airways, leading to hypoxia.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client diagnosed with obstructive sleep apnea (OSA) reports daytime sleepiness and morning headaches and asks what is happening during sleep. The nurse's best response is:","a":["The muscles around the upper airway relax and obstruct airflow while you sleep","Your brain forgets to send the signal to breathe","Excess mucus in the lower airways blocks your breathing at night","The diaphragm spasms and cannot relax"],"c":0,"t":"Sleep Apnea","fb":"OSA is mechanical: upper airway soft tissue and muscle relaxation causes repeated obstructions and oxygen drops during sleep.","d":"m"},{"q":"COVID is a viral illness associated with a wide range of presentations. Which part of the respiratory system does the virus primarily target?","a":["The epithelial cells lining the alveolar wall","The smooth muscle of the bronchi","The pleural membranes","The hilar lymph nodes"],"c":0,"t":"COVID","fb":"SARS-CoV-2 binds receptors on alveolar epithelial cells, which is why severe cases produce diffuse alveolar damage and ARDS.","d":"m"},{"q":"A nursing student asks which type of respiratory problem asthma and bronchitis primarily cause. The best answer is that they impair:","a":["Ventilation","Perfusion","Diffusion","Pleural pressure"],"c":0,"t":"V/Q Mismatch","fb":"Both diseases narrow the airways and increase mucus, which interferes with air movement (ventilation).","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with pneumoconiosis is short of breath and cannot tolerate exercise. The nurse explains that dyspnea occurs because:","a":["Inhaled particles produced chronic inflammation that scarred the lung tissue, so the lungs cannot expand well","Mucus plugs are blocking the bronchi","An autoimmune attack destroyed surfactant in the alveoli","Bacteria are filling the alveoli with pus"],"c":0,"t":"Pneumoconiosis","fb":"Long-term dust/chemical inhalation drives interstitial inflammation and fibrosis, stiffening the lungs and limiting expansion.","d":"m"},{"q":"Pulmonary hypertension caused by chronic lung disease often progresses to right-sided heart failure. What is the underlying mechanism?","a":["Damaged lungs raise pressure in the pulmonary arteries, forcing the right ventricle to work harder until it fails","The left ventricle stops pumping, which backs blood up into the right heart","Pulmonary arteries shunt blood away from the right ventricle","Surfactant is lost, pulling the right ventricle into the pleural space"],"c":0,"t":"Pulmonary Hypertension","fb":"Chronically elevated pulmonary artery pressure overloads the right ventricle, eventually producing cor pulmonale (right-sided heart failure).","d":"m"},{"q":"A client struck in the chest is found to have hypotension, tachycardia, and decreased breath sounds on one side. A hemothorax is suspected. Which best describes its pathophysiology?","a":["Blood is collecting in the pleural space","Air is rapidly accumulating in the pleural space under pressure","Lymphatic fluid is leaking into the alveoli","Surfactant production is shut off acutely"],"c":0,"t":"Hemothorax","fb":"A hemothorax is blood in the pleural space, which compresses the lung and reduces venous return — hence hypotension and tachycardia.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client develops a tension pneumothorax after a stab wound to the chest. Which findings would the nurse most likely observe?","a":["Tracheal deviation away from the affected side and tachycardia","Bilateral wheezing and frothy pink sputum","Cushing's triad of bradycardia, hypertension, and bradypnea","Painless gross hematuria with clots"],"c":0,"t":"Tension Pneumothorax","fb":"Rapid air accumulation under pressure shifts the mediastinum and trachea away from the injured side and impairs venous return, driving the heart rate up.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client in a motor vehicle collision has multiple rib fractures. The nurse observes a section of the chest wall moving inward on inspiration and outward on expiration. This finding is called:","a":["Flail chest","Tension pneumothorax","Atelectasis","Hemothorax"],"c":0,"t":"Flail Chest","fb":"Paradoxical chest wall movement (opposite of the normal direction) is the hallmark of flail chest from multiple adjacent rib fractures.","d":"e"},{"q":"A toddler is being evaluated for a middle ear infection. The nurse knows that otitis media is most often caused by:","a":["An inflamed or blocked eustachian tube that traps fluid behind the eardrum","Direct bacterial invasion of the inner ear from the bloodstream","Loud noise damaging the cochlea","Excessive cerumen in the external auditory canal"],"c":0,"t":"Ear Infections","fb":"Eustachian tube blockage prevents middle ear drainage; trapped fluid then becomes a culture medium for infection.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client is admitted with an intrarenal acute kidney injury. Which of the following are common causes of an intrarenal AKI? Select all that apply.","a":["IV contrast dye","Frequent NSAID use","Glomerulonephritis","Severe dehydration before contrast","A kidney stone blocking the ureter"],"c":0,"t":"Acute Kidney Injury","fb":"Intrarenal AKI = damage inside the kidney itself (nephrotoxins like contrast/NSAIDs, glomerular disease). Dehydration is pre-renal; obstruction is post-renal.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"Acute tubular necrosis (ATN) is the leading cause of intrarenal acute kidney injury. Which best describes its pathophysiology?","a":["Tubular epithelial cells die and slough off, clogging the tubules and producing tissue edema","Antibodies coat the glomerular basement membrane and block filtration","A clot lodges in the renal artery and stops perfusion","A tumor compresses both ureters from the outside"],"c":0,"t":"Acute Tubular Necrosis","fb":"In ATN, dead tubular cells form casts that obstruct the tubule, and the surrounding edema worsens the injury.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client has untreated hydroureter that has progressed to hydronephrosis. If still untreated, what is the eventual outcome?","a":["Loss of nephrons and an inability to concentrate urine","Spontaneous resolution within a few weeks","Conversion to an upper urinary tract infection only","Development of polycystic kidneys"],"c":0,"t":"Hydronephrosis","fb":"Persistent backpressure crushes nephrons; the kidney loses its concentrating ability and progresses toward failure.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client is in the oliguric phase of recovery from an acute kidney injury. The nurse expects what change in urinary output?","a":["Urine output will be markedly low (often less than 400 mL/day)","Urine output will be excessively high (3-5 L/day)","Urine output will be normal but very dilute","Urine output will alternate between anuria and polyuria within hours"],"c":0,"t":"AKI Phases","fb":"The oliguric phase is defined by reduced urine output as the kidneys cannot filter; BUN and creatinine climb during this stage.","d":"e"},{"q":"A client reports that every time they laugh, sneeze, or cough, a small amount of urine leaks out. The nurse recognizes this as:","a":["Stress incontinence","Urge incontinence","Overflow incontinence","Functional incontinence"],"c":0,"t":"Incontinence","fb":"Stress incontinence is leakage with increased intra-abdominal pressure from laughing, sneezing, or coughing.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client newly diagnosed with urge incontinence asks the nurse what is causing the problem. The best response is:","a":["Your bladder muscle contracts too strongly and too often","Your urethra has a structural blockage","Your bladder is so full it overflows passively","Your pelvic floor is too weak to handle pressure"],"c":0,"t":"Incontinence","fb":"Urge incontinence is driven by an overactive (hyperactive) detrusor (bladder) muscle producing sudden, strong urges to void.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with a chronic urethral stricture has a very full bladder and reports constant dribbling of urine. The nurse identifies this pattern as:","a":["Overflow incontinence","Stress incontinence","Urge incontinence","Reflex incontinence"],"c":0,"t":"Incontinence","fb":"When outflow is obstructed the bladder over-fills until urine dribbles passively past the obstruction — overflow incontinence.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client recovering from an acute kidney injury is now in the diuretic phase, with markedly high urine output. What additional finding would the nurse anticipate?","a":["Hypotension with BUN and creatinine beginning to stabilize","Severe hypertension with rising potassium","Crackles in both lung bases and rising weight","Anuria and worsening metabolic acidosis"],"c":0,"t":"AKI Phases","fb":"In the diuretic phase, the kidneys flush large volumes of fluid and electrolytes, which can drop the blood pressure while BUN/creatinine plateau.","d":"m"},{"q":"Chronic kidney disease (CKD) is characterized by damage to which structure?","a":["The glomerulus, resulting in a decreased glomerular filtration rate","The renal pelvis, blocking urinary outflow","The bladder mucosa, leading to recurrent infection","The adrenal cortex, with loss of aldosterone production"],"c":0,"t":"Chronic Kidney Disease","fb":"CKD is glomerular damage and scarring that progressively lowers the GFR over months to years.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which conditions are the two most common causes of chronic kidney disease in the United States?","a":["Diabetes mellitus and hypertension","Polycystic kidney disease and lupus","Recurrent UTIs and kidney stones","Glomerulonephritis and bladder cancer"],"c":0,"t":"Chronic Kidney Disease","fb":"Diabetes and hypertension damage the glomerular capillaries over years and account for most CKD cases.","d":"e"},{"q":"A client with chronic kidney disease has developed anemia and asks why. The nurse's best explanation is:","a":["Damaged kidneys make less erythropoietin, so the bone marrow produces fewer red blood cells","CKD destroys red blood cells as they pass through the kidneys","CKD blocks the absorption of iron from the gut","CKD causes the spleen to enlarge and trap red cells"],"c":0,"t":"Chronic Kidney Disease","fb":"The kidneys produce erythropoietin (EPO); when they fail, EPO drops and RBC production falls, producing the normocytic anemia of CKD.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client is diagnosed with urothelial carcinoma, the most common type of bladder cancer. Which presenting sign would the nurse most likely encounter?","a":["Painless, intermittent gross hematuria","Severe flank pain radiating to the groin","Cloudy, foul-smelling urine with fever","Anuria with rising creatinine"],"c":0,"t":"Bladder Cancer","fb":"The classic presentation of bladder cancer is painless gross hematuria that comes and goes.","d":"m"},{"q":"Impaired diffusion across the alveolar membrane is most characteristic of which two diseases?","a":["Pneumonia and emphysema","Asthma and acute bronchitis","Pulmonary embolism and DVT","Hydronephrosis and pyelonephritis"],"c":0,"t":"V/Q Mismatch","fb":"Pneumonia fills alveoli with exudate and emphysema destroys alveolar walls — both block gas movement across the membrane.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client reports a slow change in vision. On exam the nurse notes a gradual rise in intraocular pressure with poor canal drainage, but the iris remains in its normal position. Which condition is most likely?","a":["Open-angle glaucoma","Closed-angle (acute angle-closure) glaucoma","Cataract","Macular degeneration"],"c":0,"t":"Glaucoma","fb":"Open-angle glaucoma is a slow, painless rise in IOP due to poor drainage; the iris stays in place. Closed-angle is sudden and painful.","d":"m"},{"q":"What is the most common cause of a post-renal acute kidney injury?","a":["Bilateral ureteral obstruction (for example, metastatic cervical cancer involving both ureters)","Severe dehydration from vomiting","Glomerulonephritis","Use of IV contrast dye"],"c":0,"t":"Acute Kidney Injury","fb":"Post-renal AKI requires obstruction; unilateral blockage is usually compensated by the other kidney, so true post-renal AKI typically involves both sides.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which statement best describes the pathophysiology of polycystic kidney disease (PKD)?","a":["An inherited disorder in which numerous fluid-filled cysts develop in the kidneys, enlarging them and progressively destroying function","An autoimmune attack on the glomerular basement membrane","A bacterial infection that ascends from the bladder and scars the kidneys","Crystallization of uric acid within the renal tubules"],"c":0,"t":"Polycystic Kidney Disease","fb":"PKD is genetic; cysts grow, expand the kidneys, and progressively crowd out functioning nephrons.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which statement best describes the pathophysiology of influenza?","a":["A viral respiratory infection that targets the epithelial cells of the respiratory tract","A bacterial infection that fills the alveoli with pus","A chronic inflammatory disorder of the bronchi","A fungal overgrowth in the upper airways"],"c":0,"t":"Influenza","fb":"Influenza is viral and infects respiratory epithelium, producing classic flu symptoms and predisposing to bacterial pneumonia.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with severe sepsis develops rapid breathing, tachycardia, and rising CO2. Chest imaging shows widespread pulmonary inflammation and alveolar edema. Which condition is most consistent with these findings?","a":["Acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS)","Acute bronchitis","Atelectasis","Pulmonary embolism"],"c":0,"t":"ARDS","fb":"ARDS is diffuse alveolar injury (often triggered by sepsis) with non-cardiogenic pulmonary edema and impaired oxygenation.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with a recurrent upper urinary tract infection is most likely to form which type of kidney stone?","a":["Struvite stones","Uric acid stones","Calcium oxalate stones","Cystine stones"],"c":0,"t":"Kidney Stones","fb":"Struvite (magnesium ammonium phosphate) stones form in alkaline urine produced by urease-splitting bacteria during upper UTIs.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with a long history of glomerulonephritis presents with rising creatinine and an acute kidney injury. The nurse classifies this AKI as:","a":["Intra-renal","Pre-renal","Post-renal","Functional only — not a true AKI"],"c":0,"t":"Acute Kidney Injury","fb":"Glomerulonephritis damages the glomeruli inside the kidney itself, making any resulting AKI intra-renal.","d":"m"},{"q":"After a long surgery under general anesthesia, a client has dropping oxygen saturations and rapid, shallow breathing. Which complication does the nurse most likely suspect?","a":["Atelectasis","Pneumonia","Asthma exacerbation","Pneumothorax"],"c":0,"t":"Atelectasis","fb":"Prolonged supine anesthesia and shallow breathing let alveoli collapse, producing post-op atelectasis with shallow tachypnea and hypoxia.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client in the ICU developed an acute kidney injury after hypovolemic shock. Over the last 24 hours their urine output was less than 300 mL and BUN and creatinine are climbing. Which AKI phase is the client most likely in?","a":["Oliguric phase","Diuretic phase","Recovery phase","Onset (initiation) phase only"],"c":0,"t":"AKI Phases","fb":"Low urine output (less than 400 mL/day) with rising nitrogenous wastes is the hallmark of the oliguric phase.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which findings would the nurse expect in a client with end-stage chronic kidney disease (renal failure)? Select all that apply.","a":["Markedly decreased GFR","Metabolic acidosis","Hyperkalemia","Hypernatremia with concentrated urine","Erythrocytosis from excess EPO"],"c":0,"t":"End-Stage CKD","fb":"End-stage CKD: GFR is low, the kidneys cannot excrete acid (acidosis) or potassium (hyperkalemia). EPO is decreased (anemia), not increased.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"A client admitted with severe vomiting and diarrhea over the past three days now has a serum creatinine that has doubled and a urine output of 250 mL in 24 hours. The nurse classifies this acute kidney injury as which type?","a":["Pre-renal","Intra-renal","Post-renal","Functional only, not a true AKI"],"c":0,"t":"Acute Kidney Injury","fb":"Volume loss from GI fluids drops renal perfusion before any kidney damage occurs, which defines pre-renal AKI.","d":"e"},{"q":"A client with benign prostatic hyperplasia has been unable to fully empty the bladder for several weeks and now has rising creatinine. The nurse identifies this as which category of AKI?","a":["Post-renal","Pre-renal","Intra-renal","Reversible glomerular AKI"],"c":0,"t":"Acute Kidney Injury","fb":"Bladder outlet obstruction blocks urinary outflow distal to the kidneys, producing a post-renal AKI.","d":"e"},{"q":"A client recovering from an acute kidney injury is now urinating roughly 4 liters per day with low specific gravity. The nurse should monitor closely for which complications? Select all that apply.","a":["Hypotension","Hypokalemia","Dehydration","Hyperkalemia","Pulmonary edema"],"c":0,"t":"AKI Phases","fb":"The diuretic phase floods the body with dilute urine, so blood pressure drops and potassium and water are lost. Hyperkalemia and pulmonary edema fit the earlier oliguric phase.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"A client is several weeks into the recovery phase after an acute kidney injury. Which finding best indicates that the kidneys are healing as expected?","a":["BUN and creatinine are trending back toward baseline","Urine output is dropping to less than 400 mL/day","Serum potassium is rising above 6 mEq/L","Daily weight is climbing by 2 kg per day"],"c":0,"t":"AKI Phases","fb":"The recovery phase is marked by gradual normalization of waste removal, so BUN and creatinine return toward baseline.","d":"e"},{"q":"A client received IV contrast for a CT scan yesterday and now has a creatinine that has jumped from 0.9 to 2.1 mg/dL. The nurse understands that contrast-induced acute tubular necrosis primarily damages which structure?","a":["The epithelial cells lining the renal tubules","The glomerular basement membrane","The renal pelvis","The bladder trigone"],"c":0,"t":"Acute Tubular Necrosis","fb":"Nephrotoxins like IV contrast injure the tubular epithelium; sloughed cells form casts that obstruct flow within the tubule.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with severe hypovolemic shock has developed ischemic acute tubular necrosis. The nurse explains that the tubular cells died because:","a":["Blood flow to the kidneys dropped so low that the tubular cells did not receive enough oxygen","Bacteria attacked the tubules directly","Antibodies destroyed the tubular epithelium","Crystalline stones blocked the tubular lumen"],"c":0,"t":"Acute Tubular Necrosis","fb":"Prolonged hypoperfusion deprives the highly metabolic tubular cells of oxygen, killing them — the ischemic form of ATN.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which clients are at increased risk for developing an intra-renal acute kidney injury? Select all that apply.","a":["A client receiving IV vancomycin and gentamicin together","A client with newly diagnosed glomerulonephritis","A client who takes ibuprofen several times daily for chronic pain","A client with severe vomiting and dry mucous membranes","A client with a kidney stone obstructing the right ureter"],"c":0,"t":"Acute Kidney Injury","fb":"Nephrotoxic drugs (vancomycin, aminoglycosides, NSAIDs) and glomerular inflammation cause intra-renal injury. Volume depletion is pre-renal; stone obstruction is post-renal.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"A client with longstanding hypertension is told they have stage 4 chronic kidney disease. The nurse explains that high blood pressure damages the kidneys by:","a":["Scarring the glomerular capillaries so they can no longer filter effectively","Increasing erythropoietin production beyond normal","Causing calcium oxalate crystals to form in the urine","Blocking the ureters from the outside"],"c":0,"t":"Chronic Kidney Disease","fb":"Chronic high pressure injures glomerular capillaries and leads to glomerulosclerosis, lowering the GFR over years.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with chronic kidney disease has a hemoglobin of 8.2 g/dL. The nurse anticipates an order for which medication that directly addresses the underlying cause?","a":["Epoetin alfa","Oral iron supplementation only","A blood transfusion every two weeks","A loop diuretic"],"c":0,"t":"Chronic Kidney Disease","fb":"CKD-related anemia is caused by erythropoietin (EPO) deficiency, so replacing EPO with an agent like epoetin alfa treats the root cause.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with end-stage renal disease misses a scheduled hemodialysis session. Which findings would most concern the nurse? Select all that apply.","a":["Serum potassium of 6.9 mEq/L with peaked T waves on ECG","Crackles in both lung bases with new dyspnea","Weight gain of 4 kg since the last dialysis","Serum sodium of 138 mEq/L","Blood glucose of 110 mg/dL"],"c":0,"t":"End-Stage CKD","fb":"Failed kidneys cannot excrete potassium, sodium, or water; missed dialysis quickly produces hyperkalemia, fluid overload, and weight gain.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"A client with end-stage renal disease has a phosphorus of 7.8 mg/dL and a calcium of 7.6 mg/dL. The nurse anticipates which long-term skeletal complication?","a":["Renal osteodystrophy from chronic mineral and vitamin D imbalance","Osteomyelitis from bacterial seeding","Paget's disease of bone","Avascular necrosis from steroid use"],"c":0,"t":"End-Stage CKD","fb":"Failing kidneys cannot activate vitamin D or excrete phosphate; high phosphate and low calcium drive bone demineralization, known as renal osteodystrophy.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with end-stage renal disease is described as having uremia. Which findings would the nurse expect to see? Select all that apply.","a":["Pruritus (itching)","Nausea and a metallic taste in the mouth","Uremic frost on the skin","Polyuria with very dilute urine","Hypoglycemia from increased insulin clearance"],"c":0,"t":"End-Stage CKD","fb":"Uremia is the systemic toxic effect of accumulated wastes — itching, GI upset, taste changes, and uremic frost are classic. Polyuria and hypoglycemia are not features of late CKD.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"A client newly diagnosed with autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease asks if their children will get it. The nurse's best response is:","a":["Each of your children has about a 50% chance of inheriting the gene","Only your sons will be affected","Only your daughters will be affected","This disease is not inherited, so your children will not be at risk"],"c":0,"t":"Polycystic Kidney Disease","fb":"Autosomal dominant PKD passes to children with 50% probability per pregnancy, regardless of sex.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with polycystic kidney disease reports a sudden, severe headache and visual changes. The nurse recognizes this client is at increased risk for which complication?","a":["Ruptured cerebral (berry) aneurysm","Acute myocardial infarction","Glaucoma","Pulmonary embolism"],"c":0,"t":"Polycystic Kidney Disease","fb":"PKD is associated with cerebral berry aneurysms; sudden severe headache should raise concern for rupture.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with hydronephrosis from a ureteral stone reports a dull, constant flank ache and a slight rise in creatinine. The nurse explains that hydronephrosis is best described as:","a":["Backflow and accumulation of urine that dilates the renal pelvis and calyces","Bacterial infection of the renal pelvis","A tumor growing inside the renal cortex","An autoimmune attack on the renal tubules"],"c":0,"t":"Hydronephrosis","fb":"Hydronephrosis is dilation of the renal collecting system from obstructed urine flow; pressure on the parenchyma eventually destroys nephrons.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with severe hydronephrosis from a malignant pelvic mass has had a nephrostomy tube placed. The nurse explains the purpose of this tube is to:","a":["Drain urine directly from the kidney, relieving the backpressure on the nephrons","Deliver chemotherapy directly into the renal pelvis","Measure intra-abdominal pressure","Provide a route for hemodialysis"],"c":0,"t":"Hydronephrosis","fb":"A nephrostomy bypasses the obstruction so urine drains out, dropping pressure inside the kidney and preserving remaining nephrons.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client passes a kidney stone that lab analysis identifies as calcium oxalate. Which dietary recommendation is most appropriate?","a":["Limit foods very high in oxalate, such as spinach, beets, and rhubarb","Increase intake of organ meats and shellfish","Eliminate all dairy products from the diet","Avoid drinking water with meals"],"c":0,"t":"Kidney Stones","fb":"Calcium oxalate stones form when urine oxalate is high, so cutting oxalate-rich foods reduces the risk of recurrence. Severe calcium restriction is no longer recommended.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with gout is found to have a kidney stone. Which type of stone is most likely?","a":["Uric acid","Struvite","Cystine","Calcium phosphate"],"c":0,"t":"Kidney Stones","fb":"High serum uric acid (as in gout) acidifies the urine and produces uric acid stones.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client passed a kidney stone and asks how to prevent another. Which teaching points should the nurse include? Select all that apply.","a":["Drink at least 2 to 3 liters of water daily","Strain all urine until cleared by the provider","Limit very high-oxalate foods such as spinach and rhubarb","Take a daily NSAID for prevention","Restrict all fluid intake after dinner"],"c":0,"t":"Kidney Stones","fb":"High fluid intake, straining urine to identify any stones passed, and reducing high-oxalate foods are core kidney stone preventive measures.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"A client presents with painless, intermittent gross hematuria. Which question is most important for the nurse to ask first when collecting the history?","a":["Do you have a history of smoking or industrial chemical exposure?","Have you had any recent throat infections?","Do you drink less than two cups of water per day?","Have you traveled internationally in the past month?"],"c":0,"t":"Bladder Cancer","fb":"Smoking and occupational chemical exposures (aromatic amines, dyes) are the strongest risk factors for urothelial bladder cancer.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client diagnosed with bladder cancer asks why painless hematuria is often missed. The nurse's best response is:","a":["The bleeding comes and goes, and without pain people often assume it has resolved","Most blood in the urine is invisible","Hematuria from bladder cancer is always microscopic","The urine is brown only in the morning"],"c":0,"t":"Bladder Cancer","fb":"The intermittent and painless nature of bladder cancer hematuria leads many clients to wait until the disease is advanced before seeking care.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client describes leaking urine immediately after feeling a sudden, strong urge to void. Which intervention is most appropriate for this type of incontinence?","a":["Bladder retraining and scheduled voiding to extend the time between trips to the bathroom","Pelvic floor (Kegel) exercises as the only treatment","Insertion of an indwelling urinary catheter","Daily NSAIDs to reduce bladder spasm"],"c":0,"t":"Incontinence","fb":"Urge incontinence comes from an overactive detrusor; bladder retraining teaches the bladder to hold larger volumes between voids.","d":"m"},{"q":"A postmenopausal client reports urine leakage when running or jumping. The nurse teaches that which intervention is the first-line, non-surgical treatment for this type of incontinence?","a":["Pelvic floor (Kegel) exercises","Insertion of a long-term indwelling catheter","Daily anticholinergic medication","Severely restricting all daily fluid intake"],"c":0,"t":"Incontinence","fb":"Stress incontinence is caused by weak pelvic floor support; strengthening with Kegel exercises is the standard first-line therapy.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with advanced dementia consistently does not reach the bathroom in time because of confusion and impaired mobility. The nurse classifies this pattern as:","a":["Functional incontinence","Stress incontinence","Overflow incontinence","Urge incontinence"],"c":0,"t":"Incontinence","fb":"Functional incontinence occurs when cognitive or physical barriers — not bladder problems — prevent a client from getting to the toilet in time.","d":"e"},{"q":"A 25-year-old client presents with frothy urine, periorbital edema, hyperlipidemia, and 3+ proteinuria. The nurse identifies this clinical picture as:","a":["Nephrotic syndrome","Nephritic syndrome","Acute pyelonephritis","Bladder cancer"],"c":0,"t":"Nephrotic Syndrome","fb":"Massive proteinuria, low albumin, edema, and high lipids together define nephrotic syndrome.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with nephrotic syndrome has lost so much albumin in the urine that serum albumin is 2.0 g/dL. The nurse anticipates which related complication?","a":["Increased risk for venous thromboembolism","Pure red cell aplasia","Acute hemolytic anemia","Hypoglycemia"],"c":0,"t":"Nephrotic Syndrome","fb":"Nephrotic syndrome causes loss of anticoagulant proteins (antithrombin III) in the urine, putting clients at high risk for DVT and PE.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which laboratory finding most strongly distinguishes nephritic syndrome from nephrotic syndrome?","a":["Red blood cell casts in the urine","Hyperlipidemia","Massive proteinuria above 3.5 g/day","Low urine specific gravity"],"c":0,"t":"Nephritic Syndrome","fb":"Red cell casts are the signature finding of glomerular inflammation (nephritic syndrome); nephrotic syndrome is dominated by heavy protein loss without active urinary sediment.","d":"m"},{"q":"A child two weeks after a streptococcal sore throat develops dark, cola-colored urine, mild facial swelling, and elevated blood pressure. Which findings of nephritic syndrome does the nurse expect to confirm on labs and assessment? Select all that apply.","a":["Hematuria","Hypertension","Mild proteinuria","Red blood cell casts in the urine","Serum albumin under 2 g/dL with anasarca"],"c":0,"t":"Nephritic Syndrome","fb":"Nephritic syndrome from post-strep glomerulonephritis: hematuria, hypertension, mild proteinuria, and red cell casts. Heavy proteinuria with severe edema describes the nephrotic picture.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,3]},{"q":"A client newly diagnosed with open-angle glaucoma asks why the disease damages vision. The nurse's best explanation is:","a":["High pressure inside the eye slowly damages the optic nerve","The lens of the eye becomes cloudy and blocks light","The macula bleeds and scars","The retina detaches from the back of the eye"],"c":0,"t":"Glaucoma","fb":"In glaucoma, elevated intraocular pressure injures the optic nerve fibers, producing gradual peripheral visual field loss.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client arrives in the emergency department with sudden onset of severe eye pain, blurred vision, headache, nausea, and a fixed mid-dilated pupil. The nurse should suspect:","a":["Acute closed-angle (angle-closure) glaucoma","Open-angle glaucoma","Cataract","Otitis externa"],"c":0,"t":"Glaucoma","fb":"Acute closed-angle glaucoma is a painful, rapid-onset rise in IOP that is an ophthalmologic emergency.","d":"m"},{"q":"A parent reports that their 2-year-old child is tugging at one ear, has a fever, and woke up crying overnight. The nurse explains that young children are more prone to otitis media because:","a":["Their eustachian tubes are short, narrow, and more horizontal, so middle-ear fluid does not drain well","Their eardrums are thinner and rupture easily","Their immune systems make too much earwax","They produce more saliva than older children"],"c":0,"t":"Ear Infections","fb":"In small children the eustachian tube anatomy promotes fluid stasis in the middle ear, leading to recurrent otitis media.","d":"m"},{"q":"A nurse is teaching about the difference between acute and chronic bronchitis. Which statement should be included?","a":["Acute bronchitis is usually triggered by an infection and resolves on its own; chronic bronchitis is part of COPD and persists for years","Acute bronchitis only occurs in smokers, while chronic bronchitis occurs only in non-smokers","Both forms cause permanent destruction of the alveoli","Chronic bronchitis lasts less than two weeks and acute bronchitis lasts months"],"c":0,"t":"Bronchitis","fb":"Acute bronchitis is self-limited and infectious; chronic bronchitis is one half of COPD (with emphysema) and is defined by a productive cough for at least 3 months per year for 2 consecutive years.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with emphysema has a barrel chest, sits leaning forward, and breathes through pursed lips. The nurse understands the client is using pursed-lip breathing to:","a":["Keep the airways open longer during exhalation and improve air movement","Increase the rate of breathing to wash out CO2","Pull air more forcefully into the alveoli","Activate the cough reflex"],"c":0,"t":"Emphysema","fb":"Pursed-lip breathing creates positive pressure in the airways during exhalation, preventing alveolar collapse and improving exhalation in air-trapped lungs.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client newly diagnosed with COPD asks what they can do that will most slow disease progression. The nurse's best response is:","a":["Stop smoking and avoid secondhand smoke","Take daily over-the-counter cough syrup","Sleep flat without any pillows","Drink less than one liter of water per day"],"c":0,"t":"COPD","fb":"Tobacco smoke is the dominant driver of COPD progression; cessation is the single most effective intervention.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with COPD has an oxygen saturation of 89%. The nurse plans to administer oxygen at 2 L/min by nasal cannula and explains that higher flow rates are typically avoided because:","a":["In some clients with COPD, very high oxygen can blunt the drive to breathe","Oxygen always causes immediate lung damage","Oxygen is flammable and forbidden in the home","Oxygen makes the client too cold"],"c":0,"t":"COPD","fb":"Some clients with longstanding hypercapnia rely on hypoxic drive; aggressive oxygen can suppress respirations. Use the lowest flow that keeps oxygen saturation in the target range.","d":"m"},{"q":"A child with asthma is in the emergency department with severe wheezing. After several minutes of treatment the wheezing dramatically quiets and the chest is now nearly silent. The nurse interprets this finding as:","a":["A worsening sign — air is no longer moving well, suggesting severe bronchoconstriction","An improving sign — the airways have relaxed","Resolved asthma attack","Normal post-exercise breathing"],"c":0,"t":"Asthma","fb":"A silent chest in a severe asthma exacerbation means too little air is moving to make sound — an ominous, late finding signaling respiratory failure.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with asthma is taught about quick-relief versus controller medications. Which medication is a quick-relief (rescue) inhaler?","a":["Albuterol","Inhaled fluticasone","Montelukast","Salmeterol"],"c":0,"t":"Asthma","fb":"Albuterol is a short-acting beta-2 agonist that quickly relaxes airway smooth muscle in an acute attack. The others are controller medications.","d":"e"},{"q":"A nurse is teaching about asthma triggers. Which factors are common triggers for an asthma exacerbation? Select all that apply.","a":["Cigarette smoke","Cold air","Exercise","Viral upper respiratory infections","A balanced, low-allergen diet"],"c":0,"t":"Asthma","fb":"Smoke, cold air, exertion, and viral infections all commonly trigger airway hyperreactivity and bronchoconstriction in asthma.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,3]},{"q":"A newborn is diagnosed with cystic fibrosis. The nurse explains that the underlying defect is in:","a":["A gene that codes for a chloride channel, which leads to thick, sticky secretions","An enzyme that breaks down surfactant","A receptor on alveolar macrophages","A clotting factor produced by the lungs"],"c":0,"t":"Cystic Fibrosis","fb":"CF is caused by mutations in the CFTR chloride channel; defective chloride transport dehydrates exocrine secretions, making them thick and sticky.","d":"m"},{"q":"In addition to lung disease, a client with cystic fibrosis is most likely to also experience which complication?","a":["Pancreatic insufficiency with steatorrhea","Hyperthyroidism","Chronic constipation from too much fluid in the bowel","Acute glomerulonephritis"],"c":0,"t":"Cystic Fibrosis","fb":"Thick pancreatic secretions block enzyme delivery to the gut, producing fat malabsorption, steatorrhea, and poor weight gain.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client diagnosed with influenza asks why people who get the flu often end up with pneumonia. The nurse's best response is:","a":["The flu damages the lining of the airways, which makes it easier for bacteria to take hold","Influenza turns directly into bacterial pneumonia","Antibiotics used for the flu cause pneumonia","Pneumonia and the flu are the same disease"],"c":0,"t":"Influenza","fb":"Influenza injures respiratory epithelium and impairs ciliary clearance, opening the door for secondary bacterial pneumonia (often Strep pneumoniae or Staph aureus).","d":"m"},{"q":"A nurse is teaching about COVID-19 transmission and prevention. Which statements should be included? Select all that apply.","a":["The virus spreads mainly through respiratory droplets and small airborne particles","Hand hygiene and ventilation reduce the risk of transmission","Vaccination reduces the risk of severe illness and hospitalization","COVID is spread primarily by contaminated food and water","COVID only affects clients older than 65 years"],"c":0,"t":"COVID","fb":"COVID-19 spreads through respiratory droplets/aerosols; hand hygiene, ventilation, and vaccination all reduce transmission and severity.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"A client severely ill with COVID develops widespread alveolar damage and refractory hypoxemia despite high-flow oxygen. The nurse recognizes the client has progressed to:","a":["Acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS)","An acute asthma attack","Pulmonary hypertension","Obstructive sleep apnea"],"c":0,"t":"ARDS","fb":"Severe COVID often progresses to ARDS — diffuse alveolar injury with non-cardiogenic pulmonary edema and hypoxemia that does not correct with oxygen alone.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with ARDS is mechanically ventilated. Which characteristic finding does the nurse expect on the chest x-ray?","a":["Bilateral diffuse infiltrates resembling a 'white-out'","A large unilateral pleural effusion","A focal lobar consolidation","Hyperlucent lung fields with flattened diaphragms"],"c":0,"t":"ARDS","fb":"ARDS produces bilateral, diffuse pulmonary edema on imaging that classically appears as a near-total 'white-out' of both lungs.","d":"m"},{"q":"An older client with community-acquired pneumonia has a productive cough with rust-colored sputum, fever, and crackles in the right lower lobe. Which assessment finding would most concern the nurse?","a":["A new drop in blood pressure with confusion and rising heart rate","A productive cough","A low-grade fever","Mild fatigue"],"c":0,"t":"Pneumonia","fb":"New hypotension, tachycardia, and altered mental status in a client with pneumonia suggest sepsis and require urgent escalation.","d":"m"},{"q":"A nurse is teaching a community group about preventing pneumonia. Which interventions should be included? Select all that apply.","a":["Receive recommended pneumococcal and influenza vaccinations","Practice good hand hygiene","Stop smoking","Hold the breath as long as possible during illness","Avoid all exercise during cold months"],"c":0,"t":"Pneumonia","fb":"Vaccination, hand hygiene, and not smoking all reduce pneumonia risk. Breath-holding and avoiding exercise do not.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"A client with severe obstructive sleep apnea (OSA) is started on continuous positive airway pressure (CPAP) at night. The nurse explains that CPAP helps because it:","a":["Holds the upper airway open with pressurized air so it does not collapse","Forces breaths into the client when they stop breathing","Sedates the muscles around the airway","Delivers a fixed dose of oxygen during the day"],"c":0,"t":"Sleep Apnea","fb":"CPAP delivers continuous positive pressure that splints the upper airway open during sleep, preventing the obstructive apneas.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which clients are at the highest risk for developing a deep vein thrombosis (DVT)? Select all that apply.","a":["A client on bed rest for three days after hip surgery","A client taking combined oral contraceptives who smokes","An obese client who recently took a 10-hour flight","A client with cancer receiving chemotherapy","A marathon runner who finished a race yesterday"],"c":0,"t":"DVT","fb":"Immobility, estrogen plus smoking, prolonged travel with obesity, and active malignancy all activate Virchow's triad. Routine exercise is protective, not a risk factor.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,3]},{"q":"A client returns from a 12-hour international flight with new unilateral leg swelling and calf tenderness. The nurse correctly recognizes which of the following are immediate priorities?","a":["Do not massage the leg, keep the client resting, and notify the provider for evaluation","Massage the leg vigorously to break up the clot","Encourage the client to walk briskly to dissolve the clot","Apply heat directly over the painful area"],"c":0,"t":"DVT","fb":"A suspected DVT should never be massaged or manipulated, as this can dislodge a clot and cause PE. The clot must be assessed and managed medically first.","d":"m"},{"q":"A nurse is reviewing common presenting findings of a pulmonary embolism. Which findings would the nurse expect to see? Select all that apply.","a":["Sudden onset of dyspnea","Pleuritic chest pain","Tachycardia","Hemoptysis","Bradycardia with rising oxygen saturation"],"c":0,"t":"Pulmonary Embolism","fb":"A PE classically produces sudden dyspnea, pleuritic chest pain, tachycardia, and sometimes hemoptysis. The heart rate rises (not falls) in compensation.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,3]},{"q":"A client with severe COPD has developed pulmonary hypertension and cor pulmonale. Which findings on assessment would the nurse expect? Select all that apply.","a":["Distended jugular veins","Bilateral lower-extremity pitting edema","Enlarged, tender liver","Frothy pink sputum and pulmonary edema","Painless gross hematuria"],"c":0,"t":"Pulmonary Hypertension","fb":"Right-sided failure backs blood up into the systemic veins (JVD, peripheral edema, hepatomegaly). Frothy pink sputum is left-sided failure; hematuria is unrelated.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"A coal miner of 30 years is diagnosed with pneumoconiosis. Which intervention is the most important long-term recommendation?","a":["Avoid further exposure to coal dust and other lung irritants","Begin daily inhaled bronchodilator therapy as the only treatment","Start a high-protein diet to repair the lungs","Take frequent daily naps to rest the lungs"],"c":0,"t":"Pneumoconiosis","fb":"Pneumoconiosis is irreversible; the only way to halt progression is to prevent further inhalation injury.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with multiple rib fractures is observed having shallow, rapid breathing and dropping oxygen saturation. Which complication is the nurse most concerned about?","a":["Atelectasis from splinting due to pain","Acute glomerulonephritis","Open-angle glaucoma","Polycystic kidney disease"],"c":0,"t":"Atelectasis","fb":"Painful breathing leads to shallow inspirations, the alveoli do not fully expand, and atelectasis develops in the underlying lung.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client who is two days post-op from abdominal surgery is reluctant to deep breathe. The nurse should encourage which intervention to prevent atelectasis?","a":["Use of an incentive spirometer every hour while awake","Strict bed rest for the next 48 hours","Avoiding coughing to protect the incision","Limiting fluid intake to less than 1 liter per day"],"c":0,"t":"Atelectasis","fb":"Incentive spirometry promotes deep inhalation, expands collapsed alveoli, and is the primary post-op atelectasis preventive.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with a stab wound to the right chest has absent breath sounds on the right, hypotension, tachycardia, and the trachea is shifted to the left. Which intervention is the highest priority?","a":["Prepare for immediate needle decompression of the chest","Apply oxygen and continue to monitor for 30 minutes","Start a slow IV infusion of normal saline","Encourage incentive spirometry use"],"c":0,"t":"Tension Pneumothorax","fb":"Tracheal deviation away from the injury with hemodynamic instability indicates tension pneumothorax — life-saving treatment is immediate needle decompression.","d":"m"}]},"7":{"title":"PATHO PRIME · CUMULATIVE — Ch 13 GI · Ch 14 Liver/Repro + 80% review","questions":[{"q":"A client arrives in the emergency department with abdominal distention, no bowel movement for three days, and copious vomiting that smells fecal. Imaging confirms a small bowel obstruction. Which finding would the nurse expect to develop first if the obstruction is not relieved?","a":["Hyperkalemia from cellular lysis","Metabolic acidosis from bicarbonate loss","Dehydration and metabolic alkalosis from upper GI fluid loss","Hyperglycemia from stress response"],"c":2,"t":"Bowel Obstruction","fb":"Proximal (small bowel) obstructions cause vomiting of acidic gastric contents, leading to fluid volume loss and metabolic alkalosis. Lower obstructions tend toward acidosis.","d":"m"},{"q":"The nurse is reviewing common causes of mechanical large bowel obstruction. Which conditions should the nurse include? Select all that apply.","a":["Colorectal cancer","Diverticular stricture","Volvulus","Paralytic ileus from opioids","Severe hypokalemia"],"c":0,"t":"Bowel Obstruction","fb":"Tumor, diverticular stricture, and volvulus are mechanical large bowel obstructions. Ileus and electrolyte-driven dysmotility are functional, not mechanical.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"A client newly diagnosed with celiac disease asks why they have lost weight and develop diarrhea after eating wheat. Which response best explains the pathophysiology?","a":["Gluten directly poisons the cells of the colon, causing fluid loss","An immune reaction to gluten flattens the villi of the small intestine and impairs nutrient absorption","Gluten ferments in the colon and produces excessive gas","An enzyme deficiency prevents gluten from being digested"],"c":1,"t":"Celiac Disease","fb":"Celiac disease is an autoimmune response to gluten that destroys the villi of the small intestine, producing malabsorption.","d":"m"},{"q":"The nurse is teaching a newly diagnosed client with celiac disease about diet. Which foods should the client AVOID? Select all that apply.","a":["Beer","Wheat-based pasta","Rice","Soy sauce containing wheat","Plain corn tortillas"],"c":0,"t":"Celiac Disease","fb":"Beer, wheat-based pasta, and most soy sauces contain gluten. Rice and corn are naturally gluten-free.","d":"e","m":[0,1,3]},{"q":"A client presents with right upper quadrant pain that radiates to the right shoulder, fever, nausea, and a positive Murphy sign. Which condition does the nurse most suspect?","a":["Acute pancreatitis","Cholecystitis","Hepatitis A","Peptic ulcer disease"],"c":1,"t":"Cholecystitis","fb":"RUQ pain, fever, and a positive Murphy sign point to acute cholecystitis, typically caused by a gallstone obstructing the cystic duct.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which client characteristics increase the risk of developing cholelithiasis and cholecystitis? Select all that apply.","a":["Female sex","Age over 40","Obesity","History of multiple pregnancies","Underweight and physically active"],"c":0,"t":"Cholecystitis","fb":"The classic 'four Fs' -- female, forty, fat, fertile -- describe the highest-risk population for gallstones.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,3]},{"q":"A client with end-stage cirrhosis develops confusion, asterixis, and a sweet, musty breath odor. Which finding best explains these neurological changes?","a":["Elevated serum ammonia from impaired hepatic detoxification","Elevated bilirubin causing direct brain toxicity","Hyperglycemia from impaired insulin clearance","Acute hyponatremia from diuretic overuse"],"c":0,"t":"Cirrhosis","fb":"Hepatic encephalopathy results from the failing liver's inability to convert ammonia to urea. Rising ammonia crosses the blood-brain barrier.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client is being evaluated for inflammatory bowel disease. Colonoscopy shows skip lesions throughout the GI tract from the mouth to the anus, with transmural inflammation. Which diagnosis is most consistent with these findings?","a":["Ulcerative colitis","Crohn's disease","Diverticulitis","Irritable bowel syndrome"],"c":1,"t":"Crohn's Disease","fb":"Crohn's disease produces skip lesions anywhere from mouth to anus and full-thickness (transmural) inflammation. UC is continuous and limited to the colon mucosa.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which feature distinguishes ulcerative colitis from Crohn's disease?","a":["Inflammation that can occur from mouth to anus","Cobblestone appearance on colonoscopy","Continuous inflammation limited to the colon and mucosal layer","Risk of fistula formation through the bowel wall"],"c":2,"t":"Ulcerative Colitis","fb":"UC is continuous, starts at the rectum, and is limited to the mucosa of the colon. The other features describe Crohn's.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which complications is the nurse most concerned about in a client with long-standing ulcerative colitis? Select all that apply.","a":["Toxic megacolon","Colorectal cancer","Severe anemia from bloody diarrhea","Gallstone formation in the cystic duct","Esophageal varices"],"c":0,"t":"Ulcerative Colitis","fb":"Toxic megacolon, increased colon cancer risk, and anemia from chronic bloody diarrhea are well-known UC complications.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"A 68-year-old client presents with left lower quadrant pain, low-grade fever, and a history of constipation. Which finding most supports diverticulitis rather than uncomplicated diverticulosis?","a":["No pain or fever, painless rectal bleeding only","Fever, leukocytosis, and localized peritoneal signs","Asymptomatic outpouchings found on routine colonoscopy","Bloody diarrhea with tenesmus"],"c":1,"t":"Diverticulitis","fb":"Diverticulosis is usually asymptomatic. Diverticulitis adds inflammation/infection -- fever, pain, and elevated WBCs.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with cirrhosis suddenly vomits a large amount of bright red blood. The nurse recognizes this is most likely due to:","a":["A bleeding peptic ulcer in the duodenum","Rupture of esophageal varices from portal hypertension","An esophageal tear from severe coughing","Mallory-Weiss tear from severe vomiting"],"c":1,"t":"Esophageal Varices","fb":"Portal hypertension in cirrhosis backs blood into the esophageal veins, which dilate into varices that can rupture and produce massive hematemesis.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with gallstones develops painless jaundice, dark urine, and pale (clay-colored) stools. Which duct is most likely obstructed?","a":["Cystic duct only","Common bile duct","Pancreatic duct only","Hepatic artery"],"c":1,"t":"Gallbladder Disease","fb":"Obstruction of the common bile duct blocks bilirubin flow into the intestine, producing pale stool, dark urine, and jaundice without the inflammation of cholecystitis.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client reports chronic heartburn, a bitter morning taste, and a persistent dry cough. The nurse knows that prolonged untreated GERD increases the client's risk for which condition?","a":["Crohn's disease","Barrett's esophagus and esophageal adenocarcinoma","Gastric volvulus","Hiatal hernia of the descending colon"],"c":1,"t":"GERD","fb":"Chronic acid exposure drives metaplasia of the esophageal lining into Barrett's esophagus, which can progress to esophageal cancer.","d":"m"},{"q":"A 55-year-old male presents with a painful, firm bulge in the groin that no longer reduces, accompanied by nausea, vomiting, and absent bowel sounds. The nurse recognizes this as which type of hernia and acuity?","a":["Reducible umbilical hernia, routine outpatient repair","Strangulated inguinal hernia, surgical emergency","Hiatal hernia, elective management","Incisional hernia, urgent but not emergent"],"c":1,"t":"Hernia","fb":"A non-reducible hernia with signs of bowel obstruction and ischemia (pain, vomiting, no bowel sounds) is strangulated and surgical emergency.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client tests positive for Helicobacter pylori on urea breath test. Which conditions is this client at increased risk for developing? Select all that apply.","a":["Peptic ulcer disease","Gastric adenocarcinoma","Chronic gastritis","Celiac disease","Hepatitis B"],"c":0,"t":"H. pylori","fb":"H. pylori burrows into the gastric mucosa, disabling protective barriers and producing chronic gastritis, peptic ulcers, and gastric cancer over time.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"A client with a duodenal ulcer caused by H. pylori reports that the pain typically:","a":["Worsens immediately during meals","Is relieved by eating and returns 2 to 3 hours after a meal","Is constant and unrelated to food intake","Improves only with antacid use while eating"],"c":1,"t":"H. pylori","fb":"Duodenal ulcers classically present with hunger-like pain relieved by food and recurring 2-3 hours after meals. Gastric ulcers worsen with food.","d":"m"},{"q":"A newborn with severe hemolytic disease develops yellowing of the skin and sclera. The nurse classifies this as which type of jaundice?","a":["Pre-hepatic (hemolytic) jaundice","Hepatic (intrahepatic) jaundice","Post-hepatic (obstructive) jaundice","Physiologic jaundice from immature liver enzymes only"],"c":0,"t":"Jaundice","fb":"Excessive RBC breakdown overwhelms the liver's ability to conjugate bilirubin, producing pre-hepatic (unconjugated) hyperbilirubinemia.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which findings would the nurse expect in EARLY liver disease? Select all that apply.","a":["Malaise and fatigue","Right upper quadrant discomfort","Loss of appetite","Ascites and caput medusae","Hepatic encephalopathy with asterixis"],"c":0,"t":"Liver Disease","fb":"Early liver disease is often vague: fatigue, malaise, anorexia, and RUQ tenderness. Ascites and encephalopathy are late-stage.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"Which findings does the nurse expect in a client with LATE-stage cirrhosis? Select all that apply.","a":["Ascites","Spider angiomas and palmar erythema","Asterixis","Gallstone pain only","Caput medusae"],"c":0,"t":"Liver Disease","fb":"Late liver failure manifests as portal hypertension and detoxification failure: ascites, encephalopathy/asterixis, caput medusae, and skin signs of estrogen excess.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,4]},{"q":"A client presents with severe epigastric pain radiating to the back, nausea, and lipase five times the upper limit of normal. Which finding helps confirm acute pancreatitis rather than cholecystitis?","a":["Pain radiating to the right shoulder only","Marked elevation of serum lipase and amylase","Positive Murphy sign with normal lipase","Painless jaundice with isolated bilirubin elevation"],"c":1,"t":"Pancreatitis","fb":"Lipase (and amylase) are the most specific markers for pancreatitis. Pancreatic pain classically radiates to the back.","d":"m"},{"q":"The nurse is reviewing the pathophysiology of acute pancreatitis. Which statement is correct?","a":["Bacterial overgrowth causes inflammation of pancreatic ducts","Activation of digestive enzymes within the pancreas causes autodigestion of the gland","Excess insulin secretion damages the pancreas from within","Gallstones cause direct mechanical erosion of the pancreatic parenchyma"],"c":1,"t":"Pancreatitis","fb":"Pancreatic enzymes activate prematurely inside the pancreas and digest the gland itself.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client awaiting appendectomy suddenly develops a rigid, board-like abdomen, rebound tenderness, tachycardia, fever, and rising blood pressure that then begins to fall. The nurse suspects:","a":["Resolution of appendicitis","Perforation with peritonitis","Bowel obstruction from adhesions","Gastroesophageal reflux"],"c":1,"t":"Peritonitis","fb":"A rigid abdomen with peritoneal signs after a known inflamed appendix indicates rupture and peritonitis -- a surgical emergency.","d":"m"},{"q":"A college student traveling abroad eats food prepared by someone who did not wash their hands and later develops hepatitis. Which hepatitis virus is most likely?","a":["Hepatitis A","Hepatitis B","Hepatitis C","Hepatitis D"],"c":0,"t":"Viral Hepatitis","fb":"Hepatitis A is transmitted via the fecal-oral route, classically through contaminated food or water.","d":"e"},{"q":"Which routes of transmission are correct for Hepatitis B? Select all that apply.","a":["Sexual contact","Sharing of needles","Mother-to-infant during birth","Contaminated drinking water","Mosquito bites"],"c":0,"t":"Viral Hepatitis","fb":"Hepatitis B is bloodborne and sexually transmitted; vertical (perinatal) transmission is common worldwide. It is NOT fecal-oral.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"A 68-year-old male reports difficulty initiating urination, a weak stream, and frequent nocturia. Which condition is most likely?","a":["Acute prostatitis","Benign prostatic hyperplasia","Bladder cancer","Testicular torsion"],"c":1,"t":"BPH","fb":"BPH compresses the urethra, producing obstructive urinary symptoms: weak stream, hesitancy, frequency, and nocturia.","d":"e"},{"q":"A nurse is teaching a women's health class about prevention of cervical cancer. Which statement reflects the most common cause?","a":["Chronic infection with human papillomavirus (HPV)","Use of oral contraceptives","Cervical inflammation from yeast infections","Family history alone"],"c":0,"t":"Cervical Cancer","fb":"Persistent infection with high-risk HPV strains (especially 16 and 18) is the dominant cause of cervical cancer.","d":"e"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman with multiple sexual partners presents with lower abdominal pain, fever, and cervical motion tenderness. Which risk factors increase the likelihood of pelvic inflammatory disease? Select all that apply.","a":["Multiple sexual partners","Unprotected sex","Prior history of PID","Use of barrier contraception only","Postmenopausal status"],"c":0,"t":"Pelvic Inflammatory Disease","fb":"STIs ascending from the cervix drive PID. Multiple partners, unprotected sex, and prior PID raise risk; barrier protection lowers it.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"A 14-year-old boy presents to the ED with sudden, severe unilateral scrotal pain and vomiting. Which findings support a diagnosis of testicular torsion? Select all that apply.","a":["Sudden onset of unilateral scrotal pain","Affected testicle riding higher than the unaffected side","Absent cremasteric reflex on the affected side","Gradual painless swelling over several weeks","Fever as the dominant initial symptom"],"c":0,"t":"Testicular Torsion","fb":"Torsion is sudden, painful, with a high-riding testis and absent cremasteric reflex. Gradual painless swelling suggests hydrocele or tumor. Surgical detorsion within 6 hours preserves the testicle.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"A client with severe DKA also has acute kidney injury that is impairing bicarbonate reabsorption. The nurse anticipates which acid-base disturbance?","a":["Pure respiratory alkalosis","Severe metabolic acidosis with elevated anion gap","Pure metabolic alkalosis","Mixed respiratory acidosis and metabolic alkalosis"],"c":1,"t":"DKA Synthesis","fb":"DKA already produces a high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis from ketones; AKI compounds this by impairing bicarbonate reclamation.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with chronic kidney disease has a serum potassium of 6.8 mEq/L and a pH of 7.30. Which finding does the nurse most expect on the ECG?","a":["Inverted U waves and a long QT interval","Peaked T waves and widened QRS complexes","Flattened P waves and ST depression with U waves","Sinus bradycardia with normal QRS"],"c":1,"t":"CKD Synthesis","fb":"Hyperkalemia, common in CKD, produces peaked T waves and a widened QRS. The acidosis worsens hyperkalemia by driving K+ out of cells.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with end-stage renal disease has chronic anemia and bone pain. Which mechanisms explain these findings? Select all that apply.","a":["Decreased erythropoietin production by the kidneys","Impaired activation of vitamin D resulting in hypocalcemia","Increased gastric acid eroding the gastric mucosa","Secondary hyperparathyroidism increasing bone resorption","Decreased renin causing hypotension"],"c":0,"t":"CKD Synthesis","fb":"Damaged kidneys produce less EPO (anemia) and less active vitamin D, lowering calcium and triggering PTH-driven bone resorption.","d":"m","m":[0,1,3]},{"q":"A client with a left-sided ischemic stroke develops fever, productive cough, and crackles in the right lower lobe two days after admission. Which complication is most likely?","a":["Pulmonary embolism from immobility","Aspiration pneumonia from dysphagia","Tuberculosis reactivation","Acute heart failure exacerbation"],"c":1,"t":"Stroke Synthesis","fb":"Stroke-related dysphagia leads to aspiration; right lower lobe pneumonia is the classic radiographic pattern.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with atrial fibrillation is admitted with sudden right-sided weakness, facial droop, and slurred speech. Which mechanism most likely caused the stroke?","a":["Hemorrhage from a ruptured berry aneurysm","An embolus formed in the left atrium that traveled to the cerebral circulation","Demyelination of corticospinal tracts","Compression of a cranial nerve by a tumor"],"c":1,"t":"Stroke Synthesis","fb":"Atrial fibrillation creates stagnant blood in the atria, where clots form and embolize to the brain, causing ischemic strokes.","d":"m"},{"q":"A 72-year-old with pneumonia develops a temperature of 39.4 C, HR 122, RR 28, BP 84/52, and lactate of 4.2. Which features of this client's presentation meet criteria for severe sepsis with shock? Select all that apply.","a":["Confirmed infection (pneumonia)","Tachycardia greater than 90","Tachypnea greater than 20","Hypotension with systolic under 90","Elevated serum lactate"],"c":0,"t":"Sepsis Synthesis","fb":"All five features are present: infection + SIRS criteria (HR, RR, temp) + hypotension + elevated lactate = septic shock.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,3,4]},{"q":"A 58-year-old man presents with crushing substernal chest pain radiating to the left arm for 45 minutes. ECG shows ST elevations in leads II, III, and aVF. Troponin is markedly elevated. Which underlying process most likely caused this event?","a":["Coronary vasospasm without atherosclerosis","Rupture of an atherosclerotic plaque with thrombus formation","Aortic dissection extending into the coronary ostia","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy without ischemia"],"c":1,"t":"STEMI Synthesis","fb":"STEMI most often arises from plaque rupture in an atherosclerotic coronary artery, triggering an occlusive thrombus and full-thickness ischemia.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with long-standing left-sided heart failure now presents with peripheral edema, jugular venous distention, and hepatomegaly. The nurse recognizes that:","a":["A new pulmonary embolism is causing right-sided overload","Chronic left heart failure has progressed to right-sided heart failure","The client has developed acute hepatitis A","The client is in early septic shock"],"c":1,"t":"Heart Failure Synthesis","fb":"Long-standing left HF backs pressure into the pulmonary circulation, eventually overloading the right ventricle and producing right-sided HF signs.","d":"m"},{"q":"A post-operative client with a femur fracture suddenly develops dyspnea, pleuritic chest pain, and oxygen saturation of 86%. The nurse most suspects:","a":["A pulmonary embolism from a deep vein thrombosis","Acute pericardial tamponade","Acute pancreatitis","Spontaneous pneumothorax"],"c":0,"t":"DVT Synthesis","fb":"Immobility + fracture + sudden dyspnea and hypoxia is the classic Virchow's triad presentation of DVT progressing to PE.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which factors of Virchow's triad raise the risk of deep vein thrombosis? Select all that apply.","a":["Venous stasis from immobility","Endothelial injury from surgery or trauma","Hypercoagulability from estrogen therapy or pregnancy","Hyperventilation","Hyperthyroidism"],"c":0,"t":"DVT Synthesis","fb":"Virchow's triad: stasis, endothelial injury, and hypercoagulability.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2]},{"q":"A client with longstanding type 2 diabetes develops nephrotic syndrome. Which findings does the nurse expect? Select all that apply.","a":["Massive proteinuria","Hyperlipidemia","Periorbital and peripheral edema","Hypoalbuminemia","Hematuria with red blood cell casts as the primary finding"],"c":0,"t":"DM Synthesis","fb":"Diabetic nephropathy damages glomeruli, producing the classic nephrotic tetrad: heavy proteinuria, low albumin, edema, hyperlipidemia. Heavy hematuria with casts is more typical of nephritic syndrome.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,3]},{"q":"A client with type 1 diabetes presents with blood glucose 488, ketones in the urine, deep rapid (Kussmaul) breathing, and a fruity breath odor. Which acid-base picture and treatment priority does the nurse expect?","a":["Metabolic alkalosis; give acetazolamide","Metabolic acidosis; give IV fluids and insulin","Respiratory acidosis; give bicarbonate IV push","Respiratory alkalosis; have the client breathe into a paper bag"],"c":1,"t":"DKA Synthesis","fb":"DKA causes a high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis. Kussmaul respirations are respiratory compensation. Treatment is fluid, insulin, and electrolyte correction.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client on chronic high-dose prednisone for rheumatoid arthritis presents with central obesity, a buffalo hump, easy bruising, hyperglycemia, and hypertension. The nurse recognizes this combination as:","a":["Addison's disease","Cushing's syndrome","Hyperthyroidism","Pheochromocytoma"],"c":1,"t":"Cushing's Synthesis","fb":"Chronic exogenous corticosteroid use produces iatrogenic Cushing's syndrome with central obesity, hyperglycemia, hypertension, and skin changes.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with untreated hypothyroidism presents with profound bradycardia, hypothermia, hyponatremia, and altered mental status. The nurse recognizes this medical emergency as:","a":["Thyroid storm","Myxedema coma","Adrenal crisis","Diabetes insipidus"],"c":1,"t":"Hypothyroid Synthesis","fb":"Myxedema coma is severe decompensated hypothyroidism: hypothermia, bradycardia, hyponatremia, and decreased consciousness.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with cirrhosis develops worsening ascites and peripheral edema despite a low sodium diet. Which mechanism best explains the edema?","a":["Increased oncotic pressure from elevated albumin","Decreased hydrostatic pressure in the portal vein","Hypoalbuminemia and portal hypertension reducing oncotic pressure and raising hydrostatic pressure","Excessive RAAS suppression"],"c":2,"t":"Cirrhosis Synthesis","fb":"Cirrhosis lowers albumin (decreased oncotic pressure) and raises portal pressure (increased hydrostatic pressure), driving fluid into the peritoneum and tissues.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with severe pancreatitis develops Chvostek and Trousseau signs. Which electrolyte abnormality does the nurse most expect?","a":["Hyperkalemia","Hypocalcemia","Hypernatremia","Hypomagnesemia is the only finding"],"c":1,"t":"Pancreatitis","fb":"Pancreatic lipase digests peripancreatic fat and binds calcium (saponification), causing hypocalcemia in severe pancreatitis.","d":"m"},{"q":"A young woman presents with a malar rash, joint pain, fatigue, and a positive ANA. She also has proteinuria and an elevated creatinine. Which autoimmune disease and complication does the nurse suspect?","a":["Rheumatoid arthritis with rheumatoid lung","Scleroderma with renal crisis only","Systemic lupus erythematosus with lupus nephritis","Sjogren syndrome with sicca syndrome only"],"c":2,"t":"SLE Synthesis","fb":"SLE produces antibodies against nucleic acids that deposit in tissues -- including kidneys, causing lupus nephritis.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with a peanut allergy ingests trace peanut at a restaurant and rapidly develops hives, wheezing, swelling of the lips and tongue, and hypotension. Which hypersensitivity reaction is occurring?","a":["Type I IgE-mediated reaction (anaphylaxis)","Type II cytotoxic reaction","Type III immune complex reaction","Type IV delayed cell-mediated reaction"],"c":0,"t":"Anaphylaxis Synthesis","fb":"Anaphylaxis is a Type I IgE-mediated hypersensitivity producing massive mast cell degranulation, bronchospasm, vasodilation, and shock.","d":"e"},{"q":"A client undergoes biopsy of a colon mass and pathology reports cells that are 'poorly differentiated, invading regional lymph nodes, with one distant metastasis to the liver.' The nurse understands this represents:","a":["Grade I, Stage I cancer","High grade with Stage IV disease","Carcinoma in situ","Benign neoplasm"],"c":1,"t":"Cancer Synthesis","fb":"Poor differentiation = high grade. Distant metastasis = Stage IV.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with a severe traumatic brain injury exhibits a blood pressure of 198/65, heart rate of 48, and irregular bradypneic respirations. The nurse recognizes this triad as:","a":["Early signs of increased ICP that will resolve spontaneously","Cushing's triad indicating dangerously elevated intracranial pressure","Autonomic dysreflexia from a spinal cord injury","Septic shock with neurogenic component"],"c":1,"t":"ICP Synthesis","fb":"Cushing's triad (HTN, bradycardia, bradypnea) is a LATE sign of severely elevated ICP and impending herniation.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with a T4 spinal cord injury suddenly develops a pounding headache, BP 220/110, bradycardia, flushing above the level of injury, and pallor below it. What is the nurse's first action?","a":["Lay the client flat and apply oxygen","Sit the client upright and identify and remove the noxious stimulus (often a distended bladder or impacted bowel)","Administer a beta blocker IV push","Begin chest compressions"],"c":1,"t":"Autonomic Dysreflexia Synthesis","fb":"Autonomic dysreflexia (in SCI at T6 or higher) is a hypertensive emergency. Sit the client up, find and remove the trigger, then treat BP if needed.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with COPD has the following ABG: pH 7.31, PaCO2 62, HCO3 30, PaO2 58. Which interpretation is correct?","a":["Acute respiratory alkalosis","Compensated metabolic acidosis with hypoxia","Partially compensated respiratory acidosis with hypoxia","Pure metabolic alkalosis"],"c":2,"t":"COPD Synthesis","fb":"Low pH and high CO2 = respiratory acidosis. Elevated HCO3 shows partial metabolic compensation. Low PaO2 = hypoxia.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which findings would the nurse expect in a client with right-sided heart failure secondary to long-standing COPD (cor pulmonale)? Select all that apply.","a":["Jugular venous distention","Peripheral edema and hepatomegaly","Crackles throughout the lungs as the primary finding","Ascites","Frothy pink sputum"],"c":0,"t":"Cor Pulmonale Synthesis","fb":"Cor pulmonale = right HF from chronic lung disease: JVD, peripheral edema, hepatomegaly, ascites. Frothy sputum is left HF.","d":"m","m":[0,1,3]},{"q":"A client with a long history of malabsorption develops numbness and tingling in the fingers and toes, a beefy red tongue, and macrocytic anemia. Which deficiency is most likely?","a":["Iron deficiency","Vitamin B12 deficiency (pernicious anemia)","Folate deficiency without neurologic involvement","Vitamin C deficiency"],"c":1,"t":"Anemia Synthesis","fb":"B12 deficiency produces macrocytic anemia AND neurologic symptoms (paresthesias, balance issues), distinguishing it from folate deficiency.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with severe sepsis develops widespread bleeding from IV sites and gums, while also showing microthrombi in the lungs and kidneys. Labs show low platelets, low fibrinogen, and elevated D-dimer. Which condition is occurring?","a":["Hemophilia A","Disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC)","Iron deficiency anemia","Idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura"],"c":1,"t":"DIC Synthesis","fb":"DIC consumes clotting factors and platelets in widespread microthrombi, then produces hemorrhage when factors are exhausted.","d":"m"},{"q":"A construction worker is found unconscious after several hours pinned under heavy machinery. On extrication, the nurse expects which complications related to crush injury?","a":["Rhabdomyolysis with hyperkalemia and acute kidney injury from myoglobin","Severe hypoglycemia and metabolic alkalosis","Spontaneous pneumothorax","Aspiration pneumonia only"],"c":0,"t":"Rhabdomyolysis Synthesis","fb":"Crush injury releases myoglobin, potassium, and CK. Myoglobin precipitates in renal tubules causing AKI; hyperkalemia can cause fatal arrhythmias.","d":"m"},{"q":"An elderly client with a UTI presents with new-onset confusion but no other classic symptoms. The nurse understands this is because:","a":["UTIs in the elderly are typically painless and may present primarily as altered mental status","Older adults cannot mount any infectious response","Confusion is unrelated to UTIs in the elderly","All elderly clients have chronic confusion"],"c":0,"t":"UTI Synthesis","fb":"Older adults often present with subtle or atypical UTI signs -- confusion may be the primary finding.","d":"e"},{"q":"A client with extensive third-degree burns is at greatest risk for which complications in the first 48 hours?","a":["Hypovolemia from fluid shifts and hypothermia from loss of skin barrier","Hypertension and bradycardia from pain","Polycythemia from dehydration","Immediate sepsis with positive blood cultures in the first hour"],"c":0,"t":"Burns Synthesis","fb":"Massive capillary leak following severe burns produces hypovolemic shock; skin loss causes uncontrolled heat loss. Sepsis tends to come later.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with poorly controlled hypertension and atrial fibrillation presents with sudden severe headache, vomiting, and a markedly elevated blood pressure of 220/130. CT shows blood in the brain parenchyma. Which type of stroke and likely mechanism is this?","a":["Ischemic stroke from cardioembolic source","Hemorrhagic stroke from rupture of a small vessel weakened by chronic hypertension","Transient ischemic attack","Subdural hematoma from trauma"],"c":1,"t":"Stroke Synthesis","fb":"Chronic HTN weakens small intracerebral vessels; sudden BP elevation can rupture them, producing intracerebral hemorrhage (hemorrhagic stroke).","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with chronic alcohol use disorder presents with confusion, ascites, hematemesis, and jaundice. Which set of pathophysiologic processes best explains all of these findings?","a":["Acute pancreatitis only","Cirrhosis with portal hypertension, hypoalbuminemia, and hepatic encephalopathy","Chronic GERD with Barrett's esophagus","Hepatitis A with cholestasis"],"c":1,"t":"Cirrhosis Synthesis","fb":"End-stage alcoholic liver disease: portal HTN -> varices/hematemesis and ascites; low albumin -> edema; impaired ammonia clearance -> encephalopathy; failed conjugation -> jaundice.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with poorly controlled type 2 diabetes presents with a non-healing foot ulcer that has become red, swollen, and warm with purulent drainage. Vitals: T 38.9, HR 118, BP 92/56, RR 24, lactate 3.4. Which describes the trajectory of this client?","a":["Stable cellulitis without systemic involvement","Cellulitis progressing to severe sepsis with shock","Allergic reaction to wound dressing","Compartment syndrome of the foot"],"c":1,"t":"Sepsis Synthesis","fb":"Diabetic foot infections can rapidly become systemic. SIRS criteria + hypotension + elevated lactate = severe sepsis with shock.","d":"m"},{"q":"A 32-year-old woman with a known history of Crohn's disease presents with right lower quadrant pain, fever, and a fistula draining stool onto the abdominal skin. The nurse understands this complication is most consistent with:","a":["Ulcerative colitis, since fistulas are common in UC","Crohn's disease, because transmural inflammation can erode through the bowel wall and form fistulas","A normal finding after antibiotic use","Celiac disease, due to villi atrophy"],"c":1,"t":"Crohn's Disease","fb":"Crohn's full-thickness inflammation classically produces fistulas, strictures, and abscesses -- features that distinguish it from UC.","d":"m"},{"q":"An older client with BPH develops a low-grade fever, dysuria, and bladder distention that has been worsening for several days. The nurse anticipates which complications? Select all that apply.","a":["Urinary retention requiring catheterization","Urinary tract infection ascending from stasis","Acute kidney injury (post-renal) from outflow obstruction","Spontaneous resolution without intervention","Hyperkalemia and acidosis if obstruction is severe"],"c":0,"t":"BPH","fb":"BPH-driven obstruction causes retention, ascending UTI, post-renal AKI, and consequent electrolyte/acid-base disturbances.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,4]},{"q":"A client with cirrhosis has a sudden drop in mental status. The nurse reviews recent labs and notes a markedly elevated ammonia level. Which dietary change does the nurse anticipate teaching for hepatic encephalopathy management?","a":["High-protein diet to support healing","Restriction of dietary protein and use of lactulose to lower ammonia","Increased red meat consumption","Unrestricted fluid intake"],"c":1,"t":"Cirrhosis Synthesis","fb":"Less protein means less ammonia produced. Lactulose traps ammonia in the gut for excretion.","d":"m"},{"q":"A teenager with cystic fibrosis presents with worsening malabsorption, oily floating stools, and poor weight gain. Which pathophysiology best explains the GI symptoms?","a":["Autoimmune destruction of intestinal villi as in celiac disease","Thick mucus obstructing the pancreatic ducts and preventing release of digestive enzymes","Bacterial overgrowth in the colon only","Excessive bile production"],"c":1,"t":"CF Synthesis","fb":"CF's defective chloride transport produces thick secretions that plug the pancreatic ducts, blocking lipase delivery and causing fat malabsorption (steatorrhea).","d":"m"},{"q":"An older adult is admitted with severe dehydration, hypernatremia, hypotension, and tachycardia after several days of decreased intake during a heat wave. Which compensatory mechanism does the nurse expect the body to activate?","a":["Suppression of RAAS to lower BP further","Activation of RAAS and ADH to increase water reabsorption and vasoconstrict","Release of ANP to decrease blood volume","Decreased aldosterone to dump sodium"],"c":1,"t":"Fluid Balance Synthesis","fb":"Hypovolemia triggers RAAS and ADH to conserve water, retain sodium, and raise blood pressure.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with chronic GERD develops dysphagia and unintentional weight loss. Endoscopy shows columnar metaplasia of the distal esophagus and a small mass. Which sequence best explains this progression?","a":["Normal esophagus -> Barrett's metaplasia -> dysplasia -> adenocarcinoma","Normal esophagus -> H. pylori infection -> gastric cancer","Normal esophagus -> viral hepatitis -> esophageal squamous cell carcinoma","Normal esophagus -> celiac disease -> small bowel cancer"],"c":0,"t":"GERD","fb":"Chronic acid exposure drives metaplasia (Barrett's), then dysplasia, and may culminate in esophageal adenocarcinoma.","d":"m"},{"q":"A nurse is teaching a community group about cancer screening. Which screening test matches the correct cancer? Select all that apply.","a":["PAP smear for cervical cancer","Colonoscopy for colon cancer","PSA for prostate cancer","Mammogram for ovarian cancer","Mole check for melanoma"],"c":0,"t":"Cancer Synthesis","fb":"Mammograms screen for breast cancer, not ovarian. The others correctly pair screening tests with their target cancers.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,4]},{"q":"A client with longstanding Crohn's disease develops gallstones and kidney stones. Which mechanism most likely explains these complications?","a":["Excessive bile production","Malabsorption of bile salts and fats leading to altered enterohepatic circulation and oxalate absorption","H. pylori infection of the gallbladder","Increased pancreatic enzyme activity"],"c":1,"t":"Crohn's Disease","fb":"Ileal disease in Crohn's impairs bile salt reabsorption (gallstone risk) and allows free oxalate to be absorbed by the colon (oxalate kidney stones).","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with severe acute pancreatitis develops shortness of breath, bilateral lung infiltrates on chest X-ray, and a PaO2/FiO2 ratio under 200. The nurse recognizes this as:","a":["Pneumonia","Acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS)","Pulmonary embolism","Asthma exacerbation"],"c":1,"t":"Pancreatitis","fb":"Severe pancreatitis can trigger systemic inflammation and capillary leak in the lungs -- ARDS. Bilateral infiltrates with low P/F ratio is the defining picture.","d":"m"},{"q":"A young woman with PID develops sudden severe lower abdominal pain six weeks after her last menstrual period and a positive pregnancy test. Vital signs show HR 122, BP 88/52, and abdominal rebound tenderness. Which complication is most likely?","a":["Routine early pregnancy nausea","Ruptured ectopic pregnancy, which is more common after PID due to tubal scarring","Cervical cancer","Endometriosis flare"],"c":1,"t":"Pelvic Inflammatory Disease","fb":"PID scars the fallopian tubes, increasing ectopic pregnancy risk. Rupture causes hemorrhage and shock -- a surgical emergency.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with a complete distal large bowel obstruction develops absent bowel sounds, marked abdominal distention, and only late onset of foul-smelling vomiting. Compared to a proximal small bowel obstruction, which acid-base pattern does the nurse most expect?","a":["Metabolic alkalosis from loss of gastric acid","Metabolic acidosis from accumulation of bowel contents and tissue ischemia","Respiratory alkalosis from pain-induced hyperventilation","No acid-base disturbance at all"],"c":1,"t":"Bowel Obstruction","fb":"Distal obstructions trap alkaline intestinal contents and produce ischemia and lactate accumulation, driving metabolic acidosis -- the mirror image of the proximal pattern.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with poorly controlled celiac disease is found to have iron deficiency anemia, low vitamin D and calcium, and easy bruising. Which underlying mechanism explains ALL of these findings?","a":["Bone marrow suppression from gluten exposure","Villi destruction in the small intestine impairing absorption of iron, fat-soluble vitamins, and vitamin K","Hepatic congestion from portal hypertension","Excess hepcidin production"],"c":1,"t":"Celiac Disease","fb":"Flattened villi cause global malabsorption: iron (anemia), vitamin D/calcium (bone disease), and vitamin K (bleeding from impaired clotting factor synthesis).","d":"m"},{"q":"A client 6 hours post laparoscopic cholecystectomy reports right shoulder pain. Which is the most appropriate nursing response?","a":["Notify the surgeon immediately for possible bile duct injury","Reassure the client that referred shoulder pain from retained CO2 gas under the diaphragm is common after laparoscopic surgery","Reposition the client and assess again","Withhold ambulation and apply heat to the abdomen"],"c":1,"t":"Cholecystitis","fb":"Insufflated CO2 irritates the diaphragm, producing phrenic-nerve-mediated referred shoulder pain. Reassurance and ambulation are appropriate; option C is also reasonable but B best explains the finding.","d":"m"},{"q":"The nurse is teaching a client recovering from diverticulitis about long-term diet. Which statements should be included? Select all that apply.","a":["Increase fiber and water intake once the acute episode has fully resolved","Avoid straining at stool","During an acute flare, follow a clear liquid then low-fiber diet","Permanently avoid nuts, seeds, and popcorn -- they all cause flares","Report fever, severe LLQ pain, or rectal bleeding promptly"],"c":0,"t":"Diverticulitis","fb":"Fiber and hydration prevent constipation and recurrence. Acute flares need bowel rest. Current evidence does NOT support permanently avoiding nuts/seeds. Worsening pain or bleeding warrants evaluation.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,4]},{"q":"A client newly diagnosed with GERD asks how to reduce symptoms. Which lifestyle changes should the nurse recommend? Select all that apply.","a":["Elevate the head of the bed 6 to 8 inches","Avoid eating within 3 hours of bedtime","Lose weight if overweight","Lie down for 30 minutes after each meal to aid digestion","Limit alcohol, caffeine, and large fatty meals"],"c":0,"t":"GERD","fb":"Anti-reflux measures keep gastric contents below the LES: upright positioning, smaller meals, avoiding bedtime eating, and limiting LES-relaxing foods. Lying down after meals WORSENS reflux.","d":"e","m":[0,1,2,4]},{"q":"A client tests positive for chronic hepatitis C and asks about long-term risks. Which complications is this client at greatest risk for developing? Select all that apply.","a":["Cirrhosis","Hepatocellular carcinoma","End-stage liver disease requiring transplant","Spontaneous resolution within 6 months in most cases","Cryoglobulinemia"],"c":0,"t":"Viral Hepatitis","fb":"Hep C becomes chronic in ~75-85% of cases and progressively destroys hepatocytes, leading to cirrhosis, HCC, and extrahepatic immune complex disease. Spontaneous clearance is uncommon.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,4]},{"q":"The nurse is teaching about viral hepatitis transmission. Which statement about hepatitis D is correct?","a":["It is transmitted via the fecal-oral route from contaminated water","It can only infect a client who is also infected with hepatitis B","It is the most common cause of acute hepatitis in the U.S.","It is exclusively transmitted by mosquitoes"],"c":1,"t":"Viral Hepatitis","fb":"Hepatitis D is a defective virus that requires the hepatitis B surface antigen to replicate. Co-infection or superinfection with HBV is required.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client is starting triple therapy for H. pylori-associated peptic ulcer disease. Which combination of agents does the nurse expect?","a":["A proton pump inhibitor and two antibiotics","Two antacids and an anticholinergic","Antiviral therapy and a corticosteroid","An H1 blocker and an antifungal"],"c":0,"t":"H. pylori","fb":"Classic triple therapy combines a PPI with two antibiotics (e.g., clarithromycin + amoxicillin) to eradicate H. pylori and heal the ulcer.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with acute viral hepatitis develops yellow sclera, fatigue, RUQ tenderness, and dark urine. Labs show elevated conjugated AND unconjugated bilirubin. The nurse classifies this jaundice as:","a":["Pre-hepatic (hemolytic)","Hepatic (intrahepatic)","Post-hepatic (obstructive) with isolated conjugated elevation only","Physiologic"],"c":1,"t":"Jaundice","fb":"Hepatocyte damage impairs both uptake/conjugation AND excretion, so BOTH fractions rise. Pre-hepatic raises unconjugated; obstructive raises conjugated.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with chronic alcoholic pancreatitis presents with steatorrhea, weight loss, and a new diagnosis of diabetes. Which mechanism explains BOTH the malabsorption and the hyperglycemia?","a":["Autoimmune destruction of villi","Progressive destruction of pancreatic exocrine and endocrine tissue","H. pylori infection of the pancreas","Bile duct obstruction by gallstones only"],"c":1,"t":"Pancreatitis","fb":"Chronic pancreatitis destroys acinar cells (no lipase -> steatorrhea) AND islet cells (no insulin -> diabetes). This is sometimes called type 3c diabetes.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client on peritoneal dialysis at home reports cloudy effluent, abdominal pain, and a low-grade fever. What is the nurse's priority action?","a":["Reassure the client this is expected after PD","Notify the provider for suspected PD-related peritonitis and obtain effluent culture","Increase the dwell time at home","Discontinue all dialysis permanently"],"c":1,"t":"Peritonitis","fb":"Cloudy effluent + abdominal pain + fever in a PD client is peritonitis until proven otherwise. Send effluent for cell count and culture; treat with intraperitoneal antibiotics.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which laboratory findings does the nurse expect in a client with advanced cirrhosis? Select all that apply.","a":["Elevated AST and ALT","Elevated ammonia","Prolonged PT/INR","Decreased albumin","Hyperkalemia from increased albumin production"],"c":0,"t":"Cirrhosis","fb":"Hepatocyte injury releases AST/ALT; loss of liver function decreases clotting factors (prolonged PT), decreases albumin, and impairs ammonia detoxification.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,3]},{"q":"A client newly diagnosed with BPH is prescribed tamsulosin (an alpha-1 blocker). Which teaching point is most important?","a":["Tamsulosin shrinks the prostate gland over 6 months","Rise slowly from sitting or lying because of the risk of orthostatic hypotension","Limit fluid intake to less than 1 L per day","Stop the medication if urinary stream improves"],"c":1,"t":"BPH","fb":"Alpha-1 blockers relax bladder neck/prostate smooth muscle and improve flow but cause vasodilation -> orthostasis. 5-alpha-reductase inhibitors (finasteride) actually shrink the gland over months.","d":"m"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman asks about cervical cancer screening. Which is the most accurate teaching?","a":["You should never need another PAP smear if your first was normal","Routine PAP smears (with or without HPV co-testing) at recommended intervals can detect cervical changes early, when they are highly treatable","HPV vaccination eliminates the need for any future screening","Only women with symptoms should be screened"],"c":1,"t":"Cervical Cancer","fb":"Cervical cancer develops slowly from HPV-driven dysplasia; routine screening catches precancerous lesions. Vaccination reduces but does not eliminate risk.","d":"m"},{"q":"A 32-year-old woman reports severe cyclic pelvic pain, dysmenorrhea, dyspareunia, and infertility. Laparoscopy reveals endometrial tissue on the ovaries and peritoneum. Which pathophysiology best explains her pain?","a":["Bacterial infection of the endometrium","Ectopic endometrial tissue responds to cyclic hormones, bleeds into the peritoneum, and causes inflammation and adhesions","Malignant transformation of ovarian cells","Autoimmune destruction of the uterine lining"],"c":1,"t":"Endometriosis","fb":"Endometriosis = endometrial-like tissue outside the uterus that cyclically bleeds, producing inflammation, scarring, and adhesions that distort pelvic anatomy and cause infertility.","d":"m"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman presents with oligomenorrhea, hirsutism, acne, obesity, and infertility. Ultrasound reveals multiple small cysts on both ovaries. Which metabolic complication is she at greatest risk for developing?","a":["Type 1 diabetes from autoimmune destruction","Insulin resistance progressing to type 2 diabetes and metabolic syndrome","Cushing's syndrome from adrenal tumor","Hyperthyroidism"],"c":1,"t":"PCOS","fb":"PCOS is tightly linked to insulin resistance -- patients are at high risk for T2DM, dyslipidemia, hypertension, and endometrial cancer (from unopposed estrogen).","d":"m"},{"q":"A nurse is teaching about long-term complications of pelvic inflammatory disease. Which complications should be included? Select all that apply.","a":["Infertility from tubal scarring","Chronic pelvic pain","Increased risk of ectopic pregnancy","Cervical cancer","Tubo-ovarian abscess"],"c":0,"t":"Pelvic Inflammatory Disease","fb":"PID-driven tubal scarring causes infertility, chronic pain, ectopic pregnancy, and abscess formation. PID does NOT directly cause cervical cancer (HPV does).","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,4]},{"q":"A nurse is teaching a high school health class about testicular health. Which symptom should prompt immediate emergency department evaluation?","a":["Mild dull ache after exercise that resolves with rest","Sudden severe unilateral scrotal pain with swelling","Painless small lump noticed during a monthly self-exam","Mild itching of the scrotal skin"],"c":1,"t":"Testicular Torsion","fb":"Sudden severe pain = possible torsion. The testicle must be detorsed within ~6 hours to be salvaged. A painless lump warrants outpatient evaluation (possible cancer) but is not as time-critical.","d":"e"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman 18 months past her last menstrual period reports hot flashes, vaginal dryness, mood swings, and is found to have low bone density on DEXA. Which underlying hormonal change explains all of these findings?","a":["Increased estrogen from ovarian hyperfunction","Decline in ovarian estrogen production","Excess testosterone from adrenal tumor","Prolactin excess from a pituitary tumor"],"c":1,"t":"Menopause","fb":"Menopause = ovarian follicle depletion -> low estrogen. Estrogen loss drives vasomotor symptoms, vaginal atrophy, mood changes, and accelerated bone loss (osteoporosis risk).","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with severe COPD develops new-onset peripheral edema, JVD, hepatomegaly, and a loud P2 on auscultation. Which physiologic sequence best explains this development?","a":["Chronic hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction -> pulmonary hypertension -> right ventricular hypertrophy and failure (cor pulmonale)","Aspirin-induced bronchospasm causing left heart failure","Acute MI of the left ventricle","Pulmonary embolism with right-to-left shunt"],"c":0,"t":"Cor Pulmonale Synthesis","fb":"Chronic alveolar hypoxia in COPD constricts pulmonary vessels. Sustained pulmonary HTN overloads the right ventricle, eventually producing right HF (cor pulmonale).","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with sickle cell disease is admitted with severe bone pain, fever, and dyspnea after a long flight. Chest X-ray shows new infiltrates. Which complication is most likely?","a":["Routine community-acquired pneumonia only","Acute chest syndrome triggered by hypoxia and dehydration causing vaso-occlusion in the pulmonary vasculature","Asthma exacerbation","Pulmonary fibrosis"],"c":1,"t":"Sickle Cell Synthesis","fb":"Hypoxia (high altitude) and dehydration trigger HbS polymerization -> sickling -> vaso-occlusion in lung vasculature = acute chest syndrome, the leading cause of death in sickle cell adults.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which triggers can precipitate a sickle cell vaso-occlusive crisis? Select all that apply.","a":["Dehydration","Hypoxia (e.g., high altitude)","Infection and fever","Cold exposure","Hyperhydration with warm temperatures"],"c":0,"t":"Sickle Cell Synthesis","fb":"Anything that promotes HbS deoxygenation or stasis -- dehydration, hypoxia, cold-induced vasoconstriction, acidosis, infection -- precipitates sickling.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,3]},{"q":"A client with stage 4 CKD and chronic AFib is admitted with new shortness of breath, irregular HR of 132, and a CHA2DS2-VASc score of 5. Which intervention is the priority concern?","a":["Immediate cardioversion without any anticoagulation assessment","Balancing stroke prevention (anticoagulation) with bleeding risk given decreased renal clearance of anticoagulants","Withholding all cardiac medications until renal function normalizes","Administering a large IV fluid bolus"],"c":1,"t":"AFib Synthesis","fb":"Patients with CKD + AFib have high stroke AND bleeding risk. Anticoagulant choice and dosing must account for reduced renal clearance (e.g., DOAC dose adjustments).","d":"m"},{"q":"A trauma client with a pelvic fracture and ruptured spleen is in hypovolemic shock. After 24 hours of resuscitation, the nurse notes decreased urine output (<20 mL/hr), bilateral lung infiltrates, hypoxemia, and confusion. Which complication is developing?","a":["Resolution of shock","Multi-organ dysfunction syndrome (MODS) with AKI and ARDS following hypoperfusion","Allergic transfusion reaction only","Spontaneous recovery"],"c":1,"t":"MODS Synthesis","fb":"Prolonged hypoperfusion damages multiple organs in sequence: kidneys (AKI), lungs (ARDS), brain, gut. This is MODS, the major late cause of death in trauma.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with small cell lung cancer presents with serum sodium of 118, decreased urine output, concentrated urine, and confusion. The nurse recognizes this paraneoplastic syndrome as:","a":["Diabetes insipidus from posterior pituitary failure","SIADH (syndrome of inappropriate ADH) from ectopic ADH production by the tumor","Cushing's syndrome from ACTH secretion","Hypercalcemia of malignancy"],"c":1,"t":"SIADH Synthesis","fb":"Small cell lung cancer commonly secretes ectopic ADH -> water retention -> dilutional hyponatremia with concentrated urine. Treatment: fluid restriction and hypertonic saline if severe.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which laboratory findings would the nurse expect in a client with disseminated intravascular coagulation? Select all that apply.","a":["Decreased platelets","Decreased fibrinogen","Elevated D-dimer","Prolonged PT and PTT","Elevated hemoglobin and hematocrit"],"c":0,"t":"DIC Synthesis","fb":"DIC consumes platelets and clotting factors (low platelets, low fibrinogen, prolonged PT/PTT) and produces fibrin degradation products (elevated D-dimer). Hgb/Hct typically fall from bleeding/microangiopathic hemolysis.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,3]},{"q":"A client with CHF on furosemide and digoxin presents with nausea, visual disturbances (yellow halos), and an irregular pulse. Labs reveal potassium 2.8 and digoxin level 2.6 ng/mL. Which mechanism explains the digoxin toxicity?","a":["The furosemide directly elevates digoxin levels","Hypokalemia from the diuretic potentiates digoxin's effect on the Na/K-ATPase pump, producing toxicity at a given digoxin level","Hypokalemia decreases digoxin's effect, so this is unrelated","The client has developed acute hepatitis"],"c":1,"t":"Heart Failure Synthesis","fb":"Digoxin and potassium compete at the Na/K-ATPase. Low K+ allows more digoxin binding -> increased toxicity at the same serum level. Replete potassium and hold digoxin.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with 45% TBSA burns 12 hours into resuscitation has urine output of 15 mL/hr and dark tea-colored urine. The nurse's priority interpretation is:","a":["Adequate resuscitation -- continue current rate","Inadequate volume resuscitation and/or myoglobinuria from deep burns -- increase fluids and assess for AKI","Acute pulmonary edema from over-resuscitation","Normal post-burn diuresis"],"c":1,"t":"Burns Synthesis","fb":"Goal urine output in burn resuscitation is ~0.5-1 mL/kg/hr. Low output + tea-colored urine suggests under-resuscitation and rhabdomyolysis -- both risk AKI. Increase isotonic fluids per Parkland.","d":"m"},{"q":"A septic client develops worsening hypoxia (PaO2 55 on FiO2 0.80), bilateral lung infiltrates on chest X-ray, and PCWP under 18. Which complication is the client most likely developing?","a":["Cardiogenic pulmonary edema","Acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS)","Pulmonary embolism","Aspiration pneumonia only"],"c":1,"t":"ARDS Synthesis","fb":"ARDS = non-cardiogenic pulmonary edema from inflammatory capillary leak. Bilateral infiltrates + severe hypoxia (P/F < 200) + normal cardiac filling pressures distinguish it from cardiogenic edema.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with a known shellfish allergy presents with stridor, hives, BP 76/40, and HR 138 after eating at a restaurant. What is the nurse's FIRST action?","a":["Administer oral diphenhydramine","Administer IM epinephrine immediately","Start an IV and give methylprednisolone","Obtain a complete allergy history"],"c":1,"t":"Anaphylaxis Synthesis","fb":"IM epinephrine is FIRST-LINE for anaphylaxis -- it reverses airway edema, bronchospasm, and vasodilation. Antihistamines and steroids are adjuncts, not life-saving.","d":"m"},{"q":"A family member calls the clinic concerned that their parent suddenly has facial droop, slurred speech, and weakness in the right arm that started 90 minutes ago. What is the nurse's BEST instruction?","a":["Have them rest at home and call back if symptoms worsen","Give aspirin and observe","Call 911 immediately -- these are stroke symptoms and time-sensitive treatment (thrombolytics, thrombectomy) is available within a narrow window","Drive them to their primary care provider tomorrow"],"c":2,"t":"Stroke Synthesis","fb":"FAST symptoms = stroke until proven otherwise. tPA window is up to 4.5 hours; thrombectomy can extend further. Every minute = 1.9 million neurons lost.","d":"e"},{"q":"A client with end-stage cirrhosis is admitted with hematemesis, altered mental status, ammonia 145, and INR 2.4. Which set of interventions does the nurse anticipate? Select all that apply.","a":["Lactulose to lower ammonia","Octreotide to reduce splanchnic blood flow and variceal bleeding","Vitamin K and fresh frozen plasma for coagulopathy","High-protein diet to support healing","Endoscopy for variceal banding"],"c":0,"t":"Cirrhosis Synthesis","fb":"End-stage cirrhosis with both encephalopathy AND variceal bleed: treat ammonia (lactulose), bleeding (octreotide + banding), and coagulopathy (vitamin K, FFP). RESTRICT protein during encephalopathy.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,4]},{"q":"A 22-year-old with type 1 diabetes in DKA has K+ 6.2, glucose 612, pH 7.18. After initial fluid bolus and insulin drip are started, the nurse anticipates which potassium change?","a":["Potassium will continue to climb rapidly -- expect 8 or higher","Potassium will likely FALL as insulin drives K+ into cells; replacement is usually needed","Potassium will remain stable throughout treatment","Potassium changes are irrelevant once insulin is started"],"c":1,"t":"DKA Synthesis","fb":"Insulin drives K+ intracellularly. Even though serum K starts high, total body K is depleted. Watch for hypokalemia and replace once K < 5.2 and urine output is adequate.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with a tibial fracture in a cast reports increasing pain that is no longer relieved by opioids, along with paresthesia and pallor of the toes. The nurse recognizes this as:","a":["Expected post-fracture pain","Compartment syndrome -- a surgical emergency requiring fasciotomy","DVT","Cast claustrophobia"],"c":1,"t":"Compartment Syndrome Synthesis","fb":"The 6 P's of compartment syndrome: Pain (out of proportion), Pallor, Pulselessness, Paresthesia, Paralysis, Poikilothermia. Untreated -> ischemia, rhabdo, AKI.","d":"m"},{"q":"An 84-year-old nursing home resident is admitted with new confusion, decreased appetite, and fall. Vitals: T 36.4, HR 102, RR 26, O2 sat 89%. Which complication should the nurse consider?","a":["Routine geriatric forgetfulness","Pneumonia, which often presents atypically in elderly clients without classic fever or cough","Major depression","Normal aging"],"c":1,"t":"Pneumonia Synthesis","fb":"Elderly clients often present with atypical infection signs: confusion, falls, anorexia, hypothermia rather than fever. A subtle change in mental status warrants infection workup.","d":"m"},{"q":"A 19-year-old with asthma is in the ED. Initially audibly wheezing and tachypneic, the client is now somnolent with a quiet chest and rising PaCO2 from 32 to 48. The nurse interprets this as:","a":["Improvement -- the wheezing has resolved","Impending respiratory failure -- a 'silent chest' and rising CO2 signal severe air trapping and exhaustion","Anxiety only","Pneumothorax"],"c":1,"t":"Asthma Synthesis","fb":"Quiet/silent chest in an asthmatic = NO air movement, not better. Normalizing or rising CO2 in a previously hyperventilating asthmatic signals fatigue and impending failure. Prepare to intubate.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with Graves disease presents with HR 156, T 40.2 C, agitation, vomiting, and altered mental status after recently stopping antithyroid medication. The nurse recognizes this emergency as:","a":["Myxedema coma","Thyroid storm","Septic shock only","Pheochromocytoma crisis"],"c":1,"t":"Thyroid Synthesis","fb":"Thyroid storm = decompensated hyperthyroidism with hyperthermia, tachyarrhythmia, and CNS dysfunction. Treat with beta blockers, thionamides, iodine (after thionamide), and corticosteroids.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with adrenal insufficiency stops hydrocortisone before a planned surgery. Postop, they develop BP 78/40, K+ 6.4, Na+ 124, and confusion. Which crisis is occurring?","a":["Thyroid storm","Adrenal (Addisonian) crisis","DKA","SIADH"],"c":1,"t":"Adrenal Synthesis","fb":"Stress without exogenous cortisol = Addisonian crisis: shock, hyperkalemia (no aldosterone), hyponatremia, hypoglycemia. Give IV hydrocortisone immediately along with fluids.","d":"m"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman presents with episodes of optic neuritis, weakness, paresthesia, and fatigue that come and go over months. MRI shows multiple white matter plaques. Which pathophysiology is occurring?","a":["Demyelination of CNS axons by an autoimmune process (multiple sclerosis)","Lower motor neuron destruction (ALS)","Loss of dopamine in the substantia nigra (Parkinson's)","Acetylcholine receptor blockade (myasthenia gravis)"],"c":0,"t":"MS Synthesis","fb":"MS is a CNS demyelinating disease producing relapsing-remitting neurologic deficits scattered in time and space.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with myasthenia gravis reports worsening ptosis, double vision, and difficulty chewing by evening that improves with rest. Which mechanism explains the FATIGUABILITY pattern?","a":["Demyelination that worsens with heat exposure","Autoantibodies against postsynaptic acetylcholine receptors at the neuromuscular junction","Degeneration of upper and lower motor neurons","Loss of dopaminergic neurons"],"c":1,"t":"Myasthenia Synthesis","fb":"MG antibodies block/destroy ACh receptors, so repeated use exhausts neuromuscular transmission -- weakness worsens with activity and improves with rest.","d":"m"},{"q":"Which clinical findings are characteristic of Parkinson's disease? Select all that apply.","a":["Resting tremor (pill-rolling)","Bradykinesia","Cogwheel rigidity","Postural instability and shuffling gait","Symmetric ascending weakness as the dominant feature"],"c":0,"t":"Parkinson's Synthesis","fb":"Parkinson's cardinal features (TRAP): Tremor (resting), Rigidity (cogwheel), Akinesia/bradykinesia, Postural instability. Ascending weakness suggests Guillain-Barre.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,3]},{"q":"A client arrives in the ED with sudden right-sided weakness and aphasia 2 hours ago. Which is the most important diagnostic step before any treatment?","a":["Administer aspirin immediately based on symptoms","Obtain a non-contrast head CT to differentiate ischemic from hemorrhagic stroke","Schedule outpatient MRI for next week","Begin therapeutic anticoagulation while awaiting workup"],"c":1,"t":"Stroke Synthesis","fb":"Antithrombotic and thrombolytic therapy are CONTRAINDICATED in hemorrhagic stroke. A non-contrast CT distinguishes blood from infarct and must be done before treatment decisions.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with HIV presents with a CD4 count of 145 and an opportunistic pneumonia (Pneumocystis jirovecii). The nurse recognizes the client is in which stage?","a":["Acute (primary) infection","Asymptomatic latent HIV","Stage 3 (AIDS)","Seronegative window period"],"c":2,"t":"HIV Synthesis","fb":"CD4 < 200 OR an AIDS-defining illness (PCP, KS, esophageal candidiasis, etc.) defines Stage 3 / AIDS.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client begins chemotherapy for an aggressive leukemia and within 48 hours develops hyperkalemia, hyperphosphatemia, hyperuricemia, hypocalcemia, and acute kidney injury. The nurse recognizes this as:","a":["Sepsis from neutropenia","Tumor lysis syndrome from rapid release of intracellular contents as cancer cells die","Allergic reaction to chemotherapy only","DKA"],"c":1,"t":"Cancer Synthesis","fb":"TLS = massive cancer cell death dumps K+, phosphate, and nucleic acids (-> uric acid) into circulation. Phosphate binds calcium (hypocalcemia); urate crystals damage tubules (AKI). Prevent with hydration and allopurinol/rasburicase.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with metastatic breast cancer presents with bone pain, polyuria, constipation, confusion, and a calcium of 14.2 mg/dL. Which findings does the nurse expect on the ECG?","a":["Peaked T waves and widened QRS","Shortened QT interval","Inverted P waves only","No ECG changes occur with hypercalcemia"],"c":1,"t":"Cancer Synthesis","fb":"Hypercalcemia shortens the QT interval. The classic mnemonic 'stones, bones, abdominal groans, psychic moans' captures the multi-system findings.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client receiving chemotherapy has an ANC of 380. Which precautions should the nurse implement? Select all that apply.","a":["Place the client in a private room with strict hand hygiene","Avoid fresh flowers, raw fruits, and raw vegetables","Take temperature q4h and notify provider for any temperature elevation","Allow visitors with active respiratory infections if family members","Avoid invasive procedures when possible"],"c":0,"t":"Cancer Synthesis","fb":"Neutropenic precautions: private room, strict hand hygiene, avoid potential pathogen sources (raw produce, flowers), prompt fever workup, minimize invasive entry points. Sick visitors are restricted.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,4]},{"q":"Match each shock state with its hemodynamic profile. Which statement is correct?","a":["Septic shock: low CO, high SVR, cold extremities","Cardiogenic shock: low CO, high SVR, cold/clammy extremities, elevated PCWP","Hypovolemic shock: high CO, low SVR, warm extremities","Anaphylactic shock: high SVR, low CO, hypertension"],"c":1,"t":"Shock Synthesis","fb":"Cardiogenic: pump failure -> low CO, body compensates with vasoconstriction (high SVR), backup -> high PCWP. Septic/anaphylactic are DISTRIBUTIVE: low SVR, warm. Hypovolemic: low CO, high SVR.","d":"m"},{"q":"A client with a C5 complete spinal cord injury is admitted. Which complications is the nurse MOST concerned about in the acute phase? Select all that apply.","a":["Respiratory failure from diaphragm and intercostal involvement","Neurogenic shock with hypotension and bradycardia","Loss of bladder and bowel control","Hypertensive crisis from immediate autonomic dysreflexia within the first hour","Risk of DVT from immobility"],"c":0,"t":"SCI Synthesis","fb":"Acute C5 SCI: respiratory compromise (phrenic = C3-5), neurogenic shock (loss of sympathetic outflow), bowel/bladder loss, DVT risk. Autonomic dysreflexia appears WEEKS later after spinal shock resolves.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,4]},{"q":"A 67-year-old with COPD, T2DM, CKD stage 3, and AFib presents with pneumonia, confusion, BP 88/54, HR 124 (irregular), RR 28, glucose 412, K+ 5.6, creatinine 2.8 (baseline 1.6), and lactate 3.8. Which problems require immediate attention? Select all that apply.","a":["Septic shock from pneumonia","Acute on chronic kidney injury","Hyperglycemia (possible HHS or stress hyperglycemia)","Rapid ventricular response in AFib","Stable home regimen -- no urgent changes needed"],"c":0,"t":"Multi-System Synthesis","fb":"This client has septic shock, AKI on CKD, hyperglycemic crisis, and rate-controlled AFib that's now decompensated. Prioritize ABCs, fluid resuscitation (cautious -- CKD/HF), antibiotics, insulin, and rate control.","d":"m","m":[0,1,2,3]},{"q":"A client with end-stage liver disease awaiting transplant has worsening ascites, hepatorenal syndrome (creatinine 3.4), variceal bleeding, and grade 3 hepatic encephalopathy. Which statement best reflects the underlying pathophysiology connecting all of these findings?","a":["Each complication is unrelated and represents separate disease processes","Portal hypertension, hypoalbuminemia, splanchnic vasodilation, and impaired hepatic synthetic/detoxification function jointly produce the constellation","These findings are caused by acute viral hepatitis A only","All findings result from a single electrolyte abnormality"],"c":1,"t":"Cirrhosis Synthesis","fb":"End-stage liver: portal HTN -> varices + ascites; splanchnic vasodilation -> renal hypoperfusion -> hepatorenal; loss of synthetic function -> low albumin + coagulopathy; loss of detoxification -> ammonia -> encephalopathy. One disease, many faces.","d":"m"}]}};
